window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][7]={"university_computer_science_en:M7-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M7-L1 – Scientific computer science: models, hypotheses and reasoning<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Master’s-oriented computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master’s orientation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: From ‘it works’ to ‘how can we justify it?’<\/h2>\n<p>The preceding modules M1–M6 described <strong>how<\/strong> computers,\nalgorithms, databases, cloud services, artificial intelligence and\nrobotics work. The scientific perspective goes further: <strong>What\nexactly is being claimed? What assumptions apply? What has been measured\nor logically proved? What counterexamples are conceivable?<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>A program might, for example, process 10,000 records in a few\nseconds. Initially, this is an observation under particular conditions.\nIt does not readily follow that the program is ‘inherently fast’: we\nknow neither the dataset nor the hardware, utilisation, cache state or a\nmeaningful method of comparison. Another implementation may be slower\nfor 10,000 records but faster for ten million.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Scientific computer science<\/strong> combines mathematical\nmodels, formal reasoning, controlled investigations and transparent\ninterpretation. A result may explicitly state that a new technique\noffered no advantage or did so only under narrowly defined conditions.\nIts value lies in understanding those conditions.<\/p>\n<p>This lesson covers models and abstractions, testable hypotheses,\nproofs and tests, scientific measurement, experimental design,\ncausality, uncertainty, reproducibility, validity and concrete examples\nfrom databases, cloud operations and AI. The Master’s core is an\n<strong>educational extension<\/strong>, not a certificate of an academic\nMaster’s degree.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three forms of knowledge in computer science<\/h2>\n<p>Computer science is neither mathematics alone nor merely the craft of\nsoftware development. In practice, at least three perspectives\ncomplement one another:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Perspective<\/th>\n<th>Guiding question<\/th>\n<th>Typical evidence<\/th>\n<th>Limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Formal computer science<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Is a procedure correct under precise rules?<\/td>\n<td>Definition, proof, invariant, counterexample<\/td>\n<td>Does not prove real execution time on specific hardware<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Empirical computer science<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>How does an implementation actually behave?<\/td>\n<td>Observation, measurement, experimental comparison, statistics<\/td>\n<td>Covers only the situations investigated<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Software and systems engineering<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Does the solution meet real operational requirements?<\/td>\n<td>Tests, architecture review, monitoring, assessment of risks and\ncosts<\/td>\n<td>Operational experience is not a complete formal guarantee<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>An algorithm may be formally correct but too slow in practice. A\ncloud service can respond very quickly on a test system but become\nunreliable during disruptions. A system that has operated without\nproblems for years is not thereby mathematically proved correct for\nevery future input.<\/p>\n<p>Complex decisions therefore often require <strong>several forms of\nevidence<\/strong>. M7-L2 examines proofs and computability in more\ndetail; M7-L3 the guarantees of distributed systems; M7-L4 architectural\ndecisions; M7-L5 AI evaluation; and M7-L6 an integrated research and\nsystems perspective.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A model is not reality<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>model<\/strong> is a simplified representation of a process\nor system that can be used to answer particular questions. A model of a\nsearch algorithm can count the number of comparisons without having to\nconsider processor temperature, the file system or network\nprotocols.<\/p>\n<p>Example: In a linear search through an unsorted list with\n<code>n<\/code> entries, <code>n<\/code> positions are examined in the\nworst case. A binary search can repeatedly halve the search range in a\nsuitable sorted list with direct access. The model shows the difference\nin asymptotic cost. However, it does not directly determine how many\nmilliseconds a particular implementation requires, since memory\naccesses, data types and hardware also play a role.<\/p>\n<p>A useful model includes:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Subject:<\/strong> What is represented – an algorithm,\ndatabase, user behaviour or distributed system?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Assumptions:<\/strong> What prerequisites apply, for instance\nsorted data or independent server failures?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Derived statement:<\/strong> What may the model predict or\njustify?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Scope:<\/strong> For which situations was the model\ndesigned?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Limitations:<\/strong> Which simplifications might become\nrelevant in real applications?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Real problem or system\n           |\n           v\nChosen question + observable properties\n           |\n           v\nAssumptions and deliberate simplifications\n           |\n           v\n         Model\n          /  \\\n         v    v\n    Logical  Measurable\n  derivation prediction\n         \\    /\n          v  v\nCompare with observations and assumptions\n           |\n           v\n  Retain, restrict or revise model<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Different models can be useful simultaneously. A <strong>descriptive\nmodel<\/strong> may show the steps of a web request. A <strong>predictive\nmodel<\/strong> estimates response time as load increases. A\n<strong>normative model<\/strong> specifies which states an order may\nhave. A normative business rule is not yet evidence that the software\nactually complies with it, however.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When useful?<\/strong> When the aim is to make complex systems\nunderstandable, compare them or examine their correctness. <strong>When\nexcessive?<\/strong> When a small one-off procedure can be assessed\nsufficiently reliably through direct visual inspection.\n<strong>Risk:<\/strong> Forgetting the model assumptions confuses a\nuseful simplification with unrestricted reality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observation, question, hypothesis and\noperationalisation<\/h2>\n<p>A typical line of reasoning begins with an observation:<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_045_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Investigation flow diagram: observation, testable hypothesis, controlled comparison, measurements, critical interpretation.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: An investigation moves from observation and a\ntestable hypothesis through a controlled comparison to measured results\nand critical interpretation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Research steps using a database index as an example: a fair\ncomparison requires controlled conditions.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Observation:<\/strong> ‘After introducing a database index,\nthe search function seemed faster.’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Question:<\/strong> ‘Does the index make this particular\nsearch query measurably faster under comparable conditions?’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Hypothesis:<\/strong> ‘For a specified table and query size,\nmean response time with the index is lower than without it.’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Investigation:<\/strong> ‘Response time is compared\nrepeatedly with the same dataset, documented load and control of other\nfactors.’<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A <strong>hypothesis<\/strong> is a reasoned conjecture that is\ntestable in principle. <strong>Operationalisation<\/strong> translates\nabstract concepts such as ‘fast’, ‘safe’, ‘reliable’ or ‘intelligent’\ninto measurable or otherwise testable characteristics.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Claim<\/th>\n<th>Measurable formulation<\/th>\n<th>Crucial unresolved definition<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Search is fast’<\/td>\n<td>Response time in milliseconds<\/td>\n<td>From the client to the response, or only database execution\ntime?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘The service is available’<\/td>\n<td>Proportion of successful requests per period<\/td>\n<td>What counts as an error?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘The AI is more accurate’<\/td>\n<td>Error rate on clearly described cases<\/td>\n<td>How were cases selected and labelled?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘The system scales’<\/td>\n<td>Throughput, latency, error rate and cost as load increases<\/td>\n<td>Do hardware and budget remain constant?<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Falsifiability<\/strong> is often discussed in the philosophy\nof science: a testable claim should be incompatible with certain\nconceivable observations. ‘My technique is somehow better under all\nconditions’ hardly permits meaningful testing. ‘Under these documented\nconditions, version B halves mean latency’ can be refuted. Karl Popper’s\napproach is an important tool, but not the only philosophy of science;\nreal measurement errors and auxiliary assumptions complicate simple\nrefutation by a single result [1].<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"41-nullhypothese-und-alternativhypothese\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Null hypothesis and\nalternative hypothesis<\/h3>\n<p>In statistical investigations,\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(null hypothesis)<\/strong> denotes, for example, the assumption that two\nmethods do not differ in a particular population characteristic.\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(alternative hypothesis)<\/strong> assumes a difference or a particular\ndirection. The test examines how compatible the available data are with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder the test assumptions. A non-significant result does not normally\nmean that both methods are identical.<\/p>\n<p>A carefully formulated question precedes the measurement. Anyone who\nrepeatedly adapts the hypothesis to the best results after many trials\nrisks producing an apparently precise explanation that arose precisely\nthrough the selection of the data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four ways of justifying a claim – and what they really\nshow<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"51-mathematischer-beweis\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mathematical proof<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>proof<\/strong> derives a statement logically from specified\npremises. Example: A linear search examines a list position by position.\nAfter <code>k<\/code> unsuccessful checks, the desired value is not among\nthe first <code>k<\/code> positions. This statement is a simple\n<strong>loop invariant<\/strong>. If the list has been completely and\ncorrectly examined, the result ‘not found’ means that no corresponding\nentry exists.<\/p>\n<p>The proof applies to the specified procedure. It does not prove that\na particular implementation is error-free, that every memory cell works\nor that the search will be fast enough on all hardware.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"52-empirischer-test\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Empirical test<\/h3>\n<p>A test uses specific inputs and observes an actual result. It can\nreveal errors or support a hypothesis but cannot automatically cover\nevery conceivable input. A test that succeeds a million times is no\nsubstitute for a formal proof of a universal property.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"53-simulation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simulation<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>simulation<\/strong> executes a simplified model. This makes\nit possible, for example, to compare many variants of network failures\nwithout having to shut down real servers. The results depend on the\nrules built into the model, however. A failure type that is absent from\nthe simulation model cannot be discovered there either.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"54-feldbeobachtung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Field observation<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>field observation<\/strong> investigates a system in actual\noperation. It can reveal unexpected interactions but is harder to\ncontrol. If the error rate falls after a server update, user load,\nnetwork quality or the dataset may have changed at the same time.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Key principle:<\/strong> Proofs require clear assumptions;\nexperiments require fair comparisons; simulations require suitable\nmodels; field observations require cautious interpretation of\ncauses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Correlation, causation and confounding factors<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Correlation<\/strong> means that two observed quantities\nchange together. <strong>Causation<\/strong> means that a cause\ninfluences an effect. If lower latency is measured after a software\nupdate, a temporal relationship has initially been observed. This\nsequence alone does not establish a cause.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>confounding factor (confounder)<\/strong> may be related\nboth to the change being investigated and to the outcome. Examples in a\nserver benchmark:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A full cache instead of a cold start.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A different dataset or different popular search terms.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Updates, background load or computer temperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Different network delays, times of day or test locations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A different number of concurrent requests.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Changed handling of errors and timeouts.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>             New software version\n                      |\n                      v\n                Response time\n                      ^\n                      |\n    Cache / load / hardware / data / network<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A sound comparison seeks to <strong>control, distribute or\ndisclose<\/strong> alternative explanations.\n<strong>Randomisation<\/strong> refers to random assignment of conditions\nor participants. <strong>Blocking<\/strong> groups comparable conditions,\nsuch as different classes of hardware or measurement days, before the\nvariants are compared within these groups. These traditional methods\nreduce possible bias; they do not guarantee absolute freedom from error\n[2, 3].<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"61-experiment-oder-blosse-beobachtung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experiment or mere\nobservation?<\/h3>\n<p>An <strong>observational study<\/strong> examines something without\ncompletely determining the conditions. A <strong>controlled\nexperiment<\/strong> deliberately changes one factor and keeps other\nrelevant influences constant or distributes them appropriately. An\n<strong>A/B test<\/strong> compares two variants, often with random\nassignment. Studies involving real users raise questions of data\nprotection, potential harm and an appropriate legal basis. Not every\nproduct optimisation justifies a live experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experimental design – the real work starts before\nmeasurement<\/h2>\n<p>An experiment needs a <strong>research question<\/strong>, a\n<strong>baseline<\/strong>, metrics and a transparent experimental plan.\nA baseline may be the old software version, a simple conventional method\nor an unchanged configuration.<\/p>\n<p>An understandable experimental plan distinguishes between:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Object of investigation:<\/strong> software version, dataset,\nconfiguration, hardware.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Factor changed:<\/strong> for example, index on/off or\ncaching on/off.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Metrics:<\/strong> response time, error rate, throughput,\nenergy and memory requirements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Factors held constant:<\/strong> input data, load, operating\nenvironment.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Repetitions and order:<\/strong> to better assess chance and\nchanges over time.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Evaluation rule:<\/strong> predefined metrics, error handling\nand exclusions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Always testing variant A before variant B can systematically favour\nthe second variant: the cache is warm, the background load has changed\nor the computer is now in a different power mode. A randomised or\nappropriately alternating order helps limit such factors.<\/p>\n<p>In a <strong>factorial design<\/strong>, several influencing variables\nare investigated in combination. A cache might, for instance, help under\nlow load but perform worse under high concurrency. This interaction\neffect remains invisible if only one ideal condition is considered at a\ntime. Factorial design requires additional trials; for simple decisions,\na narrow but sound experiment is sometimes sufficient [2].<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"71-viele-messungen-sind-nicht-immer-viele-unabhaengige-beobachtungen\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Many\nmeasurements are not always many independent observations<\/h3>\n<p>A hundred consecutive responses often share the same cache,\nconnection and load phase. They are not necessarily\n<strong>independent<\/strong> observations. If the hypothesis is that a\nsystem is reliably faster on different days, several load phases or days\nmay need to be considered. A large number of similar individual requests\ncannot remedy a poorly chosen sample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What do we measure – and what remains invisible?<\/h2>\n<p>Even apparently simple technical terms require an unambiguous\ndefinition.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Latency:<\/strong> elapsed time between two precisely\nidentified events, for example sending a request and receiving the\nresponse at the client.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Throughput:<\/strong> number of completed operations or\nprocessed bytes per unit of time.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Error rate:<\/strong> proportion of specified errors within a\ndefined set of operations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Availability:<\/strong> proportion of the period considered\nin which the service fulfils its defined function.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Resource consumption:<\/strong> including CPU time, working\nmemory, storage access, network transfer and electrical energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These quantities can conflict: a system processes twice as many\nrequests per second while the response time of individual users\nincreases. A new AI computes its results faster but makes more erroneous\ndecisions. Doubling the server provision reduces certain failures but\nincreases operating costs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>This is why one must not present only an attractive\nmetric.<\/strong> The subject of the investigation determines which\ncombination of metrics is actually relevant.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"81-mittelwert-median-und-rand-der-verteilung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mean, median and\nthe tails of a distribution<\/h3>\n<p>The <strong>arithmetic mean<\/strong> is the sum of all values divided\nby their number. The <strong>median<\/strong> is the middle value in an\nordered series, or the mean of the two middle values when the number is\neven. A <strong>percentile<\/strong> describes a threshold of a\ndistribution. The 99th percentile, often written <strong>p99<\/strong>,\nis approximately the boundary below which 99% of observed values fall;\ncalculation methods may differ for finite samples.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Constructed example:<\/strong> The five response times\n<code>10, 11, 12, 13, 54<\/code> milliseconds give:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Mean:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>54<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>5<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{10+11+12+13+54}{5}=20\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Median: <code>12 ms<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Four of the five responses take only 10–13 ms; the fifth\nsignificantly influences the mean.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>From <strong>only five<\/strong> measurements, no reliable conclusions\nabout rare p99 behaviour can be drawn. In distributed applications,\nprecisely these slower tail regions are important: if an overall query\nhas to wait for many partial responses, rare slow individual responses\ncan dominate the total latency. This is explained in the scientific\npublication <em>The Tail at Scale<\/em> [4].<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"82-messunsicherheit-kalibrierung-und-messartefakte\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement\nuncertainty, calibration and measurement artefacts<\/h3>\n<p>Measurements have a <strong>resolution<\/strong>, statistical\nvariation and possibly systematic errors. A displayed value of\n<code>4,217384 ms<\/code> is therefore not automatically more accurate\nthan <code>4,2 ms<\/code>. Accuracy depends on the measuring instrument\nand conditions, not the number of decimal places.<\/p>\n<p>In metrology, <strong>repeatability<\/strong> describes whether\nmeasurements under conditions that are as similar as possible yield\nsimilar results. <strong>Reproducibility in the metrological\nsense<\/strong> considers partly changed conditions, such as instruments,\noperators or laboratories. These terms must be distinguished from\n<em>computational reproducibility<\/em>, described later [5].<\/p>\n<p>The measurement itself can also place a load on the system: detailed\nlogging, a profiler or database analysis requires resources. In\nPostgreSQL, <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE<\/code> actually executes a query,\nshows the measured plan components and can <strong>change data<\/strong>\nwhen executing write commands. The measurement does not automatically\ncapture the same overall latency that a user experiences through the\nnetwork and user interface [6]. <code>EXPLAIN<\/code> without\n<code>ANALYZE<\/code>, by contrast, primarily shows planned, estimated\nexecution costs; these should not be understood as milliseconds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fully worked example: Does a database index bring an\nadvantage?<\/h2>\n<p>The following numbers are <strong>entirely fictional and constructed\nfor learning purposes<\/strong>, not results of executed benchmarks. We\nimagine five comparable situations in which the same kind of search\nquery is considered with and without an index.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Without index (ms)<\/th>\n<th>With index (ms)<\/th>\n<th>Difference (ms)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>14<\/td>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>11<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>13<\/td>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>15<\/td>\n<td>10<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sum<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>65<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>44<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>21<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mean<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>13,0<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>8,8<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>4,2<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The absolute difference between the two means is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>13<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">13{,}0-8{,}8=4{,}2\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRelative to the initial value, this gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>13<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>×<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{4{,}2}{13{,}0}\\times100\\approx32{,}3\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe five paired differences are <code>4, 5, 3, 4, 5 ms<\/code>; their\nmean is also <code>4,2 ms<\/code>. This is mathematically expected for\nfully paired observations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What the numbers illustrate:<\/strong> In these constructed\nexample data, the selected query with an index is on average 4,2 ms\nfaster, or about 32,3% shorter relative to the baseline. <strong>What\nthey do not show:<\/strong> that every index always helps, that the\neffect is just as large for millions of records or that a generally\nvalid statistical statement can already be derived from it. There are no\nactual random measurements and no uncertainty estimate.<\/p>\n<p>An index uses storage space and must be maintained during writes. A\nsmall table may be read faster without an index. The database planner\nmay choose different access paths depending on the filter, table\nstatistics and data volume. A scientific comparison would therefore also\nhave to consider <strong>write costs, data size, query frequency and\nuser requirements<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>An appropriate conclusion after a real, carefully documented\nexperiment might read:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Under the stated test conditions, the mean response time of this\nsearch query was lower with an index. Other data volumes, query types,\nload conditions and the costs of additional index maintenance were not\ncomprehensively investigated in this comparison.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>This is considerably more informative than the blanket claim ‘Indexes\nmake databases 32% faster’.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Statistical uncertainty: Precision is not an absolute\ndegree of truth<\/h2>\n<p>When real measurements vary, the observed difference may arise from a\ntechnical effect, chance, confounding factors or several causes.\nStatistics help describe uncertainty <strong>under stated model\nassumptions<\/strong>. They cannot magically undo methodological errors\nafterwards.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>standard deviation<\/strong> describes variation around a\nmean. If we repeatedly draw samples under the same model assumptions, a\nprocedure with a confidence level of 95% includes the fixed parameter in\napproximately 95% of the intervals calculated in this way. The 95%\ndescribes the long-run coverage rate of the procedure. Whether a\nparticular calculated interval contains the parameter is fixed, even if\nthe answer is unknown to us.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>p-value<\/strong> describes, under the null hypothesis and\nthe test assumptions, how unusual a result or a more extreme one would\nbe. It is neither the probability that the null hypothesis is true nor\nproof that an effect is practically important. A large p-value does not\nprove that two variants do not differ.<\/p>\n<p>Important complementary considerations are:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Effect size:<\/strong> How large is the observed change?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Uncertainty:<\/strong> How much can the estimate vary?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Practical relevance:<\/strong> Does the change matter at all\nfor costs, users or operations?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Sample selection:<\/strong> Are the cases investigated\nrepresentative?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A difference of 0,1 ms may be statistically clear after millions of\nmeasurements and still unimportant for a small web application. A\ndifference of one second is more relevant to users, but may be based on\nan unreliable series of measurements. These two questions must be\nseparated.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"101-risiko-und-aufwand-gehoeren-zur-bewertung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Risk and effort\nare part of the evaluation<\/h3>\n<p>Imagine a new software architecture that accelerates normal requests\nby ten milliseconds on average but raises the error rate from 0,2% to\n2%. A failed request may cause considerably more work than the ten\nmilliseconds saved. Whether the change is worthwhile then depends\nsubstantially on the evaluation of the consequences of errors.\n<strong>All numbers are a thought experiment<\/strong>, not a measured\ncloud service.<\/p>\n<p>The scientifically interesting question is not only ‘Is B faster?’\nbut also: <strong>‘Which benefits and side effects are relevant, and how\ncertain are our figures?’<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity: Are we actually investigating what we claim?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Validity<\/strong> refers to the soundness of a measurement or\ninference for a particular question. Empirical research distinguishes\nseveral typical perspectives:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Perspective<\/th>\n<th>Guiding question<\/th>\n<th>Example of a failure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Construct validity<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Does the metric capture the intended concept?<\/td>\n<td>Lines of code as the sole measure of quality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Internal validity<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Is the claimed influence actually the cause?<\/td>\n<td>New index and new hardware introduced simultaneously<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>External validity<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Does the result apply to other situations?<\/td>\n<td>Laboratory result on a laptop as evidence for worldwide cloud\noperation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conclusion validity<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Do the data and analysis support the claim?<\/td>\n<td>Too few or dependent observations; misleading evaluation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>An experiment can be very well controlled and yet have limited\ntransferability. This is not a contradiction: control facilitates the\ninvestigation of causes, while real applications have more varied\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"111-stichprobenauswahl-und-fehlende-faelle\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sample selection and\nmissing cases<\/h3>\n<p>In database and AI investigations, the <strong>selection of the\ndata<\/strong> is part of the evidence. Questions include: where do the\ndatasets come from? Which time periods do they cover? Are there\nduplicate entries? Which cases were excluded? How many entries in fact\nbelong to the same users or devices?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Selection bias<\/strong> may occur, for example, when only\nsuccessful requests are considered in evaluating a web service. The\nslowest and failed calls are then missing from the response-time\nstatistics. The result looks good even though the actual user experience\nis unreliable. A transparent report also documents timeouts,\ncancellations and the criteria for exclusions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducibility, replicability and generalisability<\/h2>\n<p>An important quality criterion for scientific work is the ability to\nindependently retrace results. However, historically, the terminology\nhas not always been used in the same way across disciplines.\n<strong>This lesson explicitly uses the definitions from the 2019 report\nof the US National Academies<\/strong> [7]:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Computational reproducibility:<\/strong> Consistent results\nare obtained using the same input data, computational steps, methods,\ncode and analysis conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Replicability:<\/strong> A new study examines the same\nscientific question, collects its own data and obtains consistent\nresults within the expected uncertainty.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Generalisability:<\/strong> The results can be transferred to\nfurther contexts or populations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The <strong>Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)<\/strong> uses\nadditional criteria for research artefacts and results validation. In\nits current badging rules, ‘Results Reproduced’ denotes main results\nobtained again by other people using at least some of the authors’\nartefacts; ‘Results Replicated’ refers to main results obtained\nindependently without the authors’ artefacts [8]. Such terms should\nalways be understood in conjunction with their <strong>explicitly named\nframework<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                 Shared research question\n                            |\n                      Original study\n                     /      |       \\\n                    v       v        v\n               Same data  New data  Other context\n                + steps   + study   / other users\n                    |       |        |\n                    v       v        v\n              Reproduc- Replicability Generalis-\n                ibility                 ability<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The possibility of executing the same computation again does not\ndemonstrate that a model produces the same effect on other hardware, in\nanother language or in a different user group.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"121-was-ist-ein-wissenschaftliches-artefakt\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What is a\nscientific artefact?<\/h3>\n<p>An <strong>artefact<\/strong> is a concrete product of an\ninvestigation: source code, test data, a simulation model, a\nconfiguration, an evaluation script, a measurement log or documented\nexperimental apparatus. The ACM distinguishes, among other things,\ndocumented functionality, reusability and long-term availability in its\nartefact assessment [8]. Sharing such artefacts makes independent\nexamination easier, insofar as rights and data protection permit.<\/p>\n<p>A sufficiently transparent technical report identifies:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The exact question and predefined success criteria.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The version of the system investigated and relevant\nconfigurations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Selection, origin and quantity of measurement data.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Hardware, operating system, environment, load and measurement\ntechnique.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The number and order of investigations and documented\ndeviations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The evaluation rules, including failures and outliers.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The most important figures and known uncertainties – not just the\nbest value.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The limitations of transferability and competing explanations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This is <strong>not an instruction to set up anything\nyourself<\/strong>. It explains why a technical claim is strengthened by\ndocumented assumptions. Personal data, secret access credentials and\ntrade secrets must not be disclosed without protection because of a\nreproducibility objective. Anonymised data, controlled access or\nsynthetic examples may be needed instead. Openness does not mean\nsuspending security rules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Verification and validation – two different evaluation\nobjectives<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Verification<\/strong> asks whether a system was implemented\naccording to its specification: does the program calculate the intended\nformula? Does the database respect the specified uniqueness of a key?\nDoes the parser follow the intended sequence of states?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Validation<\/strong> asks whether the specification or the\nsystem is suitable for its actual purpose: does image classification\nhelp real users? Does the measurement method capture the desired\nquality? Is the data structure useful for the actual workflows?<\/p>\n<p>Software can pass every defined specification test and still solve\nthe wrong problem. Conversely, a prototype may appear useful in practice\neven though its correctness in edge cases is unresolved. Scientific work\nmakes clear <strong>which kind of statement<\/strong> was actually\ninvestigated in each case.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Counterexamples, alternative explanations and negative\nresults<\/h2>\n<p>A testable claim should be examined against conceivable\n<strong>counterexamples<\/strong>. ‘This index improves the query’ does\nnot imply ‘Every index improves every query’. A small table, a different\nfilter or frequent writes may refute the blanket claim.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>negative result<\/strong> is also knowledge: a new caching\nstrategy does not help, or a complex division into microservices\nincreases maintenance effort. This may mean that the technology offers\ninsufficient benefit under the current conditions. Unfavourable results\nshould not be secretly discarded.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Selective reporting<\/strong> occurs when only the most\nfavourable results are published after testing many variants.\n<strong>Publication bias<\/strong> means systematically favouring\nparticular, often positive findings in publication. A predefined\nresearch question, transparent evaluation rules and documentation of\nunsuccessful trials also reduce such biases. However, they do not\nreplace expert examination of the method.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three related practical examples<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"151-lokales-medienarchiv-textdatei-oder-sqlite\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local media\narchive: Text file or SQLite?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Research question:<\/strong> Does SQLite provide tangible\nadvantages for a local media catalogue compared with a simple file?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Model and criteria:<\/strong> The software searches, filters\nand modifies entries. Relevant criteria are search time, effort required\nfor relationships, correct modifications and maintenance requirements. A\nwell-structured text format is the simple baseline, SQLite the\ncomparison variant.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Investigation idea:<\/strong> The same records and queries are\nexamined under comparable conditions. For modifications, it is\nadditionally investigated whether the system remains in a consistent\nstate when an operation is interrupted. Faster searches do not\nsimultaneously prove better maintainability; that must be assessed\nseparately.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> For a hundred rarely changed entries, a\nfile may be entirely sufficient. A separate database server might then\nbe even more excessive. The scientifically useful question is not ‘Which\nsolution is more modern?’ but ‘Which solution meets the actual\nrequirements with reasonable overall effort?’<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"152-cloud-anwendung-zwei-server-sind-nicht-automatisch-hochverfuegbar\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cloud\napplication: Two servers are not automatically highly available<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Research question:<\/strong> How does a second server instance\nchange the reliability of a web application?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Model:<\/strong> A load balancer sends requests to two\ninstances. Under the simplifying assumption of independent server\nfailures, the probability of a simultaneous complete failure may be\nlower. However, this assumption is often too idealised: both instances\ncould share the same database, a common load balancer, the same power\nfailure or the same faulty software version.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                    User\n                     |\n                     v\n                Load balancer\n                 /       \\\n                v         v\n            Server 1    Server 2\n                \\         /\n                 v       v\n                Shared database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Investigation idea:<\/strong> Response times, throughput and\nerror rates are compared under different loads. The test plan\ndistinguishes failure of a single instance from failure of the shared\ndatabase or the load balancer. This shows which classes of failures\nredundancy actually helps with.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> Two servers increase operating costs and\ncomplexity. A successful failure scenario does not prove that all\nconceivable disruptions are covered. Later chapters M7-L3 and M7-L4\naddress these questions in more formal and architectural terms.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"153-ki-klassifikation-hohe-trefferquote-und-trotzdem-unbrauchbar\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>AI\nclassification: High accuracy but still unusable?<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Research question:<\/strong> Is a new model better suited to\nrecognising rare critical support requests?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Model:<\/strong> In a constructed collection of 1,000\nmessages, only 10 are critical. A classifier that always says ‘not\ncritical’ achieves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>990<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>99<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{990}{1\\,000}=99\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>accuracy<\/strong> (overall proportion correct), but identifies\n<strong>none<\/strong> of the ten critical messages.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Better metrics:<\/strong> <strong>Precision<\/strong> describes\nthe proportion of genuinely critical messages among those marked as\ncritical by the model. <strong>Recall<\/strong> describes the proportion\nof recognised critical messages among all genuinely critical ones. The\nconsequences of false alarms and missed cases must also be considered. A\nrepresentative test collection separate from training is necessary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Confounding factors:<\/strong> If almost identical documents\noccur in training and testing, <strong>data leakage<\/strong> can lead to\noverestimated performance. Different message types or language habits in\nlater operation may worsen the results. High metrics do not\nautomatically permit unsupervised use in decisions with significant\nconsequences. M7-L5 discusses this evaluation in more detail.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How should scientific results be expressed\nappropriately?<\/h2>\n<p>The quality of a statement does not depend on how confident it\nsounds. The wording must match the kind of evidence:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Evidence<\/th>\n<th>Defensible wording<\/th>\n<th>Unjustified overstatement<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Formal proof<\/td>\n<td>‘Under the specified assumptions, … holds’<\/td>\n<td>‘Every actual implementation is error-free’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controlled measurement<\/td>\n<td>‘In these experiments, … was found’<\/td>\n<td>‘This product is always faster’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observation following a change<\/td>\n<td>‘Afterwards, … was observed’<\/td>\n<td>‘The change was unquestionably the cause’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simulation<\/td>\n<td>‘In the simulation model investigated, … occurred’<\/td>\n<td>‘This is how every real installation behaves’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No statistically notable difference<\/td>\n<td>‘A difference could not be reliably demonstrated’<\/td>\n<td>‘The methods are identical’<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Practical decisions must be made despite uncertainty. A solution can\nbe chosen while also stating which reasons are proved, measured,\nestimated or merely assumed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Effort, benefit, responsibility and limitations<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Rigorous scientific evaluation<\/strong> is particularly\nuseful when a decision is expensive, safety-critical, difficult to\nreverse or consequential for many people. Examples include a large\ndatabase migration, new distributed infrastructure, software for medical\nuse or automated decisions about people.<\/p>\n<p>An elaborate measurement campaign might be <strong>excessive<\/strong>\nfor a small one-off file converter whose results are directly visible\nand easily reversible. A concise, sound justification may be sufficient\nthere. The important point is proportionality between <strong>risk,\nuncertainty and investigation effort<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>Scientific transparency also has limits: raw data may contain\npersonal information, source code may expose trade secrets,\ninvestigations may disrupt production systems. Reproducibility must\ntherefore sometimes be made possible through synthetic data,\ndocumentation, controlled access or separate test environments. Openness\ndoes not mean overriding security rules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Initial question<\/th>\n<th>First useful approach<\/th>\n<th>Particular caution<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Is an algorithm correct?<\/td>\n<td>Specification, invariants and proof<\/td>\n<td>Assumptions about inputs and implementation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is one variant faster?<\/td>\n<td>Comparative measurement with a baseline<\/td>\n<td>Measurement point, variation, cache and load<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is an architecture more reliable?<\/td>\n<td>Failure model and failure experiments<\/td>\n<td>Shared causes of failure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can results be retraced?<\/td>\n<td>Data, version and method logs<\/td>\n<td>Reproducibility is not generalisability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did a change cause the effect?<\/td>\n<td>Controlled comparison and alternative explanations<\/td>\n<td>Correlation is not causation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is an AI useful in operation?<\/td>\n<td>Representative evaluation and analysis of consequences of\nerrors<\/td>\n<td>Do not optimise just one metric<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which solution makes sense overall?<\/td>\n<td>Consider benefit, evidence, costs and risks together<\/td>\n<td>The technical maximum is not always the optimal overall system<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M7-L2<\/h2>\n<p>Scientific computer science starts with <strong>clear questions and\ndeliberately limited models<\/strong>. A hypothesis needs testable terms,\na measurement needs documented conditions and a conclusion needs an\nhonest scope. <strong>Proof, experiment, simulation and\nobservation<\/strong> provide different kinds of knowledge. Experimental\ndesign, repetition, control of confounding factors and transparent\ndocumentation improve the quality of empirical statements, but they do\nnot completely eliminate uncertainty.<\/p>\n<p>The basic methodological idea is: <strong>not ‘Which technology looks\nthe most modern?’ but ‘Which property do we need, how can we justifiably\nexpect it, and when does the statement cease to apply?’<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading in the completed core path:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2 – Correctness, computability and\nanalysis of complex methods<\/a>. This explores formal specification,\nproofs, invariants, complexity limits and undecidability in greater\ndepth. Further Master’s electives are optional specialist topics to be\ndeveloped only after explicit selection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Place within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This chapter belongs to the <strong>six-part Master’s core<\/strong>,\nnot the optional additional Master’s electives. Navigation: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">next Master’s core lesson M7-L2<\/a>;\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">reading paths and subject\ncross-references<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course\nindex<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 05.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Review date:<\/strong> 05.11.2026. The following publications\nare scientific reference works, primary publications or official\ntechnical manuals. The example numbers in this lesson were explicitly\nconstructed for teaching purposes and are not measurement results from\nthose sources.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy<\/strong>, <em>Karl\nPopper<\/em>: Testable hypotheses, falsifiability and its methodological\nlimitations. <a\nhref=\"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/\">https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/popper/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NIST/SEMATECH Engineering Statistics Handbook<\/strong>,\n<em>Choosing an Experimental Design<\/em>: Research objectives,\nrandomisation, blocking, factorial designs. <a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri3.htm\">https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri3.htm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NIST/SEMATECH Engineering Statistics Handbook<\/strong>,\n<em>Completely Randomized Designs<\/em>: Random experimental arrangement\nand comparable conditions. <a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri331.htm\">https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri331.htm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Jeffrey Dean and Luiz André Barroso<\/strong>, <em>The Tail\nat Scale<\/em>, Communications of the ACM 56(2), 2013: Latency\ndistributions and rare slow partial requests. <a\nhref=\"https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/\">https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NIST<\/strong>, <em>Engineering Statistics Handbook, Chapter\n2 – Measurement Process Characterization<\/em>: Repeatability, stability\nand measurement uncertainty. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/publications/nistsematech-engineering-statistics-handbook-chapter-2-measurement-process\">https://www.nist.gov/publications/nistsematech-engineering-statistics-handbook-chapter-2-measurement-process<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Using EXPLAIN<\/em>: Query plan,\n<code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE<\/code>, measurement limitations and actual\nexecution. <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and\nMedicine<\/strong>, <em>Reproducibility and Replicability in Science<\/em>\n(2019): Conceptual distinction between reproducibility, replicability\nand generalisability. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25303/chapter/3\">https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25303/chapter/3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)<\/strong>,\n<em>Artifact Review and Badging – Current<\/em>, Version 1.1: Artefacts\nand validation of research results. <a\nhref=\"https://www.acm.org/publications/policies/artifact-review-and-badging-current\">https://www.acm.org/publications/policies/artifact-review-and-badging-current<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study within the course:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1 – Computer science as a\nscience<\/a> · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3 –\nAlgorithms<\/a> · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1 – Logic and\ngraphs<\/a> · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2 – Data\nanalysis<\/a> · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L2 – Database\nqueries<\/a>.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M7-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M7-L2 – Correctness, computability and analysis of complex algorithms<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Master’s-oriented computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master’s orientation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L4 – Algorithms and data structures\nin depth<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Overview: Three very different questions<\/h2>\n<p>A search function may return the correct result for the sample data\nand still contain a fundamental error. An algorithm whose correctness\nhas been proved mathematically may nevertheless take longer to run on\nlarge inputs than is practically acceptable. And for some tasks there is\n<strong>no algorithm at all<\/strong> that reliably decides a particular\nyes-or-no question for every possible input.<\/p>\n<p>These distinctions are the subject of the second core Master’s\nlesson:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Correctness:<\/strong> Does a procedure produce the required\nresults under its explicitly stated assumptions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Efficiency:<\/strong> How do running time, memory\nrequirements and, where applicable, communication grow as the input\nbecomes larger?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Computability:<\/strong> Is the required general decision\npossible in principle using an algorithm?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>There is also a fourth, practical question: <strong>What kind of\nevidence do we actually need?<\/strong> A small local search routine does\nnot justify the same verification effort as a payment transaction or the\ncontrol system of a safety-critical device.<\/p>\n<p>The lesson builds on the distinction in M7-L1 between models, proofs,\nmeasurements and operational observations. It is not an instruction to\nexecute the programs shown. The pseudocode is intended solely as a\n<strong>readable conceptual model<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The specification must be clear before a proof can\nbegin<\/h2>\n<p>A program can only be described as correct or incorrect relative to a\n<strong>specification<\/strong>. A specification describes the expected\nbehaviour without prescribing every internal computational operation.\nThe instruction ‘Find a video ID’, for example, leaves important\nquestions unanswered:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can the ID occur more than once, or is it unique?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Should a successful search return any matching position or the first\nposition?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the list sorted? May the procedure modify it?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What exactly does ‘not found’ mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Are invalid inputs possible, and how should the program respond to\nthem?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A condition called a <strong>precondition<\/strong> must hold before\nthe call. A <strong>postcondition<\/strong> specifies what is guaranteed\non normal return. Together they form a <strong>contract<\/strong>: anyone\nwho satisfies the precondition may rely on the promised postcondition,\nprovided the implementation is correct.<\/p>\n<p>Example: A function accepts a sequence <code>a<\/code> and an\nidentifier <code>x<\/code>, and returns an index <code>p<\/code>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component<\/th>\n<th>Precise statement<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Precondition<\/td>\n<td>The sequence is finite and accessible; <code>x<\/code> can be\ncompared with its entries.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postcondition for <code>p &gt;= 0<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>p<\/code> is a valid position and <code>a[p] = x<\/code>.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postcondition for <code>p = -1<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>x<\/code> does not occur anywhere in the sequence.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not guaranteed<\/td>\n<td>A particular matching position if <code>x<\/code> occurs more than\nonce.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The postcondition requires <strong>more<\/strong> than ‘a value that\nis found is correct’: a procedure must also not silently miss a value\nthat is present. This second part is often forgotten in superficial\ntests.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"21-spezifikation-implementierung-und-umgebung\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Specification,\nimplementation and environment<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Domain requirement\n      |\n      v\nSpecification with pre-/postconditions\n      |\n      v\nAlgorithm + correctness argument\n      |\n      v\nConcrete implementation\n      |\n      v\nRuntime environment, data, hardware, concurrency<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A mathematical proof at the algorithm level does not automatically\napply to an incorrectly programmed procedure or an unreliable\nenvironment. Conversely, an individual real application may operate\nwithout faults for a long time without thereby covering every\ntheoretical input.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Partial and total correctness<\/h2>\n<p>In formal program analysis, two statements are distinguished:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Partial correctness:<\/strong> <em>If<\/em> the program\nterminates normally and the precondition holds, its result satisfies the\npostcondition.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Total correctness:<\/strong> The program satisfies the\npostcondition <strong>and terminates<\/strong> for every input satisfying\nits precondition, within the execution model used.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A function that remains in an infinite loop for certain inputs may be\npartially correct: in that case it produces no incorrect normal result.\nIt is not totally correct.<\/p>\n<p>The typical notation comes from <strong>Hoare logic<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>{ P }  C  { Q }\n\nP = precondition\nC = program or program segment\nQ = postcondition<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The <strong>Hoare triple<\/strong> usually describes a statement about\npartial correctness: if <code>P<\/code> holds before <code>C<\/code> and\n<code>C<\/code> terminates normally, <code>Q<\/code> holds afterwards. For\ntotal correctness, termination must also be demonstrated. Exceptions,\nside effects and concurrency require appropriately extended models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:<\/strong> ‘The loop terminates’ and ‘It\nproduces the correct result’ are <strong>different<\/strong> proof\nobjectives. A program that stops quickly can be wrong; a program that\nworks logically and carefully may never finish.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Loop invariants: What remains true on every iteration<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>invariant<\/strong> is a statement that remains true at\nspecified control points in a computation. For a loop, this is typically\nimmediately before each evaluation of the loop condition.<\/p>\n<p>Consider a linear search:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>p := 0\nwhile p &lt; length(a):\n    if a[p] = x:\n        return p\n    p := p + 1\nreturn -1<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A suitable invariant is:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Before each further iteration, <code>0 &lt;= p &lt;= length(a)<\/code>\nholds, and the sought value <code>x<\/code> does not occur in any of the\nalready examined positions <code>0<\/code> to <code>p-1<\/code>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Why is this useful? The invariant divides the list into an already\nexamined region and a region that has not yet been examined. This gives\na complete argument in four steps [1]:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Initialisation:<\/strong> Before the first iteration,\n<code>p = 0<\/code>. No positions have yet been examined. The statement\nabout the empty examined region is true.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Preservation:<\/strong> If <code>a[p]<\/code> equals\n<code>x<\/code>, a valid matching position is returned. If the entry\ndiffers, after <code>p := p + 1<\/code> the entire newly examined region\nstill contains no <code>x<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Postcondition:<\/strong> If the loop ends without a match,\n<code>p = length(a)<\/code> and every index has been excluded. Therefore\n<code>-1<\/code> is correct.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Termination:<\/strong> As long as there is no match, the\nnon-negative remaining quantity <code>length(a) - p<\/code> decreases by\none on each iteration. It cannot decrease strictly infinitely\noften.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>This already constitutes a small argument for <strong>total\ncorrectness<\/strong>, provided the defined comparison and memory\noperations terminate normally.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"41-durchgerechneter-verlauf\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked execution trace<\/h3>\n<p>Take <code>a = [4, 8, 13, 21]<\/code> and <code>x = 13<\/code>:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Start of iteration<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><code>p<\/code><\/th>\n<th>Already excluded entries<\/th>\n<th>Next check<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td>none<\/td>\n<td><code>4 = 13<\/code>? No<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td><code>4<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>8 = 13<\/code>? No<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td><code>4, 8<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>13 = 13<\/code>? Yes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The return value <code>2<\/code> is valid because\n<code>a[2] = 13<\/code>. For <code>x = 7<\/code>, the loop ends with\n<code>p = 4<\/code>; it is the invariant that justifies the return value\n<code>-1<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> If a data source is modified without\nsynchronisation during the search, the assumptions about an immutable\nsequence may no longer hold. A search algorithm that is correct on paper\ndoes not make concurrent writes safe.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Binary search: A stronger argument for a faster\nalgorithm<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Binary search<\/strong> uses an additional precondition: the\nsequence is <strong>sorted in ascending order<\/strong> according to the\ncomparison ordering used. Each comparison can then exclude approximately\nhalf of the remaining region.<\/p>\n<p>A particularly easy-to-verify variant uses a <strong>half-open\ninterval<\/strong> <code>[left, right)<\/code>: the index\n<code>left<\/code> belongs to the search region, but <code>right<\/code>\ndoes not.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>left  := 0\nright := length(a)\n\nwhile left &lt; right:\n    middle := left + (right - left) // 2\n    if a[middle] &lt; x:\n        left := middle + 1\n    else:\n        right := middle\n\nif left &lt; length(a) and a[left] = x:\n    return left\nreturn -1<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Here <code>//<\/code> denotes integer division rounded down. The\nprocedure determines the <strong>first index whose value is at least\n<code>x<\/code><\/strong>. This position is known as the <em>lower\nbound<\/em>. If <code>x<\/code> is actually present there, it is the\n<strong>first<\/strong> matching position; otherwise <code>x<\/code> does\nnot occur in the sequence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Invariant:<\/strong> All positions before <code>left<\/code>\ncontain values less than <code>x<\/code>. All positions from\n<code>right<\/code> onwards contain values greater than or equal to\n<code>x<\/code>. Both statements follow from the ordering and are\npreserved at every step. The region <code>[left, right)<\/code> therefore\ncontains all candidates not yet classified for the boundary\nposition.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If <code>a[middle] &lt; x<\/code>, all positions up to and including\n<code>middle<\/code> also contain values below <code>x<\/code>:\n<code>left<\/code> may advance to <code>middle + 1<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If <code>a[middle] &gt;= x<\/code>, <code>middle<\/code> belongs to\nthe right-hand side: <code>right<\/code> may move to\n<code>middle<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">As long as <code>left &lt; right<\/code>, the interval length\n<code>right - left<\/code> becomes strictly smaller. This ensures loop\ntermination.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At the end, <code>left = right<\/code>. This position is the first\nposition whose value is at least <code>x<\/code>, or the end of the\nsequence.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"51-beispiel-mit-mehrfach-vorkommendem-schluessel\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example with a\nrepeated key<\/h3>\n<p>For <code>a = [2, 5, 5, 5, 11, 18, 20, 30]<\/code> and\n<code>x = 5<\/code>, the following half-open search regions result:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th><code>[left, right)<\/code><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Middle<\/th>\n<th>Comparison<\/th>\n<th>New region<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Start<\/td>\n<td><code>[0,8)<\/code><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td><code>11 &gt;= 5<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>[0,4)<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continue<\/td>\n<td><code>[0,4)<\/code><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td><code>5 &gt;= 5<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>[0,2)<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continue<\/td>\n<td><code>[0,2)<\/code><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td><code>5 &gt;= 5<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>[0,1)<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continue<\/td>\n<td><code>[0,1)<\/code><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td><code>2 &lt; 5<\/code><\/td>\n<td><code>[1,1)<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The boundary position is <code>1<\/code>, the first of the three\nmatches. A small change in detail – the decision <code>&gt;=<\/code>\nrather than <code>&gt;<\/code> – changes the guaranteed meaning of the\nresult here.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"52-warum-die-laufzeit-logarithmisch-waechst\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why the running time\ngrows logarithmically<\/h3>\n<p>After <code>k<\/code> successful halvings, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac n{2^k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncandidates remain. The region is reduced to a tiny remainder at the\nlatest when <code>2^k<\/code> reaches approximately <code>n<\/code>, that\nis, when <code>k<\/code> is of the order of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe search time is therefore\n<strong><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\log  n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>\nif index access has constant cost.<\/p>\n<p>Example: With one million entries, about 20 halving steps are of the\nright order of magnitude, instead of up to one million linear\ncomparisons. This is an <strong>analytical statement about\ncomparisons<\/strong>, not a guaranteed time in milliseconds. If the list\nis not sorted or does not support efficient direct positional access,\nthe specified method or running-time assumption does not apply unchanged\n[2].<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursion, induction and termination measures<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>recursive<\/strong> function calls itself on smaller\ninstances of a problem. The proof often follows <strong>mathematical\ninduction<\/strong>: if a procedure is correct for all sufficiently small\nsubproblems and constructs a correct larger result from these correct\nresults, it can also be correct as a whole.<\/p>\n<p>Example: <strong>Merge Sort<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A sequence with zero or one element is already sorted.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A longer sequence is divided into two shorter subsequences.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both subsequences are sorted recursively.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A merging procedure selects the smaller of the two leading elements\neach time until all elements have been output.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The essential <strong>merge invariant<\/strong> states that the output\nalready produced is sorted and contains exactly the elements removed so\nfar; its next possible value can only be at the head of one of the two\nremaining subsequences. This justifies the correctness of merging;\ninduction justifies the correctness of the entire sorting procedure.<\/p>\n<p>For termination, it is sufficient that recursive calls with input\nsize\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n &gt; 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreceive <strong>strictly smaller<\/strong> inputs and eventually reach\nthe base case. More generally, this is called a <strong>well-founded\nmeasure<\/strong>: there must be no infinitely long sequence of strictly\ndecreasing values. Besides natural numbers, suitable orderings over\ntuples or data structures are possible.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Caution:<\/strong> A call with ‘almost the same’ problem is\nnot enough. If the partitioning occasionally produces a subproblem of\nthe original size again, termination is not thereby proved.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Time and space complexity: What grows with the input?<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Asymptotic analysis<\/strong> describes the growth of a\nresource cost <code>T(n)<\/code> with input size <code>n<\/code>. For this\nparticular question, it disregards constant factors and smaller\nterms.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_046_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Growth of the functions log n, n, n² and 2 to the power of n\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: For input sizes one to six, log₂ n, n, n² and 2ⁿ\ngrow at different rates; the curves show function growth, not measured\nruntimes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Notation<\/th>\n<th>Typical statement<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(g(n))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Asymptotic upper bound<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(n)\\le c\\cdot g(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor sufficiently large <code>n<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega (g(n))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Asymptotic lower bound<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(n)\\ge c\\cdot g(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor sufficiently large <code>n<\/code><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Theta (g(n))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>Upper and lower bounds of the same order of magnitude<\/td>\n<td><code>T(n)<\/code> grows proportionally to the order of magnitude of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The constants are positive in each case and apply from a suitable\nthreshold in <code>n<\/code>. As an <strong>upper bound<\/strong>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^{2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not mean that the cost necessarily grows quadratically: a linear\nalgorithm also belongs to the set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^{2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a tight classification, <code>Θ(n)<\/code> would be more\ninformative.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"71-best-case-average-case-und-worst-case\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Best case, average\ncase and worst case<\/h3>\n<p>Complexity depends on the input distribution under consideration:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Best case:<\/strong> A particularly favourable input, such as\nan immediate match in a linear search.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Worst case:<\/strong> The least favourable input of the size\nconsidered.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Average case:<\/strong> The average over an\n<strong>explicitly defined probability distribution<\/strong> of inputs.\nWithout this distribution, the average case is not precisely\nspecified.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For an unindexed linear search, the best case is <code>Θ(1)<\/code>\nand the worst case is <code>Θ(n)<\/code>. If a sought entry is equally\nlikely to occur at any of the <code>n<\/code> positions, the mean number\nof comparisons is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{n+1}{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence <code>Θ(n)<\/code>. The distributional assumption is crucial.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"72-speicher-ist-eine-eigene-ressource\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Memory is a separate\nresource<\/h3>\n<p>A procedure with good running time may require a great deal of\nmemory. In a typical array implementation, for example, Merge Sort needs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadditional memory for merging. Some in-place sorting methods require\nconsiderably less auxiliary memory but have different time or error\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<p>For network applications, bytes transferred, number of messages,\ndatabase accesses, energy and waiting times also matter. A function that\ntheoretically performs only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlocal computational work may trigger an expensive external database\nquery on every operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two complete growth comparisons<\/h2>\n<h3\nid=\"81-n2-gegenueber-nlog2n\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^{2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nversus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\cdot \\log_2(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/h3>\n<p>Let us compare two idealised comparison counts without confusing them\nwith real running times:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Input size <code>n<\/code><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\cdot\\log_2(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(rounded)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000,000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9,966<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000,000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1,000,000,000,000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">19,931,569<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The input becomes a thousand times larger. The quadratic expression\ngrows by a factor of <strong>1,000,000<\/strong>, whereas the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\cdot \\log_2(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpression grows by only about a factor of <strong>2,000<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>This explains why an initially simple nested loop may become\nproblematic as the amount of data grows. It does not prove that a\nparticular existing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n \\log  n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimplementation is faster than another\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^{2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimplementation at as few as 1,000 records: memory accesses and constants\ncan dominate for small inputs.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"82-eine-rekurrenz-schritt-fuer-schritt\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A recurrence step by\nstep<\/h3>\n<p>For Merge Sort, we can make the simplifying assumption that\n<code>n<\/code> is a power of two and merging costs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\cdot n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntime:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nT(n)&amp;=2\\cdot T(n/2)+c\\cdot n\\\\\nT(1)&amp;=d\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At the first level, <code>n<\/code> elements are merged; at the next\nlevel, there are again <code>n<\/code> elements in total across two\nsubproblems; at every further level, there are likewise approximately\n<code>n<\/code>. There are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuch levels. Together, the leaves incur a linear base cost. It follows\nthat:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nT(n)&amp;=c\\cdot n\\cdot\\log_2(n)+d\\cdot n\\\\\n&amp;=\\Theta(n\\log n)\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This calculation assumes correct partitioning and a linear-time merge\nprocedure. If a real implementation uses slow data structures or creates\nadditional copies, the costs must be analysed again.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lower bounds: Why some shortcuts are impossible<\/h2>\n<p>Analysis can show not only how fast <strong>a known<\/strong>\nprocedure is. Sometimes it can also be proved that <strong>no procedure\nin a clearly defined class<\/strong> can go below a certain bound.<\/p>\n<p>For sorting <code>n<\/code> distinct elements using <strong>only\npairwise comparisons<\/strong>, there are <code>n!<\/code> possible\norderings. Each comparison has at most two outcomes; a binary decision\ntree needs a depth of at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(n!)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto distinguish <code>n!<\/code> terminal cases. This expression grows as\n<code>Ω(n log n)<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>Comparison-based sorting algorithms therefore require\n<code>Ω(n log n)<\/code> comparisons in the worst case. Procedures using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n \\log  n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomparisons are asymptotically optimal with respect to this\n<strong>model<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The restriction is essential:<\/strong> Counting Sort or Radix\nSort exploit additional structure of the keys and are not pure\ncomparison sorts. They therefore do not refute the comparison lower\nbound. The statement ‘Sorting can never be linear’ would be technically\nincorrect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Amortised analysis: An expensive operation need not be\nexpensive on average<\/h2>\n<p>When its capacity is exhausted, a dynamic array can move to a larger\nmemory region. An individual growth step may copy <code>n<\/code>\nelements and cost\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNevertheless, appending over <strong>a sequence<\/strong> of operations\ncan be very inexpensive.<\/p>\n<p>Assume an idealised <strong>doubling<\/strong> of capacity: when\ngrowing from 1 to 2, 2 to 4, 4 to 8 and so on, copying costs of\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+2+4+\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare incurred. For up to <code>n<\/code> append operations this sum\nremains below a constant multiple of <code>n<\/code>. Including the\nactual <code>n<\/code> insertions gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntotal cost, hence <strong>amortised\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper append<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Amortised<\/strong> does not mean a random or empirically\nmeasured average time. It is a mathematical statement about the\n<strong>entire sequence of costs<\/strong>, even if individual operations\nare expensive. Anyone who needs to meet a hard response-time limit on\neach individual call cannot rely on this guarantee alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision problems and optimisation problems<\/h2>\n<p>Complexity theory often studies <strong>decision problems<\/strong>\nwith a <code>Yes<\/code> or <code>No<\/code> answer. An optimisation\nproblem, in contrast, seeks the best possible solution. This distinction\nfacilitates precise statements.<\/p>\n<p>Example of a route through a graph:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Decision:<\/strong> ‘Is there a round trip visiting every\nrequired stop and costing at most 100 cost units?’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Optimisation:<\/strong> ‘What is the cost of the shortest\nsuch round trip?’<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The decision version may be used as a building block for studying the\noptimisation version. The two are related, but they do not express the\nsame task. Input size must also be measured correctly: a number\n<code>N<\/code> requires only approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(N)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits in binary notation. An algorithm whose running time grows\nproportionally to the <strong>numeric value<\/strong> <code>N<\/code> is\ntherefore not necessarily polynomial in the <strong>bit length of the\ninput<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>P, NP, NP-completeness and NP-hardness<\/h2>\n<p><strong>P<\/strong> comprises decision problems for which a solution\nusing a <strong>polynomial<\/strong> number of steps in the input length\nis known on a conventional deterministic theoretical model of\ncomputation. <strong>NP<\/strong> comprises decision problems whose\nyes-answers can be verified in polynomial time using a suitable\npolynomial-length <strong>certificate<\/strong>; the original formal\ndefinition uses nondeterministic computation. We have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>⊆<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P \\subseteq  \\mathrm{NP}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For example, for the question of whether a graph has a path visiting\neach vertex exactly once, a proposed path is easy to verify. However, no\nequally fast procedure for finding the path follows automatically from\nthis.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class/concept<\/th>\n<th>Precise interpretation<\/th>\n<th>Common misconception<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>P<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Decidable in polynomial time<\/td>\n<td>‘Always fast in practice’ – high polynomial degrees can be\nimpractical<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NP<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Yes-certificates can be verified in polynomial time<\/td>\n<td>‘Non-polynomial’ – this is precisely not what NP means<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NP-hard<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>At least as hard as all problems in NP under the reductions\nused<\/td>\n<td>Need not itself belong to NP<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NP-complete<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Belongs to NP <strong>and<\/strong> is NP-hard<\/td>\n<td>Is not ‘unsolvable’ by definition<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The famous <strong>P versus NP<\/strong> problem asks whether every\nyes-solution that can be verified in polynomial time can also be found\nin polynomial time, or whether the decision problem can be solved in\npolynomial time. As of the <strong>verification date\n05.11.2026<\/strong>, the Clay Mathematics Institute still lists it as\n<strong>unsolved<\/strong> [3]. In particular, it has <strong>not been\nproved<\/strong> that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P \\ne  \\mathrm{NP}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mere observation that no fast algorithm is known today is not a\nsubstitute for such a proof.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"121-wie-man-np-schwere-mittels-reduktion-begruendet\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How to\nestablish NP-hardness by reduction<\/h3>\n<p>A <strong>reduction<\/strong> efficiently transforms every input of a\nproblem <code>A<\/code> into an input of <code>B<\/code>, preserving the\nyes-or-no answer. The notation for a polynomial-time reduction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\le_p B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Instance of A\n      |\n      v\nPolynomial-time transformation\n      |\n      v\nInstance of B --&gt; Answer by procedure for B\n      |\n      v\nCorresponding answer for A<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>To show that <code>B<\/code> is NP-hard, a problem <code>A<\/code>\n<strong>already known to be NP-hard<\/strong> is reduced <strong>to\nB<\/strong>. If <code>B<\/code> could be solved quickly, <code>A<\/code>\ncould then also be solved quickly. The direction of the reduction is\nimportant: accidentally reducing <code>B<\/code> to a hard <code>A<\/code>\nhas not established NP-hardness of <code>B<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>SAT<\/strong> (<em>Boolean Satisfiability Problem<\/em>, the\nsatisfiability problem for propositional logic) is a classic NP-complete\nstarting point. A formula is satisfiable if there is an assignment of\ntruth values that makes it true. A certificate is precisely such an\nassignment. Very powerful solvers often exist for practical SAT\ninstances; NP-completeness does not mean that <strong>every<\/strong>\nparticular formula is hard [4].<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What if the exact solution is too expensive?<\/h2>\n<p>A theoretically hard class of problems does not make practical work\npointless. Other objectives frequently suffice:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Special cases:<\/strong> A problem becomes efficient under\nadditional structural assumptions. Some graph classes permit\nconsiderably simpler procedures than arbitrary graphs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Heuristics:<\/strong> A procedure often provides useful\nsolutions, but without a general quality guarantee.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Approximation algorithms:<\/strong> Under specified\nassumptions, an algorithm guarantees a defined closeness to the\noptimum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Parameterised algorithms:<\/strong> Difficulty is\nadditionally captured by a parameter <code>k<\/code>. In\n<strong>fixed-parameter tractability (FPT)<\/strong>, for example, the\nrunning time may be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(k)\\cdot n^c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor small <code>k<\/code> this may be favourable in practice.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Branch and bound / bounded search:<\/strong> Lower or upper\nbounds eliminate unpromising subregions. In the worst case the cost can\nnevertheless remain exponential.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Time limit with the best result so far:<\/strong> If\nnecessary, the procedure returns an incomplete or suboptimal solution\nexplicitly labelled as such.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For route planning with ten destinations, an exact search may work\nwell. For thousands of destinations and many constraints, a heuristic\nmay be the more sensible operational decision. The quality of the result\nshould be judged on the requirements and available checks, not merely on\nan impressive algorithm name.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computability: Even unlimited computing power cannot solve\neverything<\/h2>\n<p>So far we have considered <strong>hard<\/strong> but in principle\ncomputable tasks. <strong>Computability theory<\/strong> asks the more\nfundamental question of whether a general mechanical decision procedure\nexists at all.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>Turing machine<\/strong> is an abstract model of computation\nwith finite control and a potentially unbounded tape. It is not a\nconstruction guide for a modern computer. Various classical\nformalisations of mechanical computation lead to the same computable\nfunctions. The <strong>Church–Turing thesis<\/strong> connects these\nmathematical models with the informal concept of an effectively\nexecutable computational method. It is not a single mathematical\nequation, provable in the usual sense, between two independently\nformally defined concepts [5].<\/p>\n<p>The important conceptual distinction:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Decidable:<\/strong> There is an algorithm that halts on\n<strong>every<\/strong> permissible input and provides the correct\nyes-or-no answer.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Semi-decidable / recursively enumerable:<\/strong> An\nalgorithm can eventually confirm a yes-answer; in the no-case it may\ncontinue indefinitely.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Undecidable:<\/strong> No comprehensive, always-terminating\ndecision algorithm exists.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Undecidability does <strong>not<\/strong> mean ‘too slow on current\nhardware’. It means a mathematically proved limit of the computational\nmodel used.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The halting problem: A surprising counterexample<\/h2>\n<p>The general <strong>halting problem<\/strong> asks:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Given the description of any program <code>P<\/code> and any\npermissible input <code>x<\/code>: will <code>P<\/code> eventually halt on\n<code>x<\/code>?<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>Suppose a perfect checking program <code>HALT(P, x)<\/code> could\nalways correctly report <code>YES<\/code> or <code>NO<\/code> for\n<strong>every<\/strong> such pair and always terminate itself. We then\ndefine a program <code>CONTRADICTION(Q)<\/code>:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>if HALT(Q, Q) = YES:\n    run forever\nelse:\n    halt immediately<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Now we ask what happens when\n<code>CONTRADICTION(CONTRADICTION)<\/code> is called:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If <code>HALT<\/code> says <code>YES<\/code> for this program, the\ndefinition of <code>CONTRADICTION<\/code> causes it to <strong>run\nindefinitely<\/strong>. The prediction would be wrong.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If <code>HALT<\/code> says <code>NO<\/code>, the definition causes it\nto <strong>halt immediately<\/strong>. This prediction would also be\nwrong.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Both possibilities lead to a contradiction. Therefore no such\nuniversal <code>HALT<\/code> algorithm can exist. This type of argument\nis called <strong>diagonalisation<\/strong> [5]. The proof assumes that\nthe hypothetical checking program correctly handles arbitrary programs\nof the universal model considered, including the suitably constructed\nself-application.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"151-was-diese-aussage-nicht-bedeutet\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What this statement does\nnot mean<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For many <strong>specific<\/strong> programs, termination can indeed\nbe proved, for example using a strictly decreasing natural number.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Programming tools can often detect loop errors or verify termination\nfor particular classes of programs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A test with a time limit can say: ‘Has not terminated so far.’ In\ngeneral it cannot infer from that: ‘Will never terminate.’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If a known program call halts, the yes-answer can be observed by\nactually executing it. In a non-halting computation, however, there is\ngenerally no finite point at which a definitive no-answer can be\ninferred from waiting alone.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The limit is therefore precise: <strong>No universally valid,\nalways-terminating halting decision for all programs and\ninputs.<\/strong> It by no means follows that ordinary software\nverification is useless.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A map of the limits<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<th>Type of limit<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the procedure correct?<\/td>\n<td>A search must not miss existing IDs<\/td>\n<td>Proof/specification question<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How much work does it require?<\/td>\n<td>Sorting by comparisons<\/td>\n<td>Complexity and modelling question<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is there an efficient general algorithm?<\/td>\n<td>NP-complete decision problem<\/td>\n<td>Open complexity question in general; special cases solvable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is there a universal decider at all?<\/td>\n<td>General halting problem<\/td>\n<td>Proved undecidability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Will the system operate reliably on real computers?<\/td>\n<td>Service during a network outage<\/td>\n<td>Empirical and operational question in addition to theory<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This classification avoids a common error in reasoning: a very hard\noptimisation problem, a mathematically undecidable problem and a simply\npoorly programmed application are three different reasons for ‘it does\nnot work’.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Formal methods in real development processes<\/h2>\n<p>Not every correctness statement has to be proved by hand. Various\ntechniques are available under the umbrella term <strong>formal\nmethods<\/strong>, for example:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Contracts and assertions:<\/strong> Preconditions and\npostconditions, as well as assumptions checked at runtime. An assertion\ncan detect an error, but a runtime check does not automatically prove\nevery possible execution.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Static analysis and type systems:<\/strong> Examine code\nwithout complete program execution. Precision and guarantees depend on\nthe particular method and its assumptions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Model checking:<\/strong> Systematically explores states and\ntransitions of a formal model; finds counterexamples to specified\nproperties if they are reachable within the model examined.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>SAT/SMT solvers:<\/strong> SAT solves propositional logic;\n<strong>SMT<\/strong> (<em>Satisfiability Modulo Theories<\/em>)\nadditionally incorporates mathematical theories such as those of\nintegers, bit vectors or equations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Interactive proof systems:<\/strong> Assist in constructing\nand machine-checking formal proofs. The proof holds relative to the\nformalisations, assumptions and trusted checking component.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Testing and runtime observation:<\/strong> Examine specific\ncases or actual execution sequences. They complement formal methods but\ncannot replace their general guarantees without further arguments.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"171-eine-kleine-zustandsmaschine\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A small state machine<\/h3>\n<p>An import job might have the states <code>new<\/code>,\n<code>waiting<\/code>, <code>running<\/code>, <code>finished<\/code> and\n<code>failed<\/code>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>[new] --&gt; [waiting] --&gt; [running] --&gt; [finished]\n                            |\n                            +----------&gt; [failed]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A formal model could require that a job is <strong>never\nboth<\/strong> <code>finished<\/code> and <code>failed<\/code>. This is a\n<strong>safety property<\/strong>: ‘Something bad never happens.’ A\n<strong>liveness property<\/strong> could require that every accepted job\nis eventually completed under certain fairness and availability\nassumptions: ‘Something good eventually happens.’ This distinction is\ncentral to concurrent and distributed systems [6].<\/p>\n<p>A model checker can systematically search possible sequences in a\nfinite, fully represented state space. Large or infinite state spaces\nrequire abstraction, bounds or additional proof techniques.\n<strong>Successful bounded checking is not an automatic proof for every\ninfinitely large real execution.<\/strong> For TLA+ and PlusCal, the\ndocumentation accordingly distinguishes model checking from formal proof\n[7].<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three practical architectures with different proof\nobjectives<\/h2>\n<h3\nid=\"181-lokales-medienarchiv-keine-verlorenen-oder-doppelten-datensaetze\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local\nmedia archive: No lost or duplicate records<\/h3>\n<p>A local program reads files and writes corresponding entries to a\nmedia catalogue. The domain requirement is ‘complete import without\nduplicates’.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Source files\n    |\n    v\nIdentifier check\n    |\n    v\nTransaction --&gt; new catalogue entry\n    |                 |\n    +--&gt; Error -------+--&gt; rollback / log<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>correctness invariant<\/strong> might state that each\nexternal identifier occurs at most once in the target table. The\ndatabase supports this rule with a unique constraint (<em>UNIQUE\nconstraint<\/em>). The program must also decide how to handle an already\nimported record on a retry. The requirement for\n<strong>idempotency<\/strong> concerns the domain effect, not the number\nof incoming messages.<\/p>\n<p><strong>What is proved?<\/strong> Under assumptions about\ntransactions, unique keys and error handling, the objective can be\nformally specified. <strong>What remains open?<\/strong> Whether files\nremain available, whether correct identifiers are assigned, and whether\na crash before commit causes data loss in the source system. Such\nquestions require further system models and recovery tests.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> As soon as duplicate or missing\nentries would be costly to correct. <strong>When is it\nexcessive?<\/strong> For a one-off catalogue of a few easily checked\nentries.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"182-eine-datenbankueberweisung-ganz-oder-gar-nicht\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A database\ntransfer: ‘All or nothing’<\/h3>\n<p>Two accounts have balances <code>A<\/code> and <code>B<\/code>. A\nsuccessful transfer of an amount <code>t<\/code> should result in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A&#39;=A-t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B&#39;=B+t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAssuming no fees are charged and no other operations access the accounts\nconcurrently, the following <strong>conservation invariant<\/strong>\nholds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A&#39;+B&#39;=(A-t)+(B+t)=A+B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This simple equation demonstrates the required domain consistency of\nthe planned change. However, a system additionally needs rules for\nsufficient funds, rounding, concurrency, errors, retries, authorisation\nand durability. A database transaction supports atomic changes. It does\nnot automatically prove that the entire user action – including, for\ninstance, an external payment confirmation – is atomic.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operational consequence:<\/strong> Correct locking or\nversioning strategies may reduce throughput; additional checks and\nauditable logs require development and maintenance. Real payment systems\nare also subject to security and regulatory requirements that this\nillustrative equation does not cover.<\/p>\n<h3\nid=\"183-cloud-auftragsverarbeitung-korrekt-bei-wiederholung-aber-irgendwann-fertig\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cloud\njob processing: Correct on retries, but eventually finished?<\/h3>\n<p>A web service accepts requests and places them in a queue. Several\nworkers read and process them. Jobs can be delivered more than once in\nthe event of outages or timeouts.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Web API --&gt; Queue --&gt; Worker\n              |          |\n              |          v\n              +-- Retry --&gt; Database<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A <strong>safety property<\/strong> would be that at most one\ndomain-level result entry is created for the same job identifier. A\n<strong>liveness property<\/strong> would be that a valid job that has\nbeen durably accepted is eventually processed. The second property\nrequires assumptions about workers remaining available, fair scheduling\nand retries eventually succeeding. Without such assumptions, completion\ncannot be forced from infrastructure that continues to fail\nindefinitely.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> When high reliability in the\npresence of repeated messages matters. <strong>When is it\nunnecessary?<\/strong> For a purely local script with no queue.\n<strong>Cost of the guarantee:<\/strong> Additional state management,\nmonitoring, retry rules, storage, operation and fault diagnosis.<\/p>\n<p>A more rigorous analysis of the distributed aspects follows in\n<strong>M7-L3<\/strong>. A local correctness proof cannot simply be\ninterpreted as a guarantee for an entire system distributed across\nnetworks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources of error that can invalidate even good proofs<\/h2>\n<p>A formally correct model is only as useful as the correspondence\nbetween its assumptions and the eventual system. Particularly important\nexamples are:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hidden assumption<\/th>\n<th>Possible consequence<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Integers are unbounded<\/td>\n<td>Overflow with fixed-width integer types<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparisons are exact<\/td>\n<td>Rounding errors with floating-point numbers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All inputs are valid<\/td>\n<td>Crash or incorrect results with corrupt data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data remains unchanged during computation<\/td>\n<td>Race condition due to concurrent changes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jobs arrive exactly once<\/td>\n<td>Duplicate entries due to retries<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resources are unlimited<\/td>\n<td>Timeout, memory exhaustion, outage<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The model captures all important states<\/td>\n<td>Undetected error outside the model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Security is also a correctness question <strong>under a threat\nmodel<\/strong>: a correct function may work correctly for ordinary users\nand be vulnerable to deliberately malicious inputs. A mathematical proof\nabout a sorting function is not a security assessment of the entire\nprogram.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How much formality is appropriate?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Project<\/th>\n<th>Sensible approach<\/th>\n<th>Likely excessive<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Short local utility<\/td>\n<td>Clear inputs, simple tests, comprehensible error cases<\/td>\n<td>Complete formal proof of every helper routine<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large-scale data import<\/td>\n<td>Unique keys, contracts, transactions, repeatability, recovery\ntest<\/td>\n<td>Complete theoretical analysis of the entire operating system<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Demanding optimisation<\/td>\n<td>Resource model, complexity estimate, benchmarks, approximation where\nappropriate<\/td>\n<td>A universal exact solution at any cost<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distributed job processing<\/td>\n<td>State model, safety/liveness properties, assumptions about outages\nand retries<\/td>\n<td>Claim of absolute reliability without a failure model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety- or time-critical device<\/td>\n<td>More rigorous specification, verifiable invariants, runtime bounds,\nindependent assessment<\/td>\n<td>Exclusive reliance on a few successful demonstrations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Cost question:<\/strong> Formal methods require precise\nrequirements, skilled work and continuous maintenance when changes\noccur. Their benefit increases with the consequences of an error and the\ndifficulty of detecting errors retrospectively. Even a perfect formal\nspecification may precisely prove the <strong>wrong<\/strong> domain\nobjective; validation of the actual need as discussed in M7-L1 therefore\nremains necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide: Which evidence answers which question?<\/h2>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>What is to be demonstrated?\n        |\n        +--&gt; &quot;Always correct under assumptions?&quot;\n        |       -&gt; Specification, invariant, proof\n        |\n        +--&gt; &quot;Does it terminate?&quot;\n        |       -&gt; Termination argument, well-founded measure\n        |\n        +--&gt; &quot;How does the cost scale?&quot;\n        |       -&gt; Complexity model + real measurements\n        |\n        +--&gt; &quot;Is it decidable in principle?&quot;\n        |       -&gt; Computability theory, possibly reduction\n        |\n        +--&gt; &quot;Does the actual software work?&quot;\n                -&gt; Implementation review, tests, operation<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The strongest approach combines appropriate answers instead of\ndeclaring one method a universal solution. It is possible to prove that\nan algorithm computes the correct solution, derive a running-time upper\nbound and then check on real hardware whether the solution is suitable\nunder actual load conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S22\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M7-L3<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Correctness<\/strong> starts with a precise specification.\nPreconditions, postconditions, loop invariants and termination measures\njustify why a procedure works and terminates. <strong>Complexity\nanalysis<\/strong>, by contrast, describes resource costs as a function\nof input size. An asymptotic upper bound is not the same as an actual\nrunning-time guarantee. Finally, <strong>computability theory<\/strong>\nshows that even with unlimited idealised time and memory some general\ndecisions – such as the halting problem – are not algorithmically\npossible.<\/p>\n<p>Three principles connect these perspectives:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>A correct result is not the same as guaranteed\ncompletion.<\/strong> Partial and total correctness differ.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Hard does not mean impossible.<\/strong> NP-completeness and\nundecidability describe different phenomena; P versus NP remains\nopen.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>A proof proves exactly the modelled statement.<\/strong> Real\ncomponents, networks, security requirements and human expectations must\nalso be considered.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Continue reading in the completed core path:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3 – Distributed systems as formal\nand practical system models<\/a>. Network delay, the absence of a common\nclock, partial failures, replication, consensus and system guarantees\nare examined together there. The eight optional Master’s lessons remain\norganisationally separate and are developed only upon explicit\nselection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S23\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Position within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This chapter belongs to the <strong>six-part Master’s core<\/strong>,\nnot the optional additional Master’s topics. Navigation: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">previous Master’s core lesson\nM7-L1<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">next Master’s core\nlesson M7-L3<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">reading paths and\nsubject-related cross-references<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S24\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 05.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>The following sources support the theoretical foundations and tools\ndiscussed. The data series, search examples, diagrams and three\npractical cases in this lesson are original <strong>teaching\nexamples<\/strong>, not published measurements or research results.\n<strong>Verification date: 05.11.2026.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Cornell University, CS 2110 – Loop Invariants\n(2026):<\/strong> Initialisation, preservation, progress and termination\nof loops. <a\nhref=\"https://courses.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs2110/2026fa/lectures/lec04/\">https://courses.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs2110/2026fa/lectures/lec04/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Cornell University, CS 2110 – Loop Invariants\n(2022):<\/strong> Detailed proof framework for binary search with an\ninvariant and a termination argument. <a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs2110/2022fa/lectures/loopinv/\">https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs2110/2022fa/lectures/loopinv/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Clay Mathematics Institute – P versus NP:<\/strong> The\nstatus of the problem is still listed as <em>Unsolved<\/em>; the question\nremains open. <a\nhref=\"https://www.claymath.org/millennium/p-vs-np/\">https://www.claymath.org/millennium/p-vs-np/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>MIT OpenCourseWare, 6.006 – Introduction to Algorithms,\nSyllabus:<\/strong> Distinction between algorithm description,\ncorrectness argument and time/space analysis; <a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-fall-2011/pages/syllabus/\">https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-fall-2011/pages/syllabus/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy – The Church-Turing\nThesis / Entscheidungsproblem:<\/strong> Effective computation, the\nChurch–Turing thesis and limits of algorithmic decidability. <a\nhref=\"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/church-turing/\">https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/church-turing/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/church-turing/decision-problem.html\">https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/church-turing/decision-problem.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Leslie Lamport – Safety, Liveness, and Fairness\n(2019):<\/strong> Mathematical distinction between safety and liveness\nproperties. <a\nhref=\"https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/safety-liveness.pdf\">https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/safety-liveness.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Leslie Lamport – PlusCal Tutorial and TLA+ Tools:<\/strong>\nDescription of the translation from PlusCal into TLA+, model checking\nwith TLC and its relationship to proof tools. <a\nhref=\"https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/tutorial/intro.html\">https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/tutorial/intro.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/tools.html\">https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/tla/tools.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Context:<\/strong> Sources [1] and [2] support the proof\ntechnique; the specific example programs are independently formulated\nhere. [3] confirms the time-dependent research status, not a general\nstatement about the practical difficulty of every NP instance. [6] and\n[7] explain formal tools but expressly do not establish that the three\nexample systems have already been formally verified. The entire\npresentation is a reading course, not a software certificate.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M7-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M7-L3 – Distributed systems as formal and practical system models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Master’s-oriented computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master’s orientation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Note:<\/strong> Objective: not merely to know that several\ncomputers can cooperate, but to identify precisely which ordering,\nconsistency, availability and fault tolerance guarantees a design\noffers, and under what assumptions. The teaching diagrams and numbers\nare constructed examples, not measured performance figures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The real challenge: There is no shared instant<\/h2>\n<p>A single program can often be described as follows: there is a\ncurrent memory state, then one step takes place, then the next. In a\ndistributed system, several processes cooperate with their own memories,\nclocks and failures. Messages take time; they can be delayed, reordered,\nduplicated or, depending on transmission and the type of failure, never\ndelivered at all. Each process therefore sees only part of the entire\nsystem.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>      Service A                 Service B\n      own state                 own state\n          |                         |\n          | ---- Message 1 -------&gt; |\n          |      Delay?             |\n          | &lt;---- Message 2 ------- |\n          v                         v\n      local clock               local clock<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>‘Computer B is not responding’ is not yet an unambiguous diagnosis. B\nmay have crashed; the connection may be disrupted; B may be working and\nmerely lose its response; B may simply be slow. This distinction is not\npedantry: the diagnosis determines whether repeating an operation\nduplicates data, whether another machine may take over, or whether a\nuser temporarily receives no decision.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>distributed system<\/strong> is therefore not merely ‘an\napplication on multiple servers’. It is a system in which\n<strong>incomplete knowledge of other participants<\/strong> itself\nbecomes a design problem. The academic approach begins by explicitly\ndefining observations, desired properties and permitted failures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From an architecture diagram to a verifiable model<\/h2>\n<p>A diagram labelled ‘API → database → three replicas’ explains\n<em>which components<\/em> exist, not <em>how they behave in the event of\nfailures<\/em>. A formal system model adds at least five details:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Participants:<\/strong> for example, clients, three database\nreplicas and a coordinator.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Local state:<\/strong> data values, version numbers, jobs not\nyet acknowledged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Transitions:<\/strong> reading, writing, sending or receiving\nmessages, restarting, crashing.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Environmental assumptions:<\/strong> message delay, clocks,\npermitted failures, storage durability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Properties:<\/strong> What must never happen? What progress\nmust eventually take place under which conditions?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A greatly simplified model of a replicated setting is:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>State: value per replica, version number per replica\nActions: write attempt, replication message,\n         receipt, read, failure, reconnection\nSafety objective: No change declared committed may be lost\n                  within the defined failure model\nLiveness objective: With enough reachable replicas,\n                    a permitted change can be committed<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>These objectives require additional precise definitions: what does\n‘committed’ mean? How many replicas must respond? What happens if a\nreplica crashes after receipt but before durable storage? An\narchitecture diagram alone cannot decide this.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> A model may abstract. It need not contain\nevery byte buffer or every CPU instruction. However, it must not omit\nprecisely those failures about which it is meant to make a\nguarantee.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing safety and liveness properties<\/h2>\n<p>Two terms come from M7-L2:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Safety (safety property):<\/strong> Something prohibited\nnever happens. Example: two simultaneously active leaders must not both\nestablish conflicting binding values for the same log position.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Liveness (progress property):<\/strong> Under specified\nconditions, something desirable eventually happens. Example: once enough\nhealthy participants communicate reliably, a job can be completed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The distinction explains an uncomfortable operational decision: a\nsystem can <strong>stop<\/strong> when the situation is unclear in order\nto prevent conflicting transactions. It then violates an availability\nrequirement but preserves a safety requirement. Conversely, a system may\nprovide responses from disconnected regions and thus remain available,\nbut must later reconcile conflicting changes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>A correctness proof is always conditional:<\/strong> anyone\nwishing to show that a replicated system is safe must explicitly state\nits failure model and guarantees. The blanket claim ‘highly available\nand consistent’ is insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Time, event ordering and ‘happened-before’<\/h2>\n<p>In 1978, Leslie Lamport showed why the order of distributed events\ncannot simply be read from the clocks of individual computers. The key\nis <strong>causal ordering<\/strong> (<em>happened-before<\/em>, written\n<code>a → b<\/code>):<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two events in the same process are ordered according to its local\nexecution order.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Sending a message happens before receiving it.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The relation is transitive: if <code>a → b<\/code> and\n<code>b → c<\/code>, then <code>a → c<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Process A:  a1 --send M-- a2 -------- a3\n                    |\n                    | Message M\n                    v\nProcess B:  b1 ---receive M--- b2 --- b3<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Sending <code>M<\/code> causally precedes its receipt. By contrast, no\ncausal relationship may be identifiable between <code>a1<\/code> and\n<code>b1<\/code>. Such events are considered <strong>concurrent<\/strong>\nin the model: neither <code>a1 → b1<\/code> nor <code>b1 → a1<\/code> need\nhold. This does not mean that their physical order could never be\ndiscovered, but that it cannot be inferred from the communication\nrelations under consideration.<\/p>\n<p>The event order is a <strong>partial order<\/strong>. There is\nnormally no single global order immediately known to every participant.\nAn artificial total order can be agreed upon technically; it is not\nautomatically the actual causal order.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Logical clocks and vector clocks<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>Lamport clock<\/strong> uses integers instead of system\ntime. In simplified terms: before a local event, a process increments\nits counter; a message carries the current counter; on receipt, the\nrecipient sets its counter to <code>max(local, received) + 1<\/code>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>A: Event (1) -- sends M(2) ----------&gt; (further steps)\nB: Event (1) ------ receives M -----&gt;  max(1,2)+1 = 3<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>It follows that if <code>a → b<\/code>, the Lamport timestamp of\n<code>a<\/code> is less than that of <code>b<\/code>. <strong>The converse\ndoes not hold.<\/strong> Smaller timestamps do not prove that the events\nare causally related.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Vector clocks<\/strong>, by contrast, store one counter\ncomponent per process considered. With two participants, A might carry\n<code>[1,0]<\/code> after a local event, and B independently carry\n<code>[0,1]<\/code>. These vectors are incomparable component by\ncomponent: neither is less than or equal to the other in every\ncomponent. This demonstrates the concurrency of the two events in the\nusual vector-clock model. After receiving a message sent by A, B would\nmerge the components and increment its own component, for example to\n<code>[1,2]<\/code> – depending on the specific preceding sequence of\nevents.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> Vector clocks require metadata and\nparticipant identities. Managing these is costly in very large or\nchanging groups of participants. They also do not automatically resolve\nconflicts between two concurrently modified records; initially, they\nhelp to <em>detect<\/em> the conflict.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical clocks are useful, but do not magically establish\norder<\/h2>\n<p>Real systems need times for logs, expiry times, auditing and\nmeasurements. Yet clocks may differ because of synchronisation errors,\ndrift, pauses or time jumps. Even an accurate clock cannot turn a\nnetwork without guaranteed message delivery times into a position from\nwhich all participants can be reliably observed together.<\/p>\n<p>A common mistake: ‘The change on server A has 10:00:00.100, and the\nchange on B has 10:00:00.090; therefore B was first.’ This assumes that\nthe clocks were synchronised more accurately than the time difference\nand that the timestamps actually capture the events of interest\ncorrectly. If the uncertainty is unclear, the order is not\nestablished.<\/p>\n<p>Systems with tightly controlled clocks and explicit uncertainty\nintervals can build stronger time-related guarantees. This is a\ndeliberately engineered architectural decision, not a property of\nordinary server clocks. For most applications, a logically agreed\nsequence of changes or transactions is easier to justify than a\nsupposedly perfect comparison of timestamps.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Types of faults: Not every failure means the same thing<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Failure model<\/th>\n<th>Assumption<\/th>\n<th>Design implication<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Crash-stop<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>A process fails and does not return during the execution\nconsidered<\/td>\n<td>Other participants must be able to withstand its failure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Crash-recovery<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>A process restarts later<\/td>\n<td>Volatile and durably stored state must be distinguished<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Omission<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Messages are not sent, received or processed<\/td>\n<td>Retries and detection of missing messages may be necessary<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Delay / partition<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Communication takes unusually long or is interrupted between\ngroups<\/td>\n<td>Unreachability must not be equated with certain failure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Byzantine<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>A participant may behave arbitrarily, including inconsistently or\nmaliciously<\/td>\n<td>Requires other protocols and usually stricter trust assumptions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A <strong>network partition<\/strong> means that parts of a network\ncannot reliably reach one another. It does not necessarily mean that the\nentire internet has failed. Even a single interrupted connection between\ndata centres can split a cluster into groups.<\/p>\n<p>A model of accidental crashes does not prove safety against\ncompromised servers. Similarly, replication within the same data centre\ndoes not guarantee independence if the shared power supply fails.\n<strong>Independence of failures is an assumption, not a property of the\nnumber ‘three’.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A timeout is a suspicion, not proof<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a job that is to mark a file authoritatively as\n‘archived’:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Client             Server\n  | --- Job 42 -----&gt; |\n  |                   | processes 42\n  |                   | stores result\n  | &lt;-- Response X ---|  (response is lost)\n  |   Timeout         |\n  | --- Job 42 -----&gt; |  (retry)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>From the client’s perspective, a crashed server looks just like a\nlost response. Anyone who repeats the same job without control after the\ntimeout may initiate a payment twice or create a duplicate record.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>idempotent operation<\/strong> is designed so that repeated\nequivalent requests have no additional unwanted business effect.\nFrequently, a unique <em>idempotency key<\/em> (operation identifier) is\nstored durably together with the result. The business operation and\nregistration of the identifier must be atomic or otherwise protected for\nthe relevant failure scenario. A mere list in RAM is not sufficient\nafter a restart.<\/p>\n<p><strong>‘Effect exactly once’ is not the same as ‘message delivered\nexactly once’.<\/strong> Repeated transmission may be intentional; the\ncorrect effect is what matters. Effects on external systems also require\na coordinated transaction or compensation strategy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Consistency is not a single property<\/h2>\n<p>The term <strong>consistency<\/strong> can refer to different things:\nvalid database rules, identical replication states, a defined ordering\nof concurrently executed operations or the view of a particular client.\nIt therefore makes sense to specify the concrete <strong>consistency\nmodel<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model / guarantee<\/th>\n<th>Intuition<\/th>\n<th>Limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Linearizability<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Each operation takes effect at a point between invocation and\nresponse; completed operations are respected in real-time order<\/td>\n<td>May prevent responses during partitions if no safely established\ncurrent state is available<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sequential consistency<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>There is a common order that respects each participant’s program\norder<\/td>\n<td>This ordering need not represent the real-time order of\nnon-overlapping operations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Causal consistency<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Causally dependent changes appear in the correct dependency\norder<\/td>\n<td>Independent concurrent changes need not be ordered identically\neverywhere<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eventual consistency<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Without new changes and once communication is restored, replicas can\neventually converge<\/td>\n<td>No immediate freshness or fixed time guarantee follows from the term\nalone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>These terms refer to specifically defined objects and operations. A\ndatabase may offer different consistency levels for different read or\nwrite operations. <strong>ACID transactions<\/strong> and\n<strong>linearizability<\/strong> are not synonymous either: ACID\ndescribes, among other things, transactional atomicity and isolation;\nthe particular view across multiple nodes additionally depends on the\nprotocol and its isolation.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"91-eine-anschauliche-chronologie\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An illustrative timeline<\/h3>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Client A:  writes price = 20  ---- Response: success\nClient B:                          reads price ----&gt;  ?<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If the write has been fully acknowledged before B starts its read\ninvocation, linearizability for this object requires B to take that\nwrite or a <em>later<\/em> valid state into account. An earlier value\n<code>15<\/code> would not be permitted here, provided no more recent\nrelevant change exists. By contrast, several legitimate orderings are\npossible for overlapping concurrent operations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Consensus: Many participants agree on a history<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Consensus<\/strong> is distributed agreement on a value or\norder of operations despite specified disruptions. An important special\ncase is the <strong>replicated log<\/strong>: several servers agree on\nthe order of entries and apply them to their local state.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>                Client: ‘set X=7’\n                        |\n                      Leader\n                     /      \\\n                 Replica 2  Replica 3\n                     \\      /\n                    committed log entry\n                          |\n                    State machine: X=7<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In <strong>Raft<\/strong>, these tasks are divided clearly for\nteaching purposes: leader election, log replication and safety rules. A\ncommitted entry is not simply ‘a message that someone received’. The\nprotocol state, log position, majority acknowledgement and election\nrules are essential. A completed commit should not disappear from the\nvalid history when the leader subsequently changes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Distinction:<\/strong> Electing a leader alone does not\nestablish safe consensus. An old leader can wake up after a pause and\nattempt to continue writing. A correct system must prevent obsolete\npermissions taking effect outside the agreed protocol, for example\nthrough term/epoch checks and, for external resources, appropriate\n<em>fencing tokens<\/em>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Majorities: A small, understandable model<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_047_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Raft with five voting servers: the leader and two followers store the new entry from term 7. For older entries, merely counting copies is insufficient.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Raft with five voting servers: the leader\nand two followers store the new entry from term 7. For older entries,\nmerely counting copies is insufficient.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Five voting servers and a new entry from the current\nterm. The majority includes the leader. Raft commits an older entry\nthrough the committed log prefix when an entry from the current term is\ncommitted.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Suppose a cluster has <strong>five voting replicas<\/strong>. A simple\nmajority is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mfrac><mn>5<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\left\\lfloor\\frac52\\right\\rfloor+1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo disjoint groups with three members each are impossible: with five\nparticipants, any two majorities of three must overlap in at least one\nreplica.<\/p>\n<p>This can be demonstrated by counting:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Majority A:   {1,2,3}\nMajority B:   {3,4,5}\nIntersection: {3}<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The overlap is an important ingredient of safety, <strong>but not yet\na complete consensus proof on its own<\/strong>. Participants must also\nfollow correct rules for versions, election periods, log entries and the\nreplacement of a leader.<\/p>\n<p>With simple crash failures, a five-vote cluster can normally continue\nforming a majority of three with <strong>up to two unavailable\nvotes<\/strong>, provided that the remaining participants communicate\nwith one another and further protocol conditions are met. In a\n<code>2 | 3<\/code> partition, only the side with three votes can form a\nmajority; the two-vote side should not commit new binding entries on its\nown. This is intentional protection against <em>split brain<\/em>\n(conflicting active states), not a defect of majority arithmetic.<\/p>\n<p>A smaller system with three votes needs two. Majority quorums\ntherefore change the possible failure scenarios, not automatically the\nmaximum processing speed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The CAP theorem: What cannot be achieved simultaneously\nduring a partition<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>CAP theorem<\/strong> (Consistency, Availability,\nPartition Tolerance) is often misunderstood as a choice of ‘two out of\nthree’. The more precise question is: what happens <strong>during a\nnetwork partition<\/strong> if both sides continue to receive\nrequests?<\/p>\n<p>In the classical formalisation:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>C:<\/strong> a strong consistency model, namely an\natomic/linearizable view of the data under consideration;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>A:<\/strong> every request to a non-failed participant\nreceives a valid response under the model;<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>P:<\/strong> the system model permits loss of communication\nbetween groups of participants, and its promised properties must still\nbe examined.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Under the assumptions of the original proof, a system in a partition\ncannot simultaneously guarantee the specified strong consistency\n<strong>and<\/strong> complete availability of all affected requests. One\nside may not know whether the other has already committed a new value.\nIf it aims to serve every read and write request successfully without\ncommunication, it may produce a conflicting history.<\/p>\n<p>The blanket statement ‘Database X is CP, database Y is AP, therefore\nX is always consistent and Y never is’ is <strong>not valid<\/strong>.\nReal systems have different operations, multiple failure models,\ntemporarily unavailable functions and configurable guarantees.\n<strong>Latency<\/strong> and <strong>durability<\/strong> are additional\nindependent design dimensions; the CAP theorem alone says nothing about\nnormal response times or overall product quality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why not every conflict requires consensus<\/h2>\n<p>Some data need a <strong>unique authoritative order<\/strong>: bank\ntransactions, permission assignments or the binding allocation of a\nresource available only once. Other data can be changed concurrently and\nmerged later: counters for independent events, some collaborative notes\nor analytical metadata.<\/p>\n<p><strong>CRDT<\/strong> stands for <em>Conflict-free Replicated Data\nType<\/em>: a replicated data structure with defined merge rules that\ncan, under suitable conditions, merge independently made changes without\na central decision on each one. A simple grow-only set, for instance,\ncan form the union of the identifiers held on both sides. Deletion,\nlimited resources or time-dependent ordering greatly increase the\nrequirements.<\/p>\n<p>CRDTs do not automatically eliminate network traffic, storage costs,\naccess control or the need for unique decisions in all business\noperations. Their strength lies in making <strong>the conflict\nmathematically manageable through the data structure<\/strong>, rather\nthan requiring every change to be approved by a global coordinator.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>FLP: A formal limit on agreement<\/h2>\n<p>The result of <strong>Fischer, Lynch and Paterson (FLP)<\/strong>\nconsiders deterministic consensus protocols in a fully asynchronous\nsystem, where messages may be delayed arbitrarily long and even a single\nprocess may fail. Under these assumptions, there is no guarantee that\nevery admissible execution <strong>terminates<\/strong> while achieving\nthe required safe consensus decision.<\/p>\n<p>This does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that consensus is impossible in\nreal systems. Practical protocols work under additional assumptions,\nsuch as periods of sufficiently reliable communication, suitable\ntimeouts or randomisation. They can preserve safety even during adverse\nphases, while the guarantee of progress depends on more favourable\ncommunication conditions.<\/p>\n<p>The connection with M7-L2 is apparent here: <strong>safety proofs and\nliveness proofs may have different assumptions.<\/strong> A correct\nprotocol may temporarily be unable to reach a decision. One must not\ninfer either ‘defective’ or ‘guaranteed always available’ from this.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Replicated state machine: Same command, same state?<\/h2>\n<p>A widespread architectural principle is the <strong>replicated state\nmachine (RSM)<\/strong>. The replicas start with the same state and apply\nthe same specified sequence of deterministic commands.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Shared log: [ +5, -2, +7 ]\nReplica A: 10 -&gt; 15 -&gt; 13 -&gt; 20\nReplica B: 10 -&gt; 15 -&gt; 13 -&gt; 20\nReplica C: 10 -&gt; 15 -&gt; 13 -&gt; 20<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This equality requires, among other things, that commands be\ninterpreted <strong>deterministically<\/strong>. A state machine that\nspontaneously queries the local time or an uncoordinated random number\non every execution can diverge. Where necessary, such inputs must be\nrecorded as shared command data.<\/p>\n<p>In a real cluster, replicas may temporarily know different prefixes\nof the log. What matters is which entries are binding and when a replica\nmay apply them or use them to answer reads.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Risk:<\/strong> Replicated logs are no substitute for backups.\nOtherwise, an accidentally deleted record or a logically incorrect\ncommand is propagated very reliably to all replicas. Availability and\nindependent recoverability are different goals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Clearly distinguishing three meanings of ‘synchronous’<\/h2>\n<p>In everyday usage, ‘synchronous’ refers to different things:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Synchronous call:<\/strong> A client waits for a response\nrather than merely initiating the job.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Synchronous replication:<\/strong> A write waits for\nspecified acknowledgements from other replicas before it is\nacknowledged.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Synchronous system model:<\/strong> There are specified upper\ntime bounds for computation and message delivery.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>These meanings are <strong>not interchangeable<\/strong>. An API can\nrespond synchronously even though data are replicated only\nasynchronously. A Raft cluster can use majority acknowledgements without\nthe network guaranteeing a hard upper bound on message delivery time. A\nsystem can be extremely fast in quiet periods but still depend on\nadditional timing assumptions for its provable liveness guarantee.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Latency, throughput and the cost of coordination<\/h2>\n<p>A distributed architecture adds communication paths and coordination.\nThis can be illustrated with <strong>constructed numbers<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<p>A client makes three independent remote calls, waiting for each in\nturn. Each requires 25 milliseconds of network and processing time in\nthis teaching model. Performed purely sequentially, these three steps\ntake\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\times25\\,\\mathrm{ms}=75\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf they can actually occur in parallel without hidden dependencies, the\ntheoretical lower bound in the model would be approximately\n<code>max(25,25,25) = 25 ms<\/code> plus coordination costs.\n<strong>These are not measurements.<\/strong> Reality includes queues,\nvariation in network timing, outliers and different response times.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Throughput<\/strong> means completed work per unit of time;\n<strong>latency<\/strong> is the duration of an individual request.\nGreater parallelism does not increase throughput without limit: shared\nresources and repeated failed attempts can become overloaded. Strong\nconsistency requirements add round trips and durable writes; weaker\nguarantees may incur subsequent conflict-handling and data-correction\ncosts.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"171-mittelwert-gegen-randfaelle\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Averages versus tail cases<\/h3>\n<p>A service may respond in 20 ms on average, while individual requests\nmay take much longer. In an operation that requires many services\nsimultaneously, even one slow component can limit the overall process.\nThis is why, in addition to averages, percentiles such as\n<strong>p95<\/strong> or <strong>p99<\/strong>, error rates and queue\nlengths are often examined.<\/p>\n<p>The joint distribution is crucial: individual p99 times must not\nsimply be added together and reported as the p99 of the overall system.\nWithout information about dependency, load and distribution, no reliable\noverall probability can be calculated from them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Retries, backoff and queues<\/h2>\n<p>For short-lived transmission failures, a retry can help. Many\nsimultaneous retries, however, can worsen the cause of a disruption.\n<strong>Backoff<\/strong> increases the interval between attempts;\n<strong>jitter<\/strong> spreads them over time so that not all clients\nreturn simultaneously. The Amazon Builders’ Library documents this\ndanger, including cascading retries across multiple service layers.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>queue<\/strong> decouples acceptance and processing, but\nwithout further rules guarantees neither ordering across multiple\nconsumers nor an exactly-once effect. In a typical\n<em>at-least-once<\/em> delivery model, a message may be delivered\nrepeatedly. Business identifiers, atomic state transitions, bounded\nretries and handling of jobs that fail permanently are therefore part of\nthe design.<\/p>\n<p>For financial or safety-critical operations, ‘we will simply try it\nagain’ is not sufficient. The client must be able to determine whether\nthe original job has already been authoritatively completed – through an\noperation identifier and a status query if necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Formal model checking: Possible executions instead of\nhope<\/h2>\n<p>A distributed system can be formulated as a <strong>state transition\nsystem<\/strong>:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Initial state\n  |       \\\n  |        \\ message delayed\n  v         v\nState A    State B\n  |          |\n  +--&gt; State C &lt;--+\n        |\n        v\n      State D<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A model checker such as <strong>TLC for TLA+<\/strong> can examine\npossible interleavings in a <em>finite, specifically bounded model<\/em>:\nwhat happens if the leader crashes after a commit? What if a message\narrives late? What if two clients claim the same resource?<\/p>\n<p>Example of a model invariant:\n<code>never two different committed values for the same log position<\/code>.\nA discovered counterexample supplies an execution that, according to the\nmodel assumptions, leads to a violation. This is more valuable than\nhoping that such a special case will occur by chance during test\noperation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limits of the result:<\/strong> A successfully checked finite\nmodel instance does not automatically prove every arbitrarily large\nconfiguration. A model can contain incorrect assumptions or omit real\nimplementation details. Implementation tests, fault injection,\nmeasurements, security reviews and – for more general statements –\nappropriate mathematical proofs are additionally necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical example 1: Media archive across two sites<\/h2>\n<p>An application manages hundreds of thousands of media metadata\nrecords. Originally, a local SQL database is sufficient. Readers from\ntwo cities should now be able to search titles and tags quickly, while a\ncentral editorial team edits the data.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Editorial team -&gt; primary database -&gt; replication -&gt; read replica at site B\n                       |\n                       +------&gt; backups in separate protection domain<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Question about the model:<\/strong> Must new tags be visible\neverywhere immediately, or may search results at site B lag behind by a\nfew seconds? If a slight delay is acceptable, asynchronous read\nreplication may make sense. A title that becomes visible a second later\nis usually less critical than an exclusive permission that is granted\ntwice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Failure model:<\/strong> If site B is only a read replica, it\nmust not accept unchecked authoritative changes when disconnected.\nOtherwise, conflicts may arise during merging. If write availability at\nboth sites is genuinely needed, a clear conflict-resolution or consensus\nmechanism is required.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Economic consequence:<\/strong> Two sites entail data\ntransfer, additional monitoring, maintenance, security work and recovery\ntests. The mere existence of a replica does not automatically make the\nsystem safer than a properly secured single server. For small archives,\na monolith with backups often remains the better architecture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical example 2: A job must be billed only once<\/h2>\n<p>A user starts a paid image analysis. An API server accepts the job, a\nworker processes the image, and the billing system debits the balance.\nThe connection can fail along the way.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Client -- POST /analyse (operation ID 4711) --&gt; API\n                  |\n                  v\n                Job record\n                  |\n                Job queue\n                  |\n                 Worker ------&gt; Analysis result\n                  |\n                  v\n              Billing action<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A robust business rule is: <strong>at most one binding debit of the\nbalance for the same precisely defined operation ID and the same\nbusiness job.<\/strong> One possible design combines a unique key in a\ntransactional database with a unique billing record. When the job is\ndelivered again, the system recognises the already completed effect. If\nthe transaction is carried out in a third-party service, the local\ndatabase transaction alone is insufficient; the external interface must\nprovide suitable idempotency or a reconciliation process.<\/p>\n<p>The safety requirement ‘do not debit twice’ and the liveness\nrequirement ‘eventually deliver a result when the system is healthy’\nmust be checked separately. Neither three workers nor a load balancer\nmeets these rules by itself.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S22\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical example 3: Control database for several\nworkers<\/h2>\n<p>A work platform uses five control replicas. They determine which\nworker is permitted to take on which task. A network fault separates two\nreplicas from the other three.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>           West site               East site\n       [R1] [R2]          X       [R3] [R4] [R5]\n         two votes                  three votes\n         no majority              majority reachable<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If the control logic uses an appropriate majority-consensus protocol,\nthe group of two must not decide on a new binding leader entry. The\ngroup of three can continue if the other requirements are met. Existing\nworkers must still check whether their previous permission remains\nvalid; otherwise, they can make external changes despite a correct\ncontrol log.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Decision:<\/strong> For a critical scheduler, temporarily\nmaking certain control operations unavailable can protect the correct\nstate. For purely statistical telemetry, the same global majority\ncoordination may be excessive. Different consistency models can\ntherefore be chosen for different kinds of data even within <em>one<\/em>\nproduct.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S23\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What can be proved, and what can only be measured?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Claim<\/th>\n<th>Appropriate evidence<\/th>\n<th>What this does <strong>not<\/strong> prove<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Never two valid conflicting leader decisions’<\/td>\n<td>Specification, protocol invariant, formal proof/model checking<\/td>\n<td>Absence of errors in a different implementation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘After at most two crash failures, the majority can continue\nworking’<\/td>\n<td>Failure model and quorum analysis, additional liveness\nassumptions<\/td>\n<td>Continuation under arbitrary network partitions and without timing\nassumptions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘p99 below 100 ms under a defined load’<\/td>\n<td>Controlled measurement with traceable load and measurement\nmethod<\/td>\n<td>A mathematical guarantee for all future load peaks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Repeated request debits the balance only once’<\/td>\n<td>Atomicity/uniqueness rules and targeted fault tests<\/td>\n<td>Exactly-once network message delivery or a correct external payment\nservice<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘Replicas hold matching data’<\/td>\n<td>Explicit consistency definition and protocol proof<\/td>\n<td>Independent protection against user error and malicious changes<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>M7-L1 helps formulate hypotheses and measurement conditions. M7-L2\nprovides the foundations of correctness, invariants and undecidability.\nM7-L3 combines both: <strong>a distributed architecture has only been\nproperly assessed when it is clear what is guaranteed, what is probable\nunder assumptions and what has merely been observed.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S24\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision guide: When is distribution worthwhile?<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation<\/th>\n<th>Often a useful starting point<\/th>\n<th>Warning sign of additional complexity<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Private media catalogue, one user<\/td>\n<td>Local file or SQLite with verified backup<\/td>\n<td>Replication cluster without multi-user or failure tolerance\nrequirements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small web application<\/td>\n<td>One well-operated server plus backup, monitoring and recovery\nplan<\/td>\n<td>Untested division into numerous services<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large read-intensive platform<\/td>\n<td>Caching, indexes, possibly read replicas<\/td>\n<td>Unclear freshness requirements for copies<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critical shared control<\/td>\n<td>Authoritative source of state, possibly consensus with a clear\nmajority<\/td>\n<td>Two independent ‘leaders’ both allowed to write<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independently mergeable events<\/td>\n<td>Event log or suitable CRDT structure<\/td>\n<td>Global lock for every non-critical change<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Billing and access permissions<\/td>\n<td>Unique business keys, transactions, reconciliation<\/td>\n<td>‘Exactly once’ as an unproven marketing promise<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The best distributed system is not the one with the most data\ncentres, but the <strong>smallest system that verifiably meets the\nrequired guarantees under realistic failure conditions<\/strong>.\nMaintainability, data protection, costs and the human ability to\ndiagnose disruptions also matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S25\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conclusion and transition to M7-L4<\/h2>\n<p>Distributed systems are demanding because no participant immediately\nknows the complete global state. Causal event ordering replaces naive\nsorting by clock time. Consistency models reveal which observations\nusers may expect. Consensus orders binding decisions under defined\nfailures. CAP and FLP mark limits – not as blanket prohibitions, but as\nstatements under precise assumptions. Measurements complement formal\nmodels but do not replace them.<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4 – Data, security and\narchitectural decisions<\/a> builds on this. It examines how competing\nquality objectives – correctness, availability, data protection,\nsecurity, costs, latency and maintainability – can be explicitly weighed\nagainst one another and documented.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S26\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Place within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This chapter belongs to the <strong>six-part Master’s core<\/strong>,\nnot the optional additional Master’s electives. Navigation: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">previous Master’s core lesson\nM7-L2<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">next Master’s core\nlesson M7-L4<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">reading paths and\nsubject cross-references<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S27\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source review:<\/strong> 05.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>The following original publications and technical texts were reviewed\non <strong>05.11.2026<\/strong> for the terminology and limits of the\nmodels. They support the academic definitions; the case studies and\nfigures in M7-L3 are constructed for teaching purposes and are not\npresented as findings of these sources.<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Leslie Lamport (1978), ‘Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of\nEvents in a Distributed System’:<\/strong> Causal ordering and logical\nclocks. <a\nhref=\"https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/time-clocks.pdf\">https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/time-clocks.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Maurice Herlihy / Jeannette Wing (1990), ‘Linearizability: A\nCorrectness Condition for Concurrent Objects’:<\/strong> Formal meaning\nof linearizability. <a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~wing/publications/HerlihyWing90.pdf\">https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~wing/publications/HerlihyWing90.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Seth Gilbert / Nancy Lynch (2002), ‘Brewer’s Conjecture and\nthe Feasibility of Consistent, Available, Partition-Tolerant Web\nServices’:<\/strong> Precise CAP failure model. <a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr22/cos418/papers/cap.pdf\">https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr22/cos418/papers/cap.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Michael Fischer / Nancy Lynch / Michael Paterson (1985),\n‘Impossibility of Distributed Consensus with One Faulty\nProcess’:<\/strong> Asynchronous consensus and liveness limits. <a\nhref=\"https://groups.csail.mit.edu/tds/papers/Lynch/jacm85.pdf\">https://groups.csail.mit.edu/tds/papers/Lynch/jacm85.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Diego Ongaro / John Ousterhout (2014), ‘In Search of an\nUnderstandable Consensus Algorithm’:<\/strong> Raft, leader election, log\nreplication and safety arguments. <a\nhref=\"https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf\">https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Amazon Builders’ Library, ‘Timeouts, retries and backoff\nwith jitter’:<\/strong> Partial failures, amplification of load and\noperational idempotency. <a\nhref=\"https://aws.amazon.com/de/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/\">https://aws.amazon.com/de/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Amazon Builders’ Library, ‘Leader election in distributed\nsystems’:<\/strong> Lease limitations, stale leaders and modelling. <a\nhref=\"https://aws.amazon.com/de/builders-library/leader-election-in-distributed-systems/\">https://aws.amazon.com/de/builders-library/leader-election-in-distributed-systems/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Shapiro, Preguiça, Baquero, Zawirski (2011), ‘A\ncomprehensive study of Convergent and Commutative Replicated Data\nTypes’:<\/strong> Principles and limits of applicability of CRDTs. <a\nhref=\"https://dsf.berkeley.edu/cs286/papers/crdt-tr2011.pdf\">https://dsf.berkeley.edu/cs286/papers/crdt-tr2011.pdf<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">9.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>TLA+ Wiki, ‘TLC Model Checker’:<\/strong> State-space\nchecking of finite models and its scope. <a\nhref=\"https://docs.tlapl.us/using%3Atlc%3Astart\">https://docs.tlapl.us/using%3Atlc%3Astart<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Course cross-references:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2 – Replication<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5 – Distributed transactions<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1 – Distributed\nprocessing<\/a>.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M7-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M7-L4 – Data, security and architectural decisions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Master’s-oriented computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master’s orientation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand architectural\ndecisions as justified, verifiable trade-offs: which data must be\npreserved, and with what level of quality? What damage is plausible?\nWhich protective measures are appropriate? How are correctness,\nmaintainability, performance, availability, data protection and costs\nrelated?<\/p>\n<p>The examples, scales and monetary amounts in this lesson are\n<strong>explicitly constructed teaching scenarios<\/strong>. They\nrepresent neither actual product prices nor measured probabilities of\nsecurity incidents or failures. The references at the end identify the\nsubject-matter foundations; an architectural proposal does not therefore\nconstitute a security certification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An architectural question is not a product question<\/h2>\n<p>When developing a system, people often begin by listing products: a\nparticular database, a cloud provider, containers, an API gateway or a\nmessage broker. These are potential <strong>answers<\/strong>, however,\nnot precise descriptions of the problem. A sound architectural decision\nbegins with questions such as: who needs the application? What must\nnever happen? How much delay is acceptable? Which data would be\nirretrievable if lost? Who could be harmed by unauthorised disclosure?\nWho will maintain the system in three years?<\/p>\n<p>An architecture can therefore be described as a set of interrelated\ndecisions. The data structure affects queries and changes. Data\ndistribution alters consistency and failure behaviour. The chosen access\nboundaries determine possible security incidents. Operational and\nrecovery requirements generate long-term costs.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Domain objective\n     |\n     v\nRequirements + constraints + risks\n     |\n     v\nPossible architectural alternatives\n     |\n     v\nTrade-off -&gt; decision -&gt; observable operation\n                 ^                |\n                 +-- new evidence-+<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Principle:<\/strong> An architecture is not good because it is\nparticularly modern or elaborate, but because it meets its\n<strong>stated requirements at acceptable risk and downstream\ncost<\/strong>. A well-modularised monolith may be more appropriate than\nten services. A local file may be more suitable than a database cluster.\nConversely, a small system handling sensitive personal data may require\nstrict security controls even though its load is low.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing functional and non-functional\nrequirements<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>functional requirement<\/strong> describes a capability:\nusers can upload an image; the application displays its title; a payment\nupdates a balance. A <strong>quality requirement<\/strong> describes how\nreliably, quickly, traceably or securely that capability must be\ndelivered. These are often called non-functional requirements; in\npractice, they determine a substantial part of the system design.<\/p>\n<p>Examples of quality attributes are <strong>correctness<\/strong>,\n<strong>reliability<\/strong>, <strong>security<\/strong>,\n<strong>performance<\/strong>, <strong>maintainability<\/strong>,\n<strong>usability<\/strong>, <strong>portability<\/strong> and\n<strong>recoverability<\/strong>. Many of these goals are in tension. An\nadditional security check may increase effort and response time. More\nreplicas may absorb failures but increase costs, failure scenarios and\nattack surfaces. Extensive normalisation may prevent inconsistencies but\nmake certain queries more complex.<\/p>\n<p>The statement “should be fast” is insufficient for a verifiable\ndesign. A better example is: “Under an explicitly described load\nprofile, 95 per cent of successful read requests should be answered\nwithin 300 milliseconds.” The <strong>95th percentile (p95)<\/strong> is\na statistical threshold; it does <strong>not<\/strong> guarantee that\nevery request remains below that time. The measurement method, period\nunder consideration and definition of response time must be\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p>Likewise, “reliable” must be made concrete: is the concern the\nfrequency of failures, the maximum tolerable interruption or the number\nof data changes that may be lost in the event of a total site failure?\nThese are different requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Constraints, assumptions and invariants<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>constraint<\/strong> limits the permissible solutions: for\nexample, legally required data processing, an existing budget, a lack of\noperations staff, offline use or a mandatory technical interface. An\n<strong>assumption<\/strong>, by contrast, is an assessment that has not\nyet been established and may prove wrong: “The application will never\nhave more than one hundred simultaneous users.” Assumptions must remain\nvisible so that they can be checked later.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>invariant<\/strong> is a rule intended to remain true\nacross all permissible state changes. For example:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A paid order must not simultaneously have the status “never\npaid”.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A user must not be able to read another user’s private file without\ncorresponding authorisation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A confirmed amount must not be debited a second time through a\nrepeated identical processing step.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These rules affect the data model, transactions, access control,\ninterfaces and failure tests. M7-L2 introduced invariants as part of\ncorrectness reasoning. M7-L4 uses them as <strong>design\nconstraints<\/strong>: an architectural alternative unable to preserve a\nrequired invariant is not chosen merely because it costs less.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Understanding data first according to its domain\nmeaning<\/h2>\n<p>Before choosing a storage system, a <strong>domain data\nmodel<\/strong> is worthwhile: which objects exist? What relationships\nhold? Who is responsible for correctness? Which information can be\nchanged or deleted? What must be logged immutably?<\/p>\n<p>For a media catalogue, examples are <code>Medium<\/code>,\n<code>Quelle<\/code>, <code>Schlagwort<\/code> and <code>Zuordnung<\/code>\n(media item, source, keyword and association). A media item may have\nseveral keywords. A source may be associated with several media items. A\nseparate model prevents the source and keyword from being incorrectly\ntreated as fixed properties of a single large table row.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Source 1 ---- n Media item n ---- n Keyword\n                    |\n                    +--- Metadata\n                    +--- File reference\n                    +--- Rights / visibility<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The <strong>file reference<\/strong> is not the file itself. A\ndatabase entry may point to an object in the file system or object\nstorage. This creates new questions about consistency: what happens if a\ndatabase entry is deleted but the media file remains? What happens if\nthe file upload succeeds but the database entry fails? A sound domain\ndesign explicitly addresses such states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data classes and life cycles instead of treating everything\nalike<\/h2>\n<p>Data differs in its confidentiality, integrity requirements, lifespan\nand recoverability:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class in the teaching example<\/th>\n<th>Typical protection requirement<\/th>\n<th>Practical design consequence<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Public metadata<\/td>\n<td>Correctness, availability<\/td>\n<td>Indexing, simple recovery<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private account data<\/td>\n<td>Confidentiality, access control<\/td>\n<td>Authorisation, limited data disclosure<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Billing events<\/td>\n<td>High integrity, traceability<\/td>\n<td>Transaction rules, auditable processes according to applicable\nobligations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short-lived cache<\/td>\n<td>Lower durability requirement<\/td>\n<td>Reconstruction instead of elaborate backups<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access keys<\/td>\n<td>Very high confidentiality<\/td>\n<td>Specialised secrets management, rotation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A <strong>data life cycle<\/strong> describes collection, processing,\ndisclosure, retention, archiving and deletion. It prevents the common\npattern: “We store everything forever; perhaps we will need it again.”\nAppropriate retention depends on the purpose, legal requirements and\nlegitimate operational interests. The course is not a substitute for\nlegal assessment of a specific project.<\/p>\n<p>The EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) includes, among\nothers, purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation and\nappropriate security as central principles (Article 5); Article 25\naddresses data protection by design and by default. [Q7] The precise\nlegal permissibility depends on the processing context.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which database suits which situation?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>relational database<\/strong> with explicit tables, keys and\ntransactions is appropriate when relationships and consistency rules\nmatter. A <strong>document store<\/strong> may be useful for variable\ndocuments that are mostly processed independently. A <strong>key-value\nstore<\/strong> is often suitable for simple accesses by key, such as\ncertain cache applications. <strong>Object storage<\/strong> is\nparticularly interesting for files or immutable binary objects. These\nare usage patterns, not mandatory product boundaries.<\/p>\n<p>An application may combine different storage classes. Each additional\nstorage type brings its own operational requirements, authorisation\nrules, backups, failure cases and possible data inconsistencies,\nhowever. The statement “We use the technically optimal database for each\ntask” is therefore only a good decision if the extra benefit justifies\nthe system complexity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Decision question:<\/strong> Must a change to several related\ndomain records occur as an indivisible unit? If so, the available\ntransaction boundaries are particularly important. If information can be\nreconstructed from a reliable source in an emergency, its durability can\nbe provided with less effort.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transactions are a domain decision, not just a technical\ndecision<\/h2>\n<p>A transaction is intended to perform or discard a defined set of\nchanges together. Example: a paid order and its corresponding stock\nchange must be consistent in domain terms. Whether they can lie within\n<strong>one<\/strong> database transaction depends on the system\nboundary. If they are distributed across different services,\ncoordination and failure problems arise.<\/p>\n<p><strong>ACID<\/strong> describes atomicity, consistency, isolation and\ndurability within the chosen database system and its configuration.\nBeing “ACID-compliant” is not a complete guarantee that domain rules are\nmodelled correctly, however. Even a technically correct transaction can\ncalculate an impermissible price if the application logic is wrong.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Check order\n    |\n    +--&gt; Create booking record\n    +--&gt; Change stock / entitlement\n    |\n  COMMIT -- only if rules are satisfied\n    |\nConfirmation to the user<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If an external payment service is involved, however, a local database\ntransaction is not enough to make all possible communication failures\n“magically” atomic. Domain states, reliable event matching, retry rules\nand reconciliation with the external system are needed. The desired\ninvariant must be enforced at the correct system boundary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distribution solves load problems and creates coordination\nproblems<\/h2>\n<p>M7-L3 showed that network partitions, delays and incomplete knowledge\nare inherent in distributed systems. A crucial principle follows for\nM7-L4: <strong>an additional server instance is not a freely available\ncopy of a single machine.<\/strong> It changes failure and security\nmodels, synchronisation and operation.<\/p>\n<p>For read-only, rarely modified media metadata, delayed replicas are\noften acceptable. For a binding reservation of the same unique resource\nby two users, a strongly coordinated decision may be needed. It is not\nsensible to treat every piece of information according to the same\nconsistency model.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Data operation<\/th>\n<th>Typical desired property<\/th>\n<th>Potential trade-off<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Public search results<\/td>\n<td>High read speed<\/td>\n<td>Temporarily older results<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Account balance after payment<\/td>\n<td>Unambiguous domain booking<\/td>\n<td>Availability during network partitions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Profile image preview<\/td>\n<td>Rapid display<\/td>\n<td>Delayed cache updates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single-use voucher redemption<\/td>\n<td>No double redemption<\/td>\n<td>Additional coordination and database load<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>No automatic technical prescription follows from this. An\narchitectural decision must define <strong>which<\/strong> deviation is\nacceptable for <strong>which<\/strong> operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security goals: confidentiality, integrity and\navailability<\/h2>\n<p>Three classical protection goals are:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Confidentiality:<\/strong> Unauthorised parties must not\nlearn information.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Integrity:<\/strong> Information and system states must not\nbe changed without authorisation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Availability:<\/strong> Authorised use must remain possible\nto the required extent.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>An individual design may give these goals different priorities. A\npublic image collection primarily needs reliable access and correct\ncontent. A private document collection requires considerably stronger\nconfidentiality. A booking system may suffer greater damage from an\nincorrect duplicate booking than from a few minutes of\nunavailability.<\/p>\n<p>Security therefore cannot meaningfully be described as a general\nscore of “8 out of 10”. First the <strong>assets<\/strong> at risk and\nspecific undesirable events must be identified. This explicitly includes\nkeys, access rights, metadata, backups, audit trails and operations\nadministration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Identifying trust boundaries<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>trust boundary<\/strong> exists where a new security\nassumption is intended to apply: from the browser to the server, between\nan external service and one’s own application, or from the application\nprocess to the database. A connection over the internal network is not\nautomatically trustworthy; the location of a device alone does not\nestablish authorisation.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>[Browser / untrusted input]\n                 |\n          TLS + API boundary\n                 v\n[Application / authenticated identity]\n                 |\n          Authorisation decision\n                 v\n[Data access with limited rights]\n                 |\n                 v\n[Private records / separate backups]<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Each boundary requires its own questions: how is identity\nestablished? Is the transmitted data structure valid? Is this person\npermitted to use the requested resource? Does an error response expose\ntoo much information externally? Can an input be treated as data rather\nthan inadvertently as a command? <strong>Encrypted transport<\/strong>\ndoes not protect against an incorrect authorisation decision.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_048_EN.png\"\nalt=\"Trust boundaries between browser, web application and database: TLS, authentication and authorisation at the API entry point, and service identities and least privilege at the database boundary.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Trust boundaries between browser, web\napplication and database: TLS, authentication and authorisation at the\nAPI entry point, and service identities and least privilege at the\ndatabase boundary.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schematic <strong>trust boundaries<\/strong> of a web\napplication. At the client boundary, identity, inputs and permissions\nare checked on the server; between the application service and database,\nseparate service identities, least privilege and appropriate transport\nprotection additionally apply. Internal network connections are not\nautomatically trustworthy either.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Threat modelling: from abstract fears to concrete\nscenarios<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>threat model<\/strong> describes assets requiring\nprotection, potential attackers or sources of error, entry paths and\ncountermeasures. The OWASP guide to threat modelling recommends\n<strong>STRIDE<\/strong>, among other approaches, as a structuring aid\nfor technical designs. [Q2]<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>STRIDE category<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<th>Example<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Spoofing<\/td>\n<td>Impersonating an identity<\/td>\n<td>Using someone else’s session token<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tampering<\/td>\n<td>Modifying data without authorisation<\/td>\n<td>Manipulating a file in transit<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repudiation<\/td>\n<td>Denying an action<\/td>\n<td>Critical change without a traceable audit trail<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Information Disclosure<\/td>\n<td>Disclosing information<\/td>\n<td>Retrieving a private file using a guessed identifier<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Denial of Service<\/td>\n<td>Blocking a service<\/td>\n<td>Expensive requests overload the server<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elevation of Privilege<\/td>\n<td>Extending permissions<\/td>\n<td>An ordinary role uses an administrator function<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>STRIDE is not a complete list of all real attacks and does not\nreplace domain-specific prioritisation. Additional perspectives are\nnecessary for data protection risks: processing that is legally\nauthorised may still be inappropriate, excessive or incomprehensible to\nthe people affected. Accidental operating errors and technical faults\nmust also be considered, even when no malicious attacker is\ninvolved.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Risk: probability, impact and uncertainty<\/h2>\n<p>A risk arises from a <strong>scenario<\/strong> and its possible\n<strong>impact<\/strong>, together with an assessment of whether and\nunder what conditions the scenario can occur. NIST SP 800-30 provides a\nmethodological framework for risk assessments. [Q1]<\/p>\n<p>A useful teaching approximation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Expected loss<\/mtext><mo>=<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">assumed event probability<\/mtext><mo>×<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">assumed magnitude of loss<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Expected loss}=\\text{assumed event probability}\\times\\text{assumed magnitude of loss}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose an explicitly fictional service values a particular data loss\nat <strong>€12,000 in damages<\/strong>, with an estimated probability of\n<strong>2%<\/strong> for the period under consideration. This gives a\n<strong>calculated expected value of €240<\/strong>. A second\ncountermeasure that could lower the loss from such an incident to €3,000\nwould give <strong>€60<\/strong> at an unchanged probability. The\napparent difference of <strong>€180<\/strong> is only as sound as the\nunderlying assumptions.<\/p>\n<p>The example explains the calculation principle, <strong>not real\nactuarial mathematics<\/strong>. Rare severe losses, uncertain\nprobabilities, legal obligations and affected people cannot responsibly\nbe reduced solely to a mean. A measure may be necessary even when its\nexpected monetary value is uncertain. Risk decisions therefore require\nqualitative justification, sensitivity analyses and clear limits on\nacceptable consequences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Authentication, authorisation and identities<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Authentication<\/strong> answers: who or what is signing in?\n<strong>Authorisation<\/strong> answers: may this identity perform the\nconcrete action on the concrete resource? A valid login is not\npermission to retrieve every file. <strong>Accounting or\nauditing<\/strong> records important events traceably, where appropriate\nand permitted.<\/p>\n<p>A robust model checks permissions on the server against the actual\nresource. Merely checking the user interface (“the button is hidden”)\ndoes not constitute access control, because requests may reach the\ninterface in other ways. In the OWASP Top 10:2025, <strong>Broken Access\nControl<\/strong> remains a central risk category; other categories\nconcern, among others, configuration, software supply chains,\ncryptography, injection and authentication. [Q3]<\/p>\n<p>The principle of <strong>least privilege<\/strong> means that a\nservice receives only the permissions it needs for its task, preferably\nonly for the required period. <strong>Zero trust<\/strong> does not\nassume trust merely because of an internal network location. NIST SP\n800-207 describes this approach for identities, devices and resources.\n[Q4] Zero trust does not mean that every request must necessarily be\nsent through a new external login service; the specific control points\ndepend on the architecture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Authorisation can be part of the data model<\/h2>\n<p>For a platform with several independent customers\n(<strong>tenants<\/strong>), it is not sufficient to add a\n<code>tenant_id<\/code> column to every record. The decisive point is\nthat <strong>every<\/strong> read and write operation enforces the\ncorrect tenant boundary – including searches, exports, background jobs\nand administrative functions.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Request: user U wants to read document D\n    |\n    v\nVerify user identity\n    |\n    v\nCheck tenant + permission + resource D\n    |\n    +-- permitted ----&gt; Read record\n    |\n    +-- not permitted -&gt; Deny<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A relational database system can restrict application access further.\nPostgreSQL, for example, supports <strong>Row-Level Security\n(RLS)<\/strong>, that is, policies for individual table rows. Such\npolicies must be explicitly enabled and correctly configured; owners and\nprivileged roles may have exceptions under certain conditions. RLS is\ntherefore <strong>an additional protective layer<\/strong>, not an\nautomatic replacement for a correct model of roles and permissions.\n[Q8]<\/p>\n<p>Regardless of storage, search indexes, caches and export files should\nalso respect tenant boundaries. A correctly protected SQL table is of\nlittle help if a shared cache delivers an already decrypted response to\nthe wrong customer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multiple protective layers and the limits of\nencryption<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Defence in depth<\/strong> means avoiding dependency on a\nsingle barrier. For a web system, several layers may work together:\nminimised public interfaces, TLS for transport connections, secure\nsession management, server-side authorisation, restricted database\nroles, suitable encryption of data at rest, protected backups and\ninformative logs.<\/p>\n<p>Cryptography is necessary but not a substitute for correct design.\n<strong>TLS<\/strong> protects data in transit between the corresponding\ncommunication endpoints. <strong>Encryption of data at rest<\/strong>,\ndepending on the threat model, protects against particular forms of\naccess to storage media and backups. If a compromised application\nprocess is legitimately permitted to decrypt data, it may still misuse\nthat data. <strong>Passwords<\/strong> in ordinary server authentication\nare not simply stored using reversible encryption, but verified with\nappropriate hash functions specially designed for passwords, together\nwith individual salts.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Key management<\/strong> is itself an architectural task: who\nmay issue keys? How are they rotated? What happens if they are lost? How\ncan a compromised permission be revoked? An application that promises\nencryption but stores its keys openly in a source code repository has\nmissed its central protection goal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data protection by design and deletion<\/h2>\n<p>Data protection should begin with the domain model. Is a precise home\naddress required, or is the city sufficient? Must a date of birth be\nstored permanently if only a one-time age check is intended? Can a\nstatistic be calculated without personal data? Are logs unnecessarily\nenriched with credentials, search terms or sensitive document\ncontent?<\/p>\n<p>Deletion is a particularly difficult design question: the primary\ndatabase may immediately remove a record, but what happens to search\nindexes, replicas, cache entries, exported copies and backups? A system\nshould have traceable periods and technical procedures for these\ndatasets. An existing backup does not make a deletion concept\nunnecessary; it needs its own recovery and retention procedure.<\/p>\n<p>The GDPR is not a blanket prohibition on backups or logs, but\nrequires legally sound, purpose-limited and proportionate processing.\nThe specific design may depend on legal obligations and the\ncircumstances. [Q7]<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data durability, recovery and the difference between RPO\nand RTO<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>backup<\/strong> is a copy from which data can be restored\nto an appropriate state. <strong>Replication<\/strong> keeps copies\nsynchronous or delayed for operation or failure tolerance. An accidental\ndeletion may also be replicated; replication is not a substitute for an\nindependent backup. Conversely, the existence of a backup file does not\nyet guarantee successful recovery.<\/p>\n<p>Two metrics structure the planning:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):<\/strong> What data state\nmust at least be recoverable after a failure? An RPO of one hour means a\ntarget maximum permitted window of lost changes of one hour – not a\nstatement about the duration of recovery.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):<\/strong> Within what time\nafter the event should a failed function become usable again? This is a\ntarget, not a commitment established without testing.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Example: a small catalogue permits <strong>RPO = 24 hours<\/strong>\nand <strong>RTO = 12 hours<\/strong>. Daily checked backups and\ndocumented recovery may be appropriate for this. A binding payment\nfunction with a much tighter RPO may require continuous logging,\nadditional data durability and a different failure model. The numerical\nvalues in the example are purely hypothetical. Costs, operation and\nrestore tests determine whether the target is realistically\nachievable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Particularly important:<\/strong> recovery does not simply\nmean “extract a file”, but may also mean restoring keys, configuration,\npermissions, database versions, external identities and the order of\ndependent services to a functioning, consistent state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Modular monolith, services and operability<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>modular monolithic application<\/strong> combines different\ndomain modules in one jointly deployed unit. This can simplify local\ntransactions and troubleshooting. <strong>Microservices<\/strong>\nseparate domain services together with their interfaces, often with\nindependent ownership of data. They may facilitate independent changes\nand scaling, but increase network communication, logging, error\nhandling, deployment coordination and the need for agreement.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Option M – modular monolith\n  One service: [Users | Media | Rights | Billing]\n                            |\n                         Database\n\nOption S – separate services\n  [User service] &lt;--&gt; [Media services] &lt;--&gt; [Billing]\n        |                    |                  |\n     Own data             Own data           Own data<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Neither representation is universally superior. A small team that\nreleases infrequently may benefit more from a clearly modularised\napplication than from a distributed system. If independent development\nteams, different load profiles or special isolation requirements exist,\na separation may be sensible. The new boundary should create genuine\norganisational or domain benefit rather than merely making an\narchitecture diagram more impressive.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Operability<\/strong> – the ability to operate, observe,\nmodify and recover a system securely – is a quality attribute in its own\nright. Architectural decisions without responsible people, alerting\npaths and recovery knowledge are incomplete.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A decision matrix: useful, but not a mathematical\njudgement<\/h2>\n<p>Consider <strong>two hypothetical alternatives<\/strong> for a small\nonline service: M (modular monolith) and S (multiple microservices).\nFive criteria are assigned weights that total 100%. The ratings from 1\n(poor) to 5 (good) are <strong>freely invented teaching\nassessments<\/strong>, not measured data.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Weight<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">M<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">S<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Easy maintenance by a team of two<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent scaling of individual functions<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End-to-end transactions<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Easy fault diagnosis<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent releases<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For M, the weighted mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}30\\times 5 + 0{,}20\\times 2 + 0{,}20\\times 5 + 0{,}20\\times 4 + 0{,}10\\times 2 = 3{,}90<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor S, the same calculation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}30\\times 2 + 0{,}20\\times 5 + 0{,}20\\times 2 + 0{,}20\\times 2 + 0{,}10\\times 5 = 2{,}90<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion only under these assumptions:<\/strong> M is better\nsuited to the constructed scenario. The scores are not objective. If\nteam size, load profile or the separability of the domain changes, the\nweights and results may differ. A decision matrix transparently shows\n<strong>which goals are preferred<\/strong>; it does not prove that the\npreferences are correct.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Exclusion criteria<\/strong> come first: if an alternative\ncannot fulfil a mandatory data protection or correctness requirement, a\ngood average score must not be allowed to compensate for it. A useful\nsensitivity check then asks how much the result changes if weights or\nratings are shifted by one point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Understanding trade-offs as a Pareto question<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>trade-off<\/strong> is a conflict between goals: an\nimprovement in one dimension may create disadvantages elsewhere. In\nsimplified terms, <strong>Pareto dominance<\/strong> occurs when one\nalternative is at least as good in every criterion considered and better\nin at least one. If no such clear winner exists, priorities must be\nestablished.<\/p>\n<p>A particularly instructive comparison is:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A higher number of replicas may increase availability under\nparticular failure assumptions, but raises costs and the attack\nsurface.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">More security tests may slow releases, but reduce the probability of\nsubsequent disruptions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Extensive logs facilitate troubleshooting, but may cause costs and\ndata protection risks.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Strong consistency may prevent incorrect domain bookings, but may\nalso block certain writes during a partition.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Architecture guides such as the Microsoft Azure Well-Architected\nFramework explicitly treat reliability, security, cost, operational\nquality and performance as goals to be balanced against each other.\nThese principles are generally understandable, even if the specific\nrecommendations of one provider do not automatically apply to every\nother operator. [Q5]<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Architecture Decision Records: why a decision must be\ntraceable<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>Architecture Decision Record (ADR)<\/strong> is a short,\nenduringly readable record of a decision. It documents not simply “We\nchoose product X”, but the initial situation, alternatives considered,\ncriteria, consequences and conditions for later reassessment. Without\nthis justification, an architecture in two years often appears arbitrary\nor “historically evolved”.<\/p>\n<p>A schematic decision record, <strong>not intended to be\nexecuted<\/strong>, might look like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>ADR-04: Data storage for a private media catalogue\nStatus: accepted for the assumed single-user operation\nContext: small dataset, local use, few writes\nDecision: embedded relational database\nAlternatives: CSV file, separate database server\nRationale: relationships and transactions without running a server\nConsequences: backup routine, schema changes, limited concurrent write load\nReassess if: multi-user operation or a substantial increase in write volume<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A decision need not remain correct forever.\n<strong>Reversibility<\/strong> is part of quality: can data be exported?\nIs the application decoupled through clearly named interfaces? Can\nsecurity measures be extended? A cautious architecture commits where\ndomain requirements are clear and avoids unnecessary commitments where\nuncertainty is high.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S22\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application scenario I: local media catalogue<\/h2>\n<p>A local media catalogue manages around 20,000 video files and\nmetadata for a single user. Typical requirements are reliable searches\nby date and keyword, easy correction of titles, recovery after a disk\nfailure and no external Internet access. The number is assumed.<\/p>\n<p>A suitable architecture might look like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Local interface\n      |\n      v\nCatalogue logic ----&gt; Embedded relational database\n      |                          |\n      +----&gt; Media directory     +--&gt; Independent backup<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Data decision:<\/strong> An embedded relational database can\nmanage relationships and transactions without requiring a server\nservice. <strong>Security decision:<\/strong> The application exposes no\npublic interface; access rights are based on the local user account and\nfile system. <strong>Operational decision:<\/strong> The database and\nmedia directory are backed up using a verifiable, consistent procedure.\n<strong>Limitation:<\/strong> If several users are to write\nsimultaneously from different computers, the access and synchronisation\nmodel must be reconsidered.<\/p>\n<p>An unnecessary three-server cluster would increase effort here\nwithout better meeting an established requirement. The benefit of a\nsimple architecture is precisely that risks, data flows and recovery\nremain manageable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S23\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application scenario II: public media platform with user\naccounts<\/h2>\n<p>A web platform permits uploads and public as well as private media.\nSeveral trust boundaries, data protection obligations and differing load\nprofiles now arise. The system needs, for example, user identities,\nsharing rules, a metadata store, object storage and background\nprocessing for thumbnails.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Internet\n   |\nHTTPS / rate limit\n   |\nWeb API -- identity / authorisation\n   |                         |\n   +--&gt; Metadata database    +--&gt; Audit event\n   |\n   +--&gt; Object storage (private and public objects)\n   |\n   +--&gt; Job queue --&gt; Thumbnail workers<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Data decision:<\/strong> Metadata and files can be managed\nseparately. Public lists may be served from a cache or read replica;\nprivate access control must apply to every resource request.\n<strong>Security decision:<\/strong> A guessable file ID must not replace\nread permission; in particular, the provision of object URLs and\nthumbnails must be checked against the sharing status.\n<strong>Operational decision:<\/strong> Failed worker jobs require retry\nand idempotence rules. The delay between upload and availability of the\npreview is made apparent to users.<\/p>\n<p>An initially modular application is often sufficient. Only when\ngenuinely different scaling or organisational requirements arise is the\nbenefit of independent services weighed against their operational\nconsequences. Using a “cloud platform” does not automatically mean using\nevery conceivable cloud product.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S24\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application scenario III: binding booking or credit balance\nsystem<\/h2>\n<p>A binding booking system must distinguish more strictly between\n<strong>displaying<\/strong>, <strong>provisionally reserving<\/strong>\nand <strong>legally binding confirmation<\/strong>. A rapidly updated\ndisplay may be incidental; a duplicate binding booking would, by\ncontrast, be a domain error. Rules and a clear transaction boundary are\nnecessary for each state transition.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Request with unique order ID\n      |\nValidate + check authorisation\n      |\nTransaction: reservation / booking rule\n      |\nPersist confirmed state\n      |\nReturn receipt or unambiguously queryable status<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Data decision:<\/strong> Unique keys and transactions prevent\ndefined duplicate processing within the database boundary.\n<strong>Security decision:<\/strong> Bookings may only be initiated by\nauthorised identities; critical changes must be logged traceably.\n<strong>Architectural decision:<\/strong> An external service or a second\ndatabase system creates new failure and reconciliation cases.\n<strong>Operational decision:<\/strong> The recovery objective and\ntreatment of jobs whose completion is unclear must be established before\nlaunch.<\/p>\n<p>After a timeout, the state is not necessarily “failed”. The request\nmay have been processed and only the receipt may be missing. The client\nmust therefore not simply initiate a second independent booking.\nInstead, it can query the status of the same order ID. This is precisely\nwhere the uncertainties of distributed systems covered in M7-L3 affect a\nconcrete architectural decision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S25\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Typical poor decisions and their causes<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Technology before requirements:<\/strong> Choose Kubernetes\nand microservices first, and look for a problem afterwards. This\nincreases the risk of unnecessary complexity.<br />\n<strong>Relying on one protective mechanism:<\/strong> A user account\ndoes not automatically constitute effective access control for every\nrecord.<br />\n<strong>Confusing a database replica with a backup:<\/strong> Replication\ncan propagate unintended changes.<br />\n<strong>Never deleting anything:<\/strong> Permanent retention without\npurpose exacerbates data protection and security risks.<br />\n<strong>Spurious precision in metrics:<\/strong> Risk matrices and\navailability percentages without data, time periods and failure models\nconvey unwarranted certainty.<br />\n<strong>Unclear responsibility:<\/strong> A complex system without\nmaintenance time, documentation and a recovery plan cannot be operated\nsustainably.<br />\n<strong>Optimising prematurely:<\/strong> Additional caches or services\nwithout a measured bottleneck create new consistency and failure\nscenarios.<br />\n<strong>Concealing trade-offs:<\/strong> The statement “faster, cheaper\nand more secure” without stated assumptions is not a technical\njustification.<\/p>\n<p>The common cause is not a lack of product knowledge, but an\n<strong>unsubstantiated transition from objective, assumption and\nevidence to a final design decision<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S26\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How decisions remain verifiable<\/h2>\n<p>A scientific approach can be derived from M7-L1: an architectural\nclaim is formulated as a hypothesis. For example: “Under the expected\nread profile, a cache reduces p95 response time without violating the\npermissible data freshness.” This leads to two measures to be checked –\nresponse time and data age – and a description of the load and failure\nprofile.<\/p>\n<p>The formal aspect comes from M7-L2: “A private record may only be\nreturned to authorised users” is a <strong>security property<\/strong>\nthat must be examined in interface, database and application tests. The\ndisruption dimension follows from M7-L3: what applies when a database\nresponse fails to arrive, a message is repeated or a replica is out of\ndate?<\/p>\n<p>No single method suffices on its own:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Claim<\/th>\n<th>Suitable evidence<\/th>\n<th>Limitation<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Domain rule is always preserved<\/td>\n<td>Model, invariant, targeted tests<\/td>\n<td>Implementation may differ from model<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Request time under defined load<\/td>\n<td>Reproducible benchmark procedure<\/td>\n<td>Future load may differ<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unauthorised access is prevented<\/td>\n<td>Threat model, permission tests, review<\/td>\n<td>Unknown errors remain possible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backup is usable<\/td>\n<td>Recovery test<\/td>\n<td>Not every conceivable disaster scenario is covered<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operation is economical<\/td>\n<td>Full-cost model and sensitivity check<\/td>\n<td>Costs and load change<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>This does not “definitively prove” the architecture. It makes it\n<strong>justifiable, verifiable and correctable in the light of new\nevidence<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S27\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition to M7-L5<\/h2>\n<p>Data, security and architectural decisions are parts of the same\nproblem. The data structure determines which consistency rules can be\nenforced easily. System boundaries define where permissions apply and\nwhich failures may occur. The failure model determines which\navailability and recovery commitments are realistic. Costs arise not\nonly from servers, but also from maintenance, protection, monitoring and\nrecovery.<\/p>\n<p>A scientifically defensible architecture makes <strong>requirements,\nassumptions, risks, alternatives, trade-offs and evidence<\/strong>\nvisible. It eliminates unnecessary complexity without downplaying\nmandatory correctness or protection requirements. It allows later\nrevision when observations contradict original expectations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Connection within the course:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1 – Scientific models<\/a> explains\nverifiable hypotheses; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2 –\nCorrectness<\/a> formal guarantees; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3 – Distributed systems<\/a>\nfailures and consistency among multiple participants. The subsequent <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5 – Evaluating AI systems\nscientifically<\/a> applies these criteria to datasets, model quality,\ndistribution shift, robustness and responsibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S28\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Position within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This chapter belongs to the <strong>six-part Master core<\/strong>,\nnot to the optional additional Master electives. Navigation: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">previous Master core lesson M7-L3<\/a>;\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">next Master core lesson M7-L5<\/a>;\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">reading paths and subject-specific\ncross-references<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course\nindex<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S29\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 05.11.2026<\/p>\n<p>The following sources establish the subject-matter principles; the\narchitectures, figures and assessment matrices described are explicitly\noriginal <strong>didactic constructions<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q1] NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1<\/strong>, <em>Guide for\nConducting Risk Assessments<\/em> (2012): <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/30/r1/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/30/r1/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q2] OWASP Cheat Sheet Series<\/strong>, <em>Threat Modeling\nCheat Sheet<\/em>: <a\nhref=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Threat_Modeling_Cheat_Sheet.html\">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Threat_Modeling_Cheat_Sheet.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q3] OWASP Top 10:2025<\/strong>, overview of web application\nrisks: <a\nhref=\"https://top10.owasp.org/2025/\">https://top10.owasp.org/2025/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q4] NIST SP 800-207<\/strong>, <em>Zero Trust\nArchitecture<\/em> (2020): <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q5] Microsoft<\/strong>, <em>Azure Well-Architected\nFramework<\/em>, quality pillars and trade-offs: <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/what-is-well-architected-framework\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/what-is-well-architected-framework<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q6] Microsoft<\/strong>, <em>Cost Optimization\nTradeoffs<\/em>, availability/security/operational consequences: <a\nhref=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/cost-optimization/tradeoffs\">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/cost-optimization/tradeoffs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q7] European Union<\/strong>, <em>GDPR, Regulation (EU)\n2016/679<\/em>, in particular Articles 5 and 25: <a\nhref=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/\">https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q8] PostgreSQL<\/strong>, <em>Row Security Policies<\/em>\n(current documentation): <a\nhref=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html\">https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Date of source consultation:<\/strong> 05.11.2026. Technical\nproduct pages and regulatory legal conditions are subject to change and\nmust be checked again for specific applications.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M7-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M7-L5 – Scientific evaluation of AI systems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Master’s-oriented computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master’s orientation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L6<\/a> · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Guiding question:<\/strong> When is the statement ‘This AI\nsystem is good, reliable or safe’ actually justified, and when is it\nmerely an insufficiently tested claim?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Note on evidence:<\/strong> All figures, measurement series,\npeople and system examples in this lesson are explicitly\n<strong>constructed for teaching purposes<\/strong>. They do not come\nfrom actual benchmark studies. Verified technical concepts and\nmethodological guidelines are supported by traceable references in the\nsources section. An illustrative calculation does not establish actual\nmodel performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting point: What exactly is being evaluated?<\/h2>\n<p>An AI model is initially a computational rule with learned\nparameters. An <strong>AI system<\/strong> encompasses more:\npreprocessing, data sources, the model, query interface, possibly search\ntools or a database, rules, user interface and the ways in which people\nhandle results. For example, a powerful language model may exist whose\napplication is nevertheless unreliable because it retrieves outdated\ndocuments or executes external actions without checking them.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_049_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Five columns covering quality, robustness, safety, costs and documentation, with example evaluation questions and evidence.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Five separate AI assessment dimensions—quality,\nrobustness, safety, cost and documentation—each with an example\ncriterion and evidence.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>AI evaluation requires distinct criteria and traceable evidence.<\/p>\n<p>Three different statements must be distinguished:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>The model can solve a task under test conditions.<\/strong>\nThis can be substantiated for selected tasks using measurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>The entire application works reliably in a particular\nenvironment.<\/strong> This includes data quality, operation and error\nhandling.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>The application should be deployed in this\nenvironment.<\/strong> This additionally requires an assessment of the\nconsequences of errors, data protection and responsibility.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>No single percentage answers any of these questions completely. Even\nthe question of <em>which<\/em> inputs are considered typical is a\ndomain-specific decision. The difference between an optional photo\ntagger and a system for reviewing medical findings is not only the\ndifficulty of the data, but above all the significance of a wrong\ndecision.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Application objective and risks\n           |\n           v\nTest tasks + suitable data + reference answers\n           |\n           v\n       Test AI system\n           |\n           v\nResult quality + uncertainty + types of error\n           |\n           v\n   Suitable for this specific use?<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Scientific assessment is therefore not a ranking, but a limited,\njustified statement about the <em>system, task, data basis, time period\nand conditions<\/em>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a marketing claim to a testable hypothesis<\/h2>\n<p>‘The new model understands our customers better’ sounds convincing,\nbut cannot be tested without verifiable terms. How is ‘understands’\nrecognised? Is the aim to classify incoming messages correctly, improve\nthe quality of the generated text or determine which cases should be\npassed to people?<\/p>\n<p>A testable statement might be: ‘For German-language enquiries from\nour support archive, system B identifies the correct one of six agreed\ntopic classes more often than system A, without increasing the number of\nserious misclassifications.’ This at least makes the target group, basis\nof comparison and error type identifiable in principle.<\/p>\n<p>For a robust evaluation, the following are documented in advance:\n<strong>purpose, population, inclusions and exclusions, success measure,\nsafety limits, comparison system, test design and decision\nrule.<\/strong> Only then does testing begin. This reduces the risk of\nsubsequently presenting only the figures that support the desired\nresult.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Context from M7-L1:<\/strong> A hypothesis is not strong\nmerely because it looks mathematical. It must be refutable on the basis\nof suitable observations. A correct calculation using unsuitable data\ndoes not yield a defensible conclusion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is this excessive?<\/strong> A research study would be\nunnecessary for privately organising a few non-critical photos. A small\ndocumented sampling procedure may suffice. <strong>When is it\nindispensable?<\/strong> Where errors may have legal, financial, medical\nor significant societal consequences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evaluation is a comparison under specified conditions<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Evaluation<\/strong> means systematic assessment against\njustified criteria. A <strong>metric<\/strong> is a defined measure; a\n<strong>benchmark<\/strong> is a standardised comparison procedure with\ntasks, data and an evaluation specification. Neither a metric nor a\nbenchmark is automatically objective for every purpose.<\/p>\n<p>A good experiment distinguishes at least five levels:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level<\/th>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Possible weakness<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Target task<\/td>\n<td>What should work?<\/td>\n<td>Objective too vague or too narrow<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dataset<\/td>\n<td>Which cases are represented?<\/td>\n<td>Relevant cases missing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference<\/td>\n<td>What is considered correct?<\/td>\n<td>Disagreement or incorrect assessment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metric<\/td>\n<td>How much does each error count?<\/td>\n<td>Important error costs remain invisible<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transfer<\/td>\n<td>Will the result still apply later?<\/td>\n<td>Different operation, users or data<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The scientific objective is not ‘the largest possible number’, but\n<strong>the greatest possible explanatory value for the actual\nquestion<\/strong>. An overall benchmark winner may lag far behind a\nsmaller specialised model on an individual task.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>Holistic Evaluation of Language Models (HELM)<\/strong>\nresearch programme therefore emphasises evaluating language models\nacross many scenarios and multiple dimensions, such as accuracy,\ncalibration, robustness, fairness and efficiency. According to the\nproject documentation, the associated open-source environment entered\nmaintenance mode in June 2026; the methodological idea remains relevant\nindependently of this [Q4, Q5].<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Population, sample and representativeness<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>population<\/strong> comprises the cases about which a\nstatement is to be made. A <strong>sample<\/strong> contains cases that\nare actually examined. A model may work exceptionally well on 10,000\npublic sample texts and fail on 100 real, substantially different\nsupport enquiries.<\/p>\n<p>Example: An image search is intended to organise private photo\narchives. Users have night photographs, old scanned paper photographs,\nblurred mobile phone images and digital illustrations. A test using\nexclusively bright, high-quality studio photographs would be convenient,\nbut hardly representative. A greater number of similar studio images\nwould not remedy this selection bias.<\/p>\n<p>The origins of the data, the collection period, the languages or\nformats included, measurement errors, duplicate occurrences and missing\ncases must therefore be documented. A dataset <strong>datasheet<\/strong>\nrecords the motivation, composition, collection and recommended use\n[Q6]. Overlooked negative examples may be particularly important: a\nsystem for detecting damaged media files must also encounter rare codecs\nand edge cases.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Representative<\/strong> does not necessarily mean exactly\nproportional. Very rare, particularly dangerous cases may deliberately\nbe overrepresented in safety tests. However, the actual error frequency\nof the overall population must not then be calculated from these test\ndata without appropriate weighting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Training, validation and testing: Three different roles<\/h2>\n<p>During <strong>training<\/strong>, model parameters are learned from\nexamples. <strong>Validation<\/strong> supports the selection of settings\nand model variants. A previously held-out <strong>test set<\/strong> is\nintended to evaluate the selected solution finally on unseen cases. This\nseparation only works if the test set does not indirectly become a\ntraining signal through many small adjustments [Q7].<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Complete dataset\n   |\n   +--&gt; Training: learn parameters\n   |\n   +--&gt; Validation: select model and thresholds\n   |\n   +--&gt; Test: predefined final assessment<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A common error is <strong>data leakage<\/strong>, the improper\ninclusion of information that would not be available under subsequent\ndeployment conditions. Duplicates, nearly identical passages or images\nfrom the same series can end up on both sides of a random split. A\nphotograph from the same short burst is often less independent than a\nphotograph from a completely different collection.<\/p>\n<p>Depending on the task, a split by <strong>person, device, building,\ndocument family or time<\/strong> is therefore more appropriate than a\npurely random row split. For temporal predictions, future information\nmust not appear unnoticed in training. Scaling or selecting features\nacross all the data before splitting can also artificially improve test\nperformance.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> With public language models, the exact\ncomposition of the training data is often not traceable. A publicly\nknown test set may have appeared in pre-training, post-training or\nindirect optimisation loops. A high benchmark score alone is therefore\nno guarantee of unaffected generalisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reference answers are themselves a source of error<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>label<\/strong> is an assignment such as ‘spam’, ‘not spam’\nor ‘device damaged’. <strong>Ground truth<\/strong> denotes the reference\ntaken to be correct. In some tasks, this is objectively verifiable, such\nas an exact calculation with clear assumptions. In other tasks there are\nlegitimate differences of judgement: Is a summary complete? Is a comment\nironic? Is an image ‘aesthetically convincing’?<\/p>\n<p>For difficult tasks, evaluation needs a <strong>scoring\nrubric<\/strong>: which properties must be present, which errors are\ncritical, and when is an answer unclear or impossible to assess? Several\nknowledgeable evaluators can judge independently. Agreement between\nevaluators is itself an informative quantity, but not proof of truth. A\nshared erroneous rule may lead to high agreement and nevertheless to an\nincorrect reference.<\/p>\n<p>When language-model responses are evaluated automatically, other\nlanguage models are sometimes used as judges. This saves effort but can\nintroduce <strong>position, style, length or model preferences<\/strong>.\nSuch assessments should be calibrated against human judgements and\nchecked by sampling important cases.<\/p>\n<p>It is important to distinguish <strong>objective agreement<\/strong>\nfrom <strong>domain usefulness<\/strong>: two differently worded answers\ncan be factually equivalent. Conversely, a linguistically similar answer\ncan contain a decisive factual error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Classification: The confusion matrix<\/h2>\n<p>For a model that distinguishes between two classes, the\n<strong>confusion matrix<\/strong> is a good starting point. Consider a\n<strong>purely invented<\/strong> test example: of 10,000 messages, 200\nare actually unwanted and 9,800 wanted. A filter correctly flags 160\nunwanted messages, misses 40 and incorrectly flags 196 wanted\nmessages.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-23\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-23\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-23\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-30\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Actual state<\/th>\n<th>Flagged as unwanted<\/th>\n<th>Not flagged<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Total<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Unwanted<\/td>\n<td>160 true positives (TP)<\/td>\n<td>40 false negatives (FN)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">200<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wanted<\/td>\n<td>196 false positives (FP)<\/td>\n<td>9,604 true negatives (TN)<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9,800<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>356<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>9,644<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>10,000<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>TP<\/strong> stands for <em>true positive<\/em>,\n<strong>FP<\/strong> for <em>false positive<\/em>, <strong>FN<\/strong> for\n<em>false negative<\/em> and <strong>TN<\/strong> for <em>true\nnegative<\/em>. These terms always refer to the previously defined\n‘positive’ class. Positive does not mean good or desirable here, but\nsimply the category sought.<\/p>\n<p>Why is the matrix more valuable than a single rate? Because it shows\n<strong>which<\/strong> errors occur. Personal messages incorrectly\nflagged as unwanted might be considerably more disruptive than a few\nadvertising messages that slip through. Another operation may weight\nexactly the same matrix in the opposite way.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Accuracy, precision, recall and M7-L1 – fully worked<\/h2>\n<p>Different quantities follow from the matrix:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Accuracy<\/strong> (overall correctness):\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>160<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>604<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>97<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{TP}+\\mathrm{TN}}N=\\frac{160+9\\,604}{10\\,000}=97{,}64\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Precision<\/strong> (precision of positive predictions):\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>160<\/mn><mn>356<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>44<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>94<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{TP}}{\\mathrm{TP}+\\mathrm{FP}}=\\frac{160}{356}\\approx44{,}94\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Recall<\/strong> (sensitivity/detection rate):\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>160<\/mn><mn>200<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{TP}}{\\mathrm{TP}+\\mathrm{FN}}=\\frac{160}{200}=80\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Specificity<\/strong> (detection rate of negative cases):\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>604<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>800<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>98<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm{TN}}{\\mathrm{TN}+\\mathrm{FP}}=\\frac{9\\,604}{9\\,800}=98\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>F1 score<\/strong>, the harmonic mean of precision and\nrecall:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>320<\/mn><mn>556<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>57<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>55<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{2\\mathrm{TP}}{2\\mathrm{TP}+\\mathrm{FP}+\\mathrm{FN}}=\\frac{320}{556}\\approx57{,}55\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The surprising result: a trivial solution that treats\n<strong>every<\/strong> message as wanted achieves <strong>98%\naccuracy<\/strong> in this dataset but detects <strong>not a\nsingle<\/strong> unwanted message. Our filter achieves 97.64% accuracy\nand is actually worse according to this measure, despite detecting a\nlarge proportion of unwanted messages.<\/p>\n<p>The reason is the imbalanced <strong>base rate<\/strong>: positive\ncases are rare. For rare events, high overall accuracy is often not\nparticularly informative. Precision answers ‘How often is a positive\nflag correct?’; recall answers ‘How many of the actual positive cases\nare detected?’ Both respond differently to thresholds and\nfrequencies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>F1 is not a universal solution:<\/strong> it accounts for\nneither true negatives nor the actual costs of different errors. It is\nonly well suited if the desired balance between precision and recall\nmatches the task. Medical decisions would require other requirements and\ndomain-specific checks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Thresholds, base rates and the costs of incorrect\ndecisions<\/h2>\n<p>Many classifiers produce a score. A positive decision is triggered at\na specified <strong>threshold<\/strong>. A high threshold may reduce\nfalse alarms but miss more genuine cases. The following figures are a\nsecond <strong>invented<\/strong> possible evaluation of the same\n10,000-case test set:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">TP<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">FP<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">FN<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">TN<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Precision<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Recall<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strict<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">120<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9,770<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80.0%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">60.0%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medium threshold<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">160<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">196<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">40<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9,604<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">44.9%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80.0%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lenient<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">180<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">500<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9,300<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">26.5%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">90.0%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>With a strict threshold, flags are more reliable, but 80 unwanted\nmessages remain undetected. With a lenient threshold, recall increases,\nbut 500 wanted messages are wrongly flagged. Which setting is\nappropriate cannot be deduced from the table alone.<\/p>\n<p>Instead of automatic deletion, a system can therefore use three\npaths: let clearly unremarkable messages through, move moderately\nsuspicious cases to a folder that can be reviewed, and automatically\nblock only clearly harmful incoming messages. The <strong>graduation of\nconsequences<\/strong> is part of the architecture, not merely a\nstatistical optimisation.<\/p>\n<p>A shift in the base rate also matters: the same sensitivity and\nspecificity can lead to a different precision when unwanted messages\noccur at a different frequency. A test result must not be directly\nextrapolated to a new population without taking its distribution into\naccount.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Probabilities and calibration<\/h2>\n<p>A model may output ‘80%’. This can mean different things: a genuine\nestimated probability, a rescaled internal measure or simply a verbal\nself-assessment. Only a defined measurement procedure and its\nverification make the number interpretable.<\/p>\n<p>For probabilistic predictions, <strong>calibration<\/strong> means, in\nsimplified terms, that of many cases with a predicted probability of\n0.8, approximately 80% should experience the specified event under\nappropriate conditions. In a constructed group of 100 predictions, all\nat 0.8, of which 72 are correct, the observed proportion is 72%. This\nindicates possible overconfidence, but with only 100 cases it is not yet\nconclusive evidence of a systematic error.<\/p>\n<p>A calibration chart known as a <strong>reliability diagram<\/strong>\ngroups predictions by estimated probability and compares predictions\nwith the observed proportion. Possible metrics are the <strong>Brier\nscore<\/strong> or <strong>log loss<\/strong>; they assess probabilities,\nnot merely hard class decisions. High classification accuracy does not\nimply good calibration.<\/p>\n<p>Particularly important for generative AI: the verbal statement ‘I am\n90% sure’ cannot automatically be equated with a calibrated probability.\nA model may answer incorrectly with great confidence; conversely, it may\nexpress a correct answer cautiously. Confidence statements must be\ntested separately on a clearly defined task distribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement uncertainty: Why 88% is not simply better than\n86%<\/h2>\n<p>Every finite test contains random fluctuations and possible\nsystematic biases. Consider another <strong>constructed<\/strong>\nexample: 350 correct predictions among 400 independent cases drawn in\nthe same way give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>350<\/mn><mn>400<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>87<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{350}{400}=87{,}5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For a binomial proportion, the simplified <strong>standard\nerror<\/strong> is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\frac{p(1-p)}n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>875<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>125<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>400<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0165<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\frac{0{,}875\\times0{,}125}{400}}\\approx0{,}0165<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about 1.65 percentage points. Using the simple normal approximation,\na rough 95% interval is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>87<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>65<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">percentage points<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">87{,}5\\,\\%\\pm1{,}96\\times1{,}65\\,\\text{percentage points}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence about <strong>84.3% to 90.7%<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>This approximation assumes, among other things, independent\nobservations with comparable distributions and appropriate sampling. For\nsmall counts, extreme proportions or dependent data, other procedures\nare more suitable, such as Wilson intervals or cluster-aware analyses.\nUnder the commonly used frequentist interpretation, a 95% confidence\ninterval does <strong>not<\/strong> mean that the unknown fixed parameter\nretrospectively lies within this particular interval with 95%\nprobability.<\/p>\n<p>For two models on the <strong>same<\/strong> cases, paired errors are\nparticularly informative: which examples does only A solve, and which\nonly B? A <strong>paired comparison<\/strong> is often more precise than\ntwo isolated proportions. If many variants are tried and only the best\nfigure is published, the risk of an apparent improvement due to chance\nalso increases. Predefined primary metrics and separate confirmation\ndata help counter this.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Generalisation: Good test figures are not a law of\nnature<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Generalisation<\/strong> refers to the ability to transfer\nlearned behaviour to suitable, previously unseen cases. A dataset,\nhowever, is only a selection of possible inputs. If its context changes,\nperformance may change as well.<\/p>\n<p>Examples of distinctions include:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Covariate shift:<\/strong> The distribution of inputs\nchanges, for example different cameras or writing styles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Label shift:<\/strong> The frequencies of the target classes\nchange, for example less spam.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Concept drift:<\/strong> The relationship between input and\ntarget changes, for example new fraud patterns.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These concepts presuppose defined variables and tasks; in practice\nseveral effects can occur together. An old text-classification model\ndoes not automatically become unusable, but yesterday’s benchmark does\nnot guarantee quality next year.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Out-of-distribution (OOD)<\/strong> describes inputs that\ndiffer substantially from the tested data environment. These include\nmalformed file formats, dialects, unusual lighting conditions or greatly\nchanged user enquiries. A sensible system not only recognises as much as\npossible, but signals when it reaches its limits under unfamiliar\nconditions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> During extended operation, with\nchanging data sources or decisions with consequences. <strong>When is it\nof little help?<\/strong> Constant retraining without demonstrated drift\ncan generate unnecessary costs and new errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Robustness: How does a model respond to small changes?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>robust<\/strong> system preserves its desired properties as\nfar as possible under relevant, foreseeable disturbances. A harmless\nrephrasing of the question, slight image compression or a typographical\nerror should not cause completely different results, provided the domain\nmeaning has not changed.<\/p>\n<p>Robustness must be tested <strong>against specific kinds of\nchange<\/strong>. A model may be insensitive to image noise but sensitive\nto an unfavourable crop. For texts, synonyms, sentence order, foreign\nlanguages, irony or deliberately misleading passages may be\nrelevant.<\/p>\n<p>There is a crucial distinction between <strong>natural\ndisturbances<\/strong> and <strong>targeted adversarial attacks<\/strong>.\nIn the latter case, someone actively tries to deceive a model. The NIST\nreport on the security of machine learning distinguishes, among other\nthings, evasion attacks, manipulation of training data and privacy\nattacks [Q3].<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Original, correct input\n       |\n       +--&gt; ordinary variant: spelling error, different light\n       |\n       +--&gt; targeted variant: attack, false embedded instruction\n       |\n       v\nCompare outputs: correct? safe? same meaning?<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>‘Robust’ must not be interpreted as a universal security guarantee.\nMeasured resistance to five disturbance types proves nothing about a\nsixth, previously untested attack.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Subgroups, fairness and concealed averages<\/h2>\n<p>An overall metric can conceal structural differences. A transcription\nsystem may achieve a different error rate for clearly spoken standard\nGerman than for dialects or loud background noise. What matters is\nwhether the affected groups are relevant to the intended use and whether\nthe system works appropriately for them.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>slice analysis<\/strong> divides test results into\nmeaningful, domain-relevant subgroups. In a purely instructional\nexample, a model achieves 90% correct answers overall. For a group of\n900 commonly represented standard cases, suppose 855 are correct (95%);\nfor 100 rare special cases, only 45 (45%): together\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>855<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>45<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{855+45}{1\\,000}=90\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe overall value conceals the very weak performance of the second\ngroup.<\/p>\n<p>Subgroup results must not be casually interpreted as stable\nstatements. With small case numbers, uncertainties are larger; many\nretrospective divisions may generate chance patterns. Sensitive\ncharacteristics and personal information also require careful legal and\nethical handling. Some fairness criteria may mathematically conflict\nwhen groups have different base rates.<\/p>\n<p>The scientifically meaningful question is not simply ‘Is the model\nfair?’, but: <strong>Which relevant disadvantage is to be ruled out,\nusing which data and criteria, with what uncertainty and under which\ntrade-offs?<\/strong> A high mean is no substitute for examining the\nconsequences of errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Language models: Why ‘correct’ is often difficult to\nmeasure<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>large language model (LLM)<\/strong> generates language\nsequences based on its trained parameters and a context. The inputs are\nopen-ended, and there are often several good answers. Exact matching to\na single model answer is therefore not always sufficient.<\/p>\n<p>For a factual question, a rubric may require domain correctness,\ncompleteness, compliance with the instruction, properly acknowledging\nmissing information and justified use of sources. For drafting text,\nreadability, suitability and style become more important; for a software\nchange, executable tests and verified boundary conditions may matter\nmore than an attractive explanation.<\/p>\n<p>Different tasks require different methods:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task<\/th>\n<th>Useful assessment dimension<\/th>\n<th>Typical blind spot<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Factual question<\/td>\n<td>Factual correctness with verifiable evidence<\/td>\n<td>Correct statement with incorrect evidence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Summary<\/td>\n<td>Essential content, no inventions<\/td>\n<td>Superficial word similarity<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Code<\/td>\n<td>Behaviour under tests, safety, maintainability<\/td>\n<td>Syntactic validity only<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dialogue<\/td>\n<td>Goal achievement, follow-up questions, uncertainty<\/td>\n<td>Individual reply without conversation context<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tool use<\/td>\n<td>Permitted and successful action<\/td>\n<td>Success message without actual effect<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>An LLM may perform well on multiple-choice questions and be\nunreliable when summarising long, unusual documents. Evaluation must\ntherefore be tailored to the <em>intended workflow<\/em>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hallucination, grounding and knowledge-supported\nsystems<\/h2>\n<p>In generative AI, a <strong>hallucination<\/strong> often refers to a\nfactually unsupported but plausibly worded output. Because the term is\nimprecise, it is better in studies to distinguish concrete error\nclasses: incorrect fact, unsupported claim, fabricated source,\nmisleading conclusion or contradiction of the supplied material.<\/p>\n<p>With <strong>retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)<\/strong>, text\ngeneration supplemented by document search, an additional chain of\npotential errors arises:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Question -&gt; Document retrieval -&gt; Retrieved passages -&gt; Language model\n                   |                     |                    |\n              Wrong match         Relevant passage?     Used correctly?\n                                                             |\n                                                             v\n                                                    Answer + evidence<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>An incorrect result may originate in retrieval, incomplete documents,\nthe context window or actual answer formulation. Therefore\n<strong>retrieval quality<\/strong>, <strong>faithfulness to\nevidence<\/strong> and <strong>answer usefulness<\/strong> are evaluated\nseparately. The mere formal presence of a citation does not constitute a\ncorrect justification. The cited passage must actually support the\nspecific claim.<\/p>\n<p>A useful example is an internal document assistant: it must answer a\nquestion about a fee rule using the currently valid document. An\noutdated but easily retrievable document must not silently take\nprecedence. Metadata, versioning, search filters and the option to\nanswer ‘insufficient evidence’ belong to the overall system under\ntest.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Prompt variants, randomness and fair comparison\nconditions<\/h2>\n<p>In generative AI, the same question may elicit different answers on\nrepeated attempts due to stochastic selection. In many language-model\nenvironments, <strong>temperature<\/strong> influences the dispersion of\ntoken selection; the concrete effects of parameters depend on the model\nand interface used. Context length, system instructions, available tools\nand the state of external knowledge sources also change results.<\/p>\n<p>A fair comparison therefore documents at least the model designation\nand, if possible, exact version, date, input texts, system context,\ntools, output restrictions, sampling settings, prompt variants and cost\nconditions. One particularly successful answer is not a reliable\nmeasurement of quality.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Repeated testing<\/strong> may reveal variability. For\nexpensive tasks, however, a decision is needed about how many\nrepetitions justify the additional information gained. Identical seeds\nor deterministic settings sometimes improve reproducibility, but do not\nguarantee it for every backend and hardware configuration.<\/p>\n<p>Rephrasing a prompt may strongly favour one model. This is not always\nmanipulation – prompt design is part of actual applications. It becomes\nunfair if one model has been extensively optimised for the test set and\nthe other candidate has not, or if this optimisation is concealed. The\n<strong>overall process actually deployed in each case<\/strong> should\nbe evaluated, including prompting techniques and tool use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model as judge: Useful, but not the final authority<\/h2>\n<p>When hundreds of answers cannot be individually reviewed by experts,\nsome evaluations use automated <strong>LLM-as-a-judge<\/strong> methods.\nA language model then judges other outputs according to a rubric. This\nis inexpensive and may be useful for clearly defined criteria.<\/p>\n<p>The danger is that the judge prefers particular response styles,\nlonger texts or its own model family. The order of two compared answers\nmay also have an influence. The figure ‘8 out of 10’ does not\nautomatically become an objective measure of quality.<\/p>\n<p>Careful application combines fixed scoring rules with <strong>blinded\nordering<\/strong>, random variation of arrangement, independent human\nsamples and checking which error types lead to systematic divergence in\nautomated judgements. A particularly delicate edge case is judging\nanswers from a domain in which the judge model itself frequently makes\nerrors.<\/p>\n<p>For unambiguous tasks, direct verification should take precedence\nwherever possible: an arithmetic result can be recalculated, a program\ntested, a supplied link opened and a database query run against defined\nsample data. A judge model can supplement such evidence but cannot\nreplace it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Multimodal systems: Image, audio and video<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Multimodal models<\/strong> process different data types, such\nas text and image, sound and transcript, or video and description. A\nsystem may be strong in one modality and weak in another. For image\nmodels, for example, image quality, prompt alignment, preservation of\nspecified details, robustness and possible biases are distinct\ndimensions [Q8].<\/p>\n<p>A generator that produces beautiful images need not reliably follow a\nprecise spatial instruction. A video model may capture a style well but\nchange objects between frames uncontrollably. And an audio model that\ntranscribes speech fluently may fail more often on proper names or\nnumbers.<\/p>\n<p>Possible test conditions include:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Image:<\/strong> Count objects, compare colours and\npositions, assess image content in the target context.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Video:<\/strong> Temporal consistency, motion sequences,\nobject identity, transitions and physically relevant errors.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Audio:<\/strong> Word error rate, language variations,\nbackground noise, changes of speaker and correct numbers.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Mixed systems:<\/strong> Does a statement about an image\nremain grounded in actually visible features? Are inputs from different\nchannels combined correctly?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Aesthetic assessment may be subjective; aesthetic judgements should\ntherefore not automatically be used as factual statements about accuracy\nor safety.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security: The model is only part of the attack surface<\/h2>\n<p>AI risks include unintended errors and deliberate attacks. Examples\nto consider are <strong>prompt injection<\/strong> (external texts\nattempting to introduce instructions into the system), harmful or\nmanipulated training data (<strong>poisoning<\/strong>), deceptive inputs\nintended to bypass a detector (<strong>evasion<\/strong>), unwanted\ndisclosure of private information and abusive tool calls [Q3].<\/p>\n<p>A particularly important <strong>trust boundary<\/strong> separates an\nauthorised user’s instruction from the content of an external web page\nor file. A document may contain words such as ‘Ignore all previous\nrules’ without thereby becoming a legitimate control authority. For a\ntool-using agent, the evaluation should also test whether the system\nderives unauthorised actions from such text.<\/p>\n<p>Isolated test environments are appropriate: restrict read\npermissions, prevent unwanted actions, log attempts and respect the\nlimits of the evaluation system itself. A test that executes a critical\naction is no longer a harmless chat experiment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>No security from benchmarks alone:<\/strong> passing a known\nset of attacks does not mean that an application is immune to previously\nunknown attacks. Protection comes from system architecture, permissions,\nmonitoring, secure standards and repeated checks, not from a blanket\nstatement that ‘the model is safe’.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From model to ongoing operation: Drift, monitoring and\nfallback<\/h2>\n<p>A one-off model test is a <strong>snapshot<\/strong>. In production,\ninputs, legal conditions, technical dependencies and the available\ndocument collection may change. Replacing the model endpoint, changing\nthe retrieval configuration or introducing a new prompt template can\nalso change results.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Monitoring<\/strong> should not merely observe response times\nand costs. Domain-relevant measures include the proportion of\nincorrectly assigned cases, manual corrections, frequency of missing\nevidence, differences between subgroups, unexpected tool actions and the\nproportion of cases in which the system justifiably makes no\ndecision.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>regression<\/strong> means a deterioration of previously\nconfirmed properties. A sensible regression test collection therefore\ncontains precisely the difficult or frequently erroneous cases. However,\nit must not completely replace the independent test set: once\ndevelopment constantly optimises against the same error cases, the risk\nof overfitting increases.<\/p>\n<p>For critical changes, a <strong>phased rollout<\/strong> may be\nappropriate: first observe only, then deploy in a limited number of\nnon-critical cases, compare results and return to a verified state if\nsignificant errors occur. A fallback plan, human escalation and\ndocumented limitations are components of evaluation of the overall\nsystem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S22\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Effort, efficiency and environmental downstream costs<\/h2>\n<p>A model may supply somewhat better answers but consume considerably\nmore time, memory, energy or financial resources.\n<strong>Efficiency<\/strong> therefore belongs to evaluation as a\ndistinct dimension. Relevant quantities include latency distributions\n(such as p50 and p95), throughput, memory requirements, cost per validly\nsolved case and effort for human review.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>didactic model calculation<\/strong>, not a current provider\nprice: system A solves 900 out of 1,000 cases correctly in domain terms\nat a total cost of 40 compute units; system B solves 930 correctly at\n100 units. The pure computational cost per domain-correct case is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>40<\/mn><mn>900<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0444<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{40}{900}\\approx0{,}0444<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits for A and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>100<\/mn><mn>930<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1075<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{100}{930}\\approx0{,}1075<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunits for B. B is somewhat more accurate, but in this example incurs\nmore than twice the computational cost per correct case.<\/p>\n<p>Which system is preferable depends on the cost of errors, human\neffort, speed and risks. For a free photo recommendation, a difference\nof three percentage points may be worth little; where the consequences\nof an error are serious, the same improvement may be very important.\nMoreover, large models can be integrated more efficiently through\nintelligent tool use, caching or tiered calls.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> Energy and cost figures depend heavily\non hardware, utilisation, location, provider conditions and system\nboundaries. Blanket statements about environmental impact are unsuitable\nwithout a defined measurement method.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S23\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Architecture example I: A local media catalogue<\/h2>\n<p>A private media catalogue is intended to sort thousands of\nphotographs and videos by subject, capture situation and technical\nproperties. A small local model produces image tags; a search function\ncombines these with file metadata.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>File archive -&gt; Metadata analysis ----+\n                                     +--&gt; Tag/search index --&gt; Results list\n             -&gt; Image model ---------+\n                      |\n                      v\n              Flag uncertain tags<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Scientific testing asks for clear categories. ‘Dog’, ‘cat’ or ‘car’\ncan be tested relatively objectively; ‘atmospheric’ requires a different\nreference. The test should include not only attractive new photographs\nbut also old scans, heavy compression and objects that are hard to\nrecognise. Multiple images of the same scene should, where possible, be\nkept together in training or testing.<\/p>\n<p>A useful assessment chain would be independent reference tags for a\nsample; precision and recall per category; error rates for old and new\nfiles; running time for large archives; and documented behaviour when\nresults are uncertain. The system could provide suggestions without\nautomatically deleting or renaming original files.<\/p>\n<p><strong>When is the technology worthwhile?<\/strong> When manually\nmaintained tags are no longer practical for large archives. <strong>When\nis it excessive?<\/strong> For a few well-named folders or where a simple\nkeyword search suffices. <strong>Operational consequences:<\/strong>\nModel updates, index recalculation, storage space and local data\nprotection properties need to be considered.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S24\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Architecture example II: A document-supported domain\nassistant<\/h2>\n<p>A domain assistant answers questions exclusively on the basis of\napproved, versioned documents. Technically, it consists of document\nmanagement, a retrieval component, a language model and an output layer\nthat makes evidence visible.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Currently approved documents ---&gt; Retrieval ---+\n                                               +--&gt; Language model --&gt; Answer\nQuestion --------------------------------------+                    +--&gt; Evidence\n                                                                      |\n                                                                      v\n                                                                  Verifiability<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>A deliberately small test set might contain four types: unambiguously\nsupported answers; ambiguous questions; questions with conflicting\ndocument versions; and questions for which the archive contains no\nanswer. A good system must <strong>not only answer<\/strong>, but\n<strong>correctly qualify or decline<\/strong> when the basis is\nlacking.<\/p>\n<p>Separate measures would include the document retrieval hit rate,\nfactual correctness, correct attribution of claims to sources,\nproportion of unjustifiably invented answers and behaviour when document\ntexts have been manipulated. Access permissions must also be tested: a\ngood answer that discloses confidential document content is\noperationally unacceptable.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Central lesson:<\/strong> A new model version may produce\nbetter wording while a poor search index keeps the overall assistant\nunreliable. The quality of the document-supported system therefore does\nnot follow from a model benchmark.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S25\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Architecture example III: Automatic preliminary ticket\nsorting<\/h2>\n<p>An organisation receives numerous technical enquiries. A\nclassification model proposes the responsible specialist group but does\nnot itself decide about refunds, suspensions or financial actions. The\ntask appears simpler than general dialogue, but has precise operational\ntargets.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Incoming ticket\n       |\n       v\n Preliminary check -&gt; Classification model -&gt; Confidence + specialist group\n                                                  |\n                                  +---------------+--------------+\n                                  |                              |\n                           high confidence                  unclear/new\n                                  |                              |\n                           adopt suggestion                 human review\n                                  +--------------+---------------+\n                                                 |\n                                          record corrections<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Testing can examine a confusion matrix for each specialist group,\nparticularly costly misrouting errors and the proportion requiring\nmanual reworking. A random test set from a single week could be\nunsuitable if certain topics occur only rarely or seasonally. An\nadditional sample from a later period would strengthen the\nconclusions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limiting automation:<\/strong> The specialist should be able\nto correct a suggestion without the system adopting their change\nunchecked as a new training label. Otherwise a feedback loop may\ndevelop: incorrect suggestions influence later data on which the next\nmodel is trained again.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Architecture connection to M7-L4:<\/strong> Roles, correction\npaths, monitoring, costs and human responsibilities are just as\nimportant as the classification rate. Good model performance is a\nnecessary but insufficient condition for good operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S26\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Documentation: What must a results report contain?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>model card<\/strong> describes a model, its purpose,\nintended and unsuitable uses, data context, evaluation and known\nlimitations. The basic idea was proposed as a systematic documentation\napproach [Q9]. Such a report is not a substitute for testing, but makes\nits scope more traceable.<\/p>\n<p>A sound evaluation report specifies at least:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>System and version:<\/strong> Model, prompt, tools, data\nsources, date and relevant configuration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Purpose and risk class:<\/strong> What should be decided or\ngenerated, and what must not happen automatically?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Data and reference:<\/strong> Where do the cases come from,\nand how were they selected and assessed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Experimental procedure:<\/strong> Independent tests,\ncomparison baseline, repetitions, defined primary metrics.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Results and uncertainty:<\/strong> Error types, variation,\nsubgroups and relevant failures.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Operation:<\/strong> Latency, costs, safety measures, update\nand fallback paths.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Limitations:<\/strong> Which statements are specifically\n<strong>not<\/strong> supported by the investigation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A report with a precise error rate and stated limitations is\nscientifically more honest than the claim ‘almost error-free’, even if\nthe latter sounds more professional. Traceable <strong>negative\nresults<\/strong> are particularly valuable: they show which tasks the\nsystem should not be used for.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S27\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>NIST risk management: Evaluation throughout the system\nlifecycle<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>National Institute of Standards and Technology\n(NIST)<\/strong> has published the <strong>AI Risk Management Framework\n(AI RMF)<\/strong>, a voluntary framework for assessing and managing\nrisks arising from AI systems. The functions <strong>GOVERN<\/strong>,\n<strong>MAP<\/strong>, <strong>MEASURE<\/strong> and\n<strong>MANAGE<\/strong> connect organisational responsibilities, context\nunderstanding, measurement and concrete risk treatment [Q1]. The\ngenerative AI profile <strong>NIST AI 600-1<\/strong> extends this\nperspective to the particular characteristics of generative systems\n[Q2].<\/p>\n<p>In simple terms:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>GOVERN: Who decides and bears responsibility?\n   |\nMAP: What can go wrong in which context?\n   |\nMEASURE: How do we measure performance, harms and uncertainty?\n   |\nMANAGE: Which safeguards and limits follow?\n   |\n   +------&gt; reassess when changes occur<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Important: A framework is <strong>not a certification<\/strong> of a\nparticular model. A document headed ‘NIST-compliant’ is no evidence that\na system is safe or suitable for a particular application unless the\nimplementation has been checked and the scope is clear. According to\nNIST, the AI RMF is also being developed further; the reference point\npresented here is the publicly documented version 1.0 and the 2024\nprofile (source check 05.11.2026).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S28\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Where scientific conclusions end<\/h2>\n<p>Even a careful evaluation answers only a subset of all questions. It\nmay show that system A performs better than B on a particular test set\naccording to a particular criterion. It cannot automatically prove that\nA handles <em>all<\/em> conceivable inputs correctly, protects against\n<em>all<\/em> attacks or is the best societal choice.<\/p>\n<p>Four kinds of statement should therefore be distinguished:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Directly measured:<\/strong> ‘In the defined test, 160 of the\n200 positive cases were detected.’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Statistically estimated:<\/strong> ‘Under the stated\nassumptions, the detection rate in a larger comparable population is\nlikely to lie approximately within this range.’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Plausibly transferred:<\/strong> ‘Similar operational data\nmight lead to comparable results’ – this transfer requires further\ntesting.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Not established:<\/strong> ‘The system generally operates\nwithout errors, discrimination or security problems.’<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Research can narrow limits and make doubts more precise, but cannot\nabolish uncertainty. Demanding computer science consists precisely in\n<strong>making the scope of a statement explicit<\/strong>. Under\nappropriate conditions, a responsible system may also output ‘not\ndecidable from the available information’ or refer the case to a\nqualified person.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S29\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and bridge to M7-L6<\/h2>\n<p>M7-L5 connects the scientific methods of <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a> with the limits of proof in\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a> and architectural\ntrade-offs in <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4<\/a>. The\nguiding conclusions are:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A <strong>model<\/strong> is not the entire <strong>AI\nsystem<\/strong>; good model scores do not guarantee good operation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Informative tests start with <strong>purpose, population, reference\nand consequences of errors<\/strong>, not a ranking figure.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and calibration<\/strong>\nanswer different questions and must not be confused.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Sampling errors, data leakage, drift, subgroup differences\nand attacks<\/strong> limit transferability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In generative and multimodal AI, <strong>factual grounding,\nassessment criteria, tool behaviour and system consequences<\/strong>\nmust be explicitly tested.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Scientific rigour is demonstrated by clearly identifying\n<strong>untested claims, uncertainty and lack of\nknowledge<\/strong>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Continue reading in the completed core path:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L6 – Cross-system synthesis and\nresearch methods<\/a> connects parts M7-L1–M7-L5 into a reasoned approach\nto architecture and research decisions. M7-L6 is now available in full\nas a reading lesson.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S30\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Position within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This chapter belongs to the <strong>six-part Master’s core<\/strong>,\nnot the optional additional Master’s topics. Navigation: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">previous Master’s core lesson\nM7-L4<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">next Master’s core\nlesson M7-L6<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">reading paths and\nsubject-related cross-references<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S31\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 05.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Verification date:<\/strong> 05.11.2026. The links point to\nscientific publications or official project and institutional pages. The\nsources support concepts, methods and methodological limitations,\n<strong>not<\/strong> the invented table values in this instructional\ntext.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q1] NIST – AI Risk Management Framework 1.0\n(2023):<\/strong> Voluntary framework for GOVERN/MAP/MEASURE/MANAGE and\nevaluation tasks. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\">https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q2] NIST – Generative AI Profile, NIST AI 600-1\n(2024):<\/strong> Risk profile of generative AI. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence\">https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-generative-artificial-intelligence<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q3] NIST – Adversarial Machine Learning, AI\n100-2e2025:<\/strong> Attack classes and security concepts. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/publications/adversarial-machine-learning-taxonomy-and-terminology-attacks-and-mitigations-0\">https://www.nist.gov/publications/adversarial-machine-learning-taxonomy-and-terminology-attacks-and-mitigations-0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q4] Liang et al., Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\n(2023):<\/strong> Multidimensional systematic model evaluation. <a\nhref=\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=iO4LZibEqW\">https://openreview.net/forum?id=iO4LZibEqW<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q5] Stanford CRFM – HELM project:<\/strong> Documentation,\nscenarios, metrics, maintenance mode since 01.06.2026. <a\nhref=\"https://crfm-helm.readthedocs.io/en/latest/\">https://crfm-helm.readthedocs.io/en/latest/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q6] Gebru et al., Datasheets for Datasets (2021):<\/strong>\nDataset documentation and usage context. <a\nhref=\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/datasheets-for-datasets/\">https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/datasheets-for-datasets/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q7] Google Machine Learning Crash Course –\nTraining/Validation/Test:<\/strong> Separation of datasets and problems\nwith repeated use of tests. <a\nhref=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets\">https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/dividing-datasets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q8] Stanford CRFM – HEIM, Holistic Evaluation of\nText-to-Image Models:<\/strong> Dimensions for image generators. <a\nhref=\"https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/heim/v1.0.0/\">https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/heim/v1.0.0/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q9] Mitchell et al., Model Cards for Model Reporting\n(2019):<\/strong> Transparent model documentation and limitations. <a\nhref=\"https://research.google/pubs/model-cards-for-model-reporting/\">https://research.google/pubs/model-cards-for-model-reporting/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q10] scikit-learn – Model Evaluation Guide:<\/strong>\nDefinitions and limits of classification metrics. <a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html\">https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> This is a scientifically oriented\nreading course, not an external model certification, a specific\ncompliance assessment or an independently conducted empirical research\nproject. Figures and processes are didactic models; actual system\nbehaviour must be checked separately in each case.<\/p>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M7-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M7-L6 – Cross-system synthesis and research methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Master’s-oriented computer science<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master’s orientation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> No fixed prerequisites\nspecified.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Note:<\/strong> Learning course – Computer Science | Master’s\ncore area F, lesson 6 of 6<\/p>\n<p><strong>Guiding question:<\/strong> How does one move from a technical\nidea to a <strong>traceable, verifiable and responsible\ndecision<\/strong> when algorithms, databases, distributed systems,\nsecurity, AI, operation and costs all play a role at the same time?<\/p>\n<p>This lesson concludes the Master’s core area. It connects the\nscientific reasoning from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a>, the limits of formal\ncorrectness from <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a>,\ndistributed system models from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a>, architectural and security\ntrade-offs from <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4<\/a> and AI\nevaluation from <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5<\/a>. M7-L6 is\nnot a repetition of these five texts. Its subject is <strong>the method\nof bringing different kinds of proof and knowledge together into a\nrobust overall assessment<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The actual research problem is rarely purely technical<\/h2>\n<p>A small program can run quickly on one’s own computer and reliably\nperform its task. Once it becomes an online service, the questions\nchange. Several people are now active simultaneously, a server may fail,\ninputs may be malicious, personal data require protection, queries\nbecome expensive and an AI module might produce plausible but incorrect\nanswers.<\/p>\n<p>One can answer each individual question in isolation and still build\nthe wrong overall system. A particularly fast algorithm, for example, is\nof little use if a database transaction creates duplicate entries on\nretries. An excellently rated AI is of little use if its document access\nbypasses permissions. A highly available architecture may incur\nunaffordable costs.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Cross-system synthesis<\/strong> therefore means that results\nfrom several disciplinary perspectives are not simply added together but\nexamined for <strong>interactions, shared assumptions and conflicting\nobjectives<\/strong>. A particular improvement in quality may depend on a\nprerequisite that is not met elsewhere.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>User problem and context\n          |\n          v\n     verifiable goals\n          |\n          v\n    several solution paths\n       /  |    |    \\\n      v   v    v     v\n Algorithm Data Security Operations/AI\n       \\  |    |    /\n          v\n Evidence + limits + interactions\n          |\n          v\n reasoned decision and reassessment<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>When is it useful?<\/strong> Especially when selecting an\narchitecture, planning migrations, undertaking research and automating\ndecisions with real consequences. <strong>When is it excessive?<\/strong>\nFor a private one-off script, a few clear criteria often suffice. The\ncore of the method – writing down the problem, assumptions and\nlimitations – remains helpful there too.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research, development and evaluation are different\nactivities<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Development<\/strong> creates a working artefact: software, a\ndatabase structure or a procedure. <strong>Evaluation<\/strong> examines\nparticular properties of that artefact. <strong>Scientific\nresearch<\/strong>, in addition, seeks methodologically justified\nfindings relevant beyond the particular case.<\/p>\n<p>Working software is therefore not yet a scientific discovery.\nConversely, a scientifically important investigation may end with a\nnegative result: a sophisticated new architecture offered no advantage\nover a simple solution under realistic conditions.<\/p>\n<p>In <strong>design science research<\/strong>, purposeful artefacts are\ndesigned and evaluated to gain knowledge about a problem and possible\nsolutions. Hevner and colleagues bring problem relevance, artefact\ndesign and evaluation together in a common framework [Q1]. This is an\nestablished research direction, but <strong>not every ordinary software\ndevelopment activity automatically becomes research as a\nresult<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>The distinction can be illustrated by three statements:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Development report:<\/strong> ‘We implemented a search\nfunction with a cache.’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Evaluation report:<\/strong> ‘In a specified test, the\nmeasured response time decreased under these conditions.’<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Research contribution:<\/strong> ‘We investigated under which\nload and data conditions caching is worthwhile compared with an index,\ntested competing explanations and formulated the limited scope of the\nresults.’<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The third statement requires traceable questions, appropriate\ncomparative measures and an open account of uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A precise research question avoids many apparent answers\nlater<\/h2>\n<p>‘Is the cloud better?’ is too general. Neither ‘cloud’ nor ‘better’\ndesignates a single measurable property. A testable question might\nbe:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>‘For a media service with 100,000 metadata records and a\npredominantly read-oriented workload, does an additional cache layer\nreduce the p95 response time compared with the same database without a\ncache, without serving outdated updates more frequently than\nagreed?’<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p><strong>p95<\/strong> denotes the <strong>95th percentile<\/strong>:\napproximately 95% of observed requests take no longer than this time.\nThe question specifies the system, workload, alternative, primary\nmeasure and a constraint. The 100,000 records mentioned here are merely\na <strong>didactic assumption<\/strong>, not a performance specification\nfor a product.<\/p>\n<p>This includes four different statements:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Research question:<\/strong> What difference does the cache\nlayer make under these conditions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Hypothesis:<\/strong> It reduces p95 latency in read-heavy\noperation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Measurement definition:<\/strong> What counts as a request,\nhow is measurement performed and which requests are excluded from\nconsideration?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Refutation criterion:<\/strong> What would fail to support\nthe claimed improvement, such as no effect, high variation or violation\nof freshness limits?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A good question is not scientific merely because it contains\nmathematical symbols. What matters is that a possible finding could\nactually change its assessment. This builds on the work on models and\nhypotheses in M7-L1.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From problem to claim: A traceable chain of evidence<\/h2>\n<p>A scientifically usable architectural decision has several\nstages:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Initial problem\n  -&gt; objective and exclusion conditions\n  -&gt; alternatives\n  -&gt; model / hypotheses\n  -&gt; proof or testing procedure\n  -&gt; results and uncertainty\n  -&gt; decision with scope\n  -&gt; observation in actual operation<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>In practice, the sequence need not be strictly linear. A test may\nshow that the research question was formulated incorrectly. A data\nprotection requirement can immediately exclude an alternative. It is\nimportant that later results can visibly alter earlier assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Claim, evidence and conclusion<\/strong> should remain\nseparate:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Statement<\/th>\n<th>Possible evidence<\/th>\n<th>What remains open<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>‘This algorithm returns the correct result for valid input.’<\/td>\n<td>Formal correctness argument under defined assumptions<\/td>\n<td>Hardware errors, incorrect implementation, invalid inputs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘This query runs faster in the test.’<\/td>\n<td>Measurement series, experimental conditions, variation<\/td>\n<td>Other workloads, data distributions, future versions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘This storage system survives the failure of one node.’<\/td>\n<td>Failure model, protocol proof, failure experiment<\/td>\n<td>Common causes of failure, operator errors, loss of an entire\nsite<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘The AI identifies suitable document passages.’<\/td>\n<td>Independent test corpus and reference judgements<\/td>\n<td>New document types, untested user groups, attacks<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>‘This solution is sensible overall.’<\/td>\n<td>Combination of criteria, risks, costs and decision-makers<\/td>\n<td>Changed requirements, normative priorities<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The word <strong>‘supported’<\/strong> is always tied to the specific\nclaim and the conditions of the evidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Four forms of knowledge must not replace one another<\/h2>\n<p>At least four kinds of knowledge are needed in computer science:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Deductive proof:<\/strong> A statement follows from model\nassumptions and logical rules. M7-L2 explains, for example, why a\ncorrectly formulated loop invariant can contribute to a justification. A\nproof holds <strong>within its model<\/strong>; it does not automatically\nprove that the implemented application fully matches that model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Empirical measurement:<\/strong> Actual processes are\nobserved. Measurements can show running time, error rates or energy\nconsumption. Uncertainty arises, among other things, from samples,\nenvironmental changes and measuring instruments.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Simulation or model calculation:<\/strong> Parameters are\nexplored in a simplified model. This supports predictions, but is not\nevidence that a real system behaves exactly in the same way.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Normative assessment:<\/strong> People determine which\nconsequences are acceptable: how important is privacy compared with\nconvenience? What cost framework is affordable? What residual risk may\nan organisation take? An algorithm can support such value decisions but\ncannot legitimise them alone.<\/p>\n<p>A common fallacy is: ‘The measured value is more precise, so the\ndecision is automatically correct.’ In reality, a <strong>justified\nobjective<\/strong> is additionally required. Measuring the exact latency\nof impermissible data processing does not make that processing\nacceptable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and dependencies as an explicit system\nmodel<\/h2>\n<p>A system can be described via its components as a directed\n<strong>dependency graph<\/strong>. A web application, for instance,\ndepends on a database, an identity service and file storage. A\ndocument-supported AI assistant adds a search index, a language model\nand access-permission control.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Browser\n   |\n   v\nWeb application ----&gt; Identity verification\n   |                          |\n   v                          v\nApplication logic ----&gt; Access rules\n   |    \\                     |\n   v     v                    |\nDatabase  Document retrieval  |\n             |                |\n             v                |\n          Language model &lt;----+\n             |\n             v\n        Answer with source<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The sketch is a <strong>logical view<\/strong>; it implies neither\nthat every component is a separate server nor that the language model\nitself reliably enforces permissions. Access rules must be implemented\nin trusted application components and enforced before protected content\nis retrieved.<\/p>\n<p>Three questions aid the analysis:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which <strong>functional chain<\/strong> does a user action actually\nrequire?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Where are the <strong>common causes of failure<\/strong>? A shared\npower supply may affect several supposedly independent servers\nsimultaneously.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What <strong>chain of trust<\/strong> arises? Not every result from\none component may be accepted unchecked as an instruction to\nanother.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The value of an architecture lies not in the number of components but\nin the manageability of its actual dependencies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research design: Comparability means controlled, not\nartificially perfect<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>experimental design<\/strong> (design of experiments, DoE)\nspecifies how alternatives are investigated so that observed differences\ncan be meaningfully interpreted. The NIST/SEMATECH handbook covers,\namong other topics, measurement processes, experimental design and the\nlimits of statistical conclusions [Q2].<\/p>\n<p>For a database comparison, data volume, hardware, indexing, network\npath and cache configuration should not change simultaneously. Otherwise\nthe cause of a difference in running time cannot be identified.\n<strong>Confounders<\/strong> are influences that vary together with the\nchange under investigation and may bias the result.<\/p>\n<p>Important methods:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Controlled comparison:<\/strong> As far as possible, vary\nonly the property under investigation between alternatives.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Randomisation:<\/strong> Assign orders or test cases by a\nrandom procedure so that time of day and order effects do not\nsystematically favour one variant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Repetition:<\/strong> Carry out measurements multiple times\nto reveal variability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Blocking:<\/strong> Compare within similar groups, for\nexample treating small and large files separately.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Predefined endpoints:<\/strong> Determine the main criterion\nand exclusion rules, wherever possible, before looking at the\nresults.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A single attractive measurement is <strong>not<\/strong> reliable\nevidence. Conversely, absolute laboratory purity is not always possible.\nA realistic test under documented uncertainty is often more valuable\nthan a perfect but completely unrealistic microbenchmark.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>End-to-end measures rather than the most attractive\nindividual figure<\/h2>\n<p>The term <strong>end-to-end<\/strong> refers to the entire chain of\neffects from initiating a user action to obtaining a usable result. A\nfast search index is of little use if the subsequent permissions check,\nnetwork transmission or AI response takes a long time.<\/p>\n<p>Consider an explicitly <strong>constructed<\/strong> response-time\nexample:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Variant A<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Variant B<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Authentication<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">18 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">18 ms<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Database query<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">75 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">26 ms<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application logic<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">29 ms<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Network and display<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">43 ms<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">43 ms<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total for one hypothetical execution<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>160 ms<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>116 ms<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The sums are arithmetically correct:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>43<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>160<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=18+75+24+43=160\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>26<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>29<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>43<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>116<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=18+26+29+43=116\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>This addition describes exactly one modelled execution<\/strong>,\nnot the p95 latency of a real system. In particular, individual\ncomponent percentiles must not simply be added to obtain an end-to-end\npercentile; this requires the distribution of complete user\nrequests.<\/p>\n<p>In addition, <strong>error frequency<\/strong>, <strong>response\ncorrectness<\/strong>, <strong>data freshness<\/strong>, <strong>data\nprotection violations<\/strong>, <strong>costs<\/strong> and\n<strong>maintenance effort<\/strong> may be relevant. Good systems\nresearch optimises perceived service quality, not merely the most\nimpressive component metric.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Average, edge case and failure: Different system\nproperties<\/h2>\n<p>A service that is fast on average may be unusable for the slowest 1%\nof requests. Low mean latency does not answer whether p99 latency is\ncritically high. Similarly, high average availability says little about\nthe clustering of failures during an important time window.<\/p>\n<p>Research on <strong>tail latency<\/strong>, the slow ends of\nrunning-time distributions, demonstrates the importance of such edge\ncases for distributed services; the underlying ideas are technically\nintroduced in M7-L3 [Q3].<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>service level indicator (SLI)<\/strong> is a measured\nindicator of service quality, such as the proportion of successful\nrequests. A <strong>service level objective (SLO)<\/strong> is a target\nfor that indicator. SRE practice (<em>site reliability engineering<\/em>)\nuses such targets to balance reliability and development effort in a\nreasoned way [Q4].<\/p>\n<p>An example that is not an actual operator’s promise: an SLO might\nrequire at least 99.5% of eligible user requests to succeed within a\ndefined observation window. To test the statement, a precise definition\nof <strong>eligible<\/strong>, <strong>successful<\/strong>, the time\nwindow and measurement location is required. With few requests,\nproportions are particularly uncertain statistically.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Failure tests<\/strong> supplement performance measures: how\ndoes the application behave during a database restart, an aborted\npayment, a failed deployment or an unavailable AI service? A system\nshould degrade in a traceable way as far as possible, rather than\nsilently returning incorrect results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S10\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interactions that isolated tests overlook<\/h2>\n<p>Many technical decisions entail <strong>interactions<\/strong>. A\ncache improves read rates but may serve stale information. More replicas\nmay improve availability but incur additional synchronisation and\noperational costs. An AI assistant saves time while increasing the need\nfor source verification and permission boundaries.<\/p>\n<p>Example of stepwise media analysis:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Upload file\n  -&gt; extract metadata\n  -&gt; store results\n  -&gt; generate AI description\n  -&gt; editorial review\n  -&gt; publish<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If metadata extraction writes twice during a retry, the system may\ncreate duplicate entries. An AI might then refer to the wrong version.\nWhat was originally a local error becomes a problem of data integrity,\ndomain quality and ultimately user trust.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Idempotency<\/strong> here means that repeating the same\ndomain-level job produces no unwanted additional effects. It does not\nguarantee that a message is physically transmitted exactly once over the\nnetwork. This fundamental principle connects M1-L4, M7-L3 and M7-L4.<\/p>\n<p>During synthesis, <strong>interfaces and state transitions<\/strong>\nmust therefore be tested, not merely individual modules. The transitions\nbetween responsible components are often precisely where apparently good\npartial results fail.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S11\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing objectives, minimum conditions and\npreferences<\/h2>\n<p>Not every property may be traded for an arbitrary amount of speed or\nmoney. <strong>Minimum conditions<\/strong> are exclusion criteria.\n<strong>Preferences<\/strong> only subsequently help rank permissible\nalternatives.<\/p>\n<p>An example:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion<\/th>\n<th>Type<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Protected documents available only to authorised users<\/td>\n<td>Minimum condition<\/td>\n<td>A variant without reliable permission checking is excluded<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recovery tested in practice<\/td>\n<td>Minimum condition, if business-critical<\/td>\n<td>Require verifiable recovery before release<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>p95 response time<\/td>\n<td>Preference with threshold<\/td>\n<td>Lower is useful if it benefits the intended use<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monthly operating expenditure<\/td>\n<td>Preference with budget limit<\/td>\n<td>Costs must remain affordable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Modifiability and comprehensibility<\/td>\n<td>Preference<\/td>\n<td>Less coupling may simplify maintenance<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The threshold ‘business-critical’ must be concretely defined. A\nprivate collection of notes may be protected differently from a system\nthat processes payments or sensitive health data.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>Pareto-dominated<\/strong> alternative is one that is no\nbetter in every dimension considered and worse in at least one than\nanother alternative. Under the stated assumptions, such an alternative\nis hardly attractive. With <strong>Pareto-non-dominated<\/strong>\nalternatives a genuine conflict of objectives remains; additional\npriorities are needed here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S12\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision matrices: Useful, but not truth machines<\/h2>\n<p>A weighted decision matrix makes priorities visible. However,\narbitrary weights and subjective ratings may just as easily conceal a\npreconceived choice. The calculation below serves <strong>only<\/strong>\nas a learning example.<\/p>\n<p>Assume two permissible architectures have the following ratings on a\n<strong>constructed<\/strong> scale from 1 (weak) to 5 (strong):<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Weight<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">A: Simple single instance<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">B: Multi-service architecture<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Comprehensibility/maintenance<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">40%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating costs<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fault tolerance<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent scaling<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10%<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Weighted overall rating<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>100%<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>4.1<\/strong><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\"><strong>2.7<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The calculation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0{,}4\\times5+0{,}3\\times5+0{,}2\\times2+0{,}1\\times2=<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>4.1<\/strong>. For B,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}4\\times2+0{,}3\\times2+0{,}2\\times4+0{,}1\\times5=<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>2.7<\/strong>. The matrix shows that <strong>under these chosen\nweights<\/strong>, A wins. It does <strong>not<\/strong> show that A is\nobjectively the better architecture or automatically more secure.<\/p>\n<p>A score is not a directly measured natural constant. Hard protection\nand legal requirements must be satisfied <strong>beforehand<\/strong> for\nselected alternatives. Figures for cost or latency should wherever\npossible be measured rather than freely rated; subjective criteria must\nbe indicated as such.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S13\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sensitivity analysis: Would the decision reverse under\ndifferent priorities?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>sensitivity analysis<\/strong> changes assumptions and\nobserves whether the conclusion remains stable. In the preceding\nexample, A depends heavily on the high weight assigned to simple\nmaintenance and costs. For a second, likewise <strong>freely\ninvented<\/strong>, emphasis of requirements, change the weights to\n<strong>10% maintenance, 10% operating costs, 45% fault tolerance and\n35% independent scaling<\/strong>. The ratings of both architectures\nremain unchanged. This gives:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>A:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>45<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>35<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}10 \\times  5 + 0{,}10 \\times  5 + 0{,}45 \\times  2 + 0{,}35 \\times  2 = 2{,}60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>B:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>45<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>35<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>95<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0{,}10 \\times  2 + 0{,}10 \\times  2 + 0{,}45 \\times  4 + 0{,}35 \\times  5 = 3{,}95<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Under the <strong>new preferences<\/strong>, the ranking reverses: B\nis ahead of A. This is genuine computational sensitivity, but still\n<strong>not empirical evidence<\/strong> for B’s availability. If the\nrequirements are additionally tightened so that only a demonstrably\nfault-tolerant multi-node architecture meets the minimum conditions, A\nis <strong>no longer permissible<\/strong> irrespective of its previous\nscore. B must also first demonstrate the required properties.<\/p>\n<p>Other useful questions are:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does the result change if demand is ten times as high?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What if network traffic rather than computing time determines the\ncosts?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which alternative remains usable if an external service fails?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How strongly does the recommendation depend on unsubstantiated\nestimates?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does an apparent improvement turn out to be only marginal under\nactual load?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A decision is <strong>robust<\/strong> not because all assumptions are\npositive, but because it remains viable under plausible changes in its\nassumptions or has a known fallback path.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S14\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research and architectural decisions have different time\nhorizons<\/h2>\n<p>A benchmark compares systems in a particular state. An architecture,\nhowever, is maintained over months or years. New software versions,\ndifferent data volumes and changes of personnel can alter its\nproperties.<\/p>\n<p>A distinction is therefore made between:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>One-off costs:<\/strong> Design, migration, checks and\ninitial setup.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Recurring costs:<\/strong> Computing, storage, data traffic,\nlicences, backups, monitoring and maintenance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Change costs:<\/strong> How much effort will the next\nmodification or a switch to another provider require?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Risk and outage costs:<\/strong> What damage can arise from\ndata loss, security vulnerabilities or prolonged unavailability?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Opportunity costs:<\/strong> What other useful work is left\nundone because a complex architecture consumes considerable\nattention?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Total cost of ownership (TCO)<\/strong> considers total costs\nover a chosen period. A single monthly invoice from the cloud portal is\nnot complete evidence for it. A central research aspect is added here to\nthe M7-L4 discussion of architecture: <strong>time and future change are\npart of the model assumptions<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>When future demand cannot be estimated, an initially simple,\nwell-modularised architecture with a traceable growth path may be more\nsensible than immediate distribution across many services. Not every\nfuture possibility must already be paid for technically today.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S15\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Security and data protection belong in the research\ndesign<\/h2>\n<p>Technically capable systems can be unacceptable because of\nimpermissible data processing or abusive access. Security and data\nprotection questions should therefore be considered as early as the\nselection of data, the test setup and the presentation of results.<\/p>\n<p>The NIST document <strong>SP 800-160 Volume 1 Revision 1<\/strong>\ntreats security as part of systems engineering throughout the lifecycle\n[Q5]. Its contribution is methodological: protection is not regarded\nafter the fact as an additional product property.<\/p>\n<p>When comparing two search methods, this may mean:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Use synthetic or lawfully supplied data for measurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Do not unnecessarily write sensitive content into raw logs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Model test users and permissions so that no concealed grants\narise.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">After completion, control data retention, deletion and access.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Do not attribute protection against personal inferences to the\npurported anonymisation of published datasets without verifying it.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Security assessment<\/strong> is more than a penetration test\nand <strong>data protection assessment<\/strong> more than encryption.\nBoth concern purposes, roles, lifecycles, misbehaviour and the limits of\npermissible operation. This lesson is not binding legal advice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S16\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distinguishing reproducibility, replicability and\ngeneralisability<\/h2>\n<p>Three terms sound similar but are not used consistently. In the sense\nof the <strong>National Academies<\/strong> report [Q6]:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Computational reproducibility:<\/strong> Consistent results\ncan be obtained using the same data, methods, computational steps and\nrelevant analysis conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Replicability:<\/strong> New investigations of the same\nscientific question obtain consistent results with their own data,\ntaking uncertainty into account.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Generalisability (generalizability):<\/strong> Results also\nhold in environments or populations other than those of the original\ninvestigation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Important:<\/strong> Some computer science conferences or\nother disciplines use ‘reproduced’ and ‘replicated’ differently. For\npublications, the <strong>specific definition<\/strong> must therefore be\nread; identical words do not guarantee identical evaluation methods.<\/p>\n<p>Instructions that rerun a benchmark improve reproducibility. An\nindependent working group on different servers can contribute to\nreplication. Whether a result also applies to a fundamentally different\nload or different users is an additional question. The three levels do\nnot automatically entail one another.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S17\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research artefacts: What would another person need to\nreceive?<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>research artefact<\/strong> is a verifiable component of an\ninvestigation, such as source code, a data schema, test data, a\nmeasurement script, a configuration file, a statistical analysis\nprocedure or a formal specification. For a repeatable investigation, the\nfollowing information should be traceable:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Artefact component<\/th>\n<th>Why important?<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Specific software and library versions<\/td>\n<td>Behaviour may change between versions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data provenance, filters, cleaning rules<\/td>\n<td>Selection decisions affect the result<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Database model, indices, data size<\/td>\n<td>Performance depends strongly on these details<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Environment and load generator<\/td>\n<td>Hardware and request distribution affect measurements<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measurement procedure and raw results<\/td>\n<td>Aggregated values alone conceal outliers and errors<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis, exclusions and statistics<\/td>\n<td>Figures must remain interpretable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limitations, licences and access rights<\/td>\n<td>Not everything may be made publicly available<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The <strong>ACM<\/strong> uses its own research artefact review\nprocedures; these distinguish, for example, whether materials are\navailable, usable or reusable, or whether results have been\nindependently confirmed [Q7]. Such labels are <strong>not a general\nsecurity guarantee<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>For data, the <strong>FAIR principle<\/strong> is also useful:\n<em>Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, Reusable<\/em> [Q8].\n<strong>Accessible does not automatically mean public<\/strong>:\ncontrolled, legally permissible access can also be an important\nelement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S18\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Describe uncertainty systematically rather than concealing\nit<\/h2>\n<p>Two results with the same score may have different degrees of\nreliability. A value from a hundred independent test cases must be\nassessed differently from a value from ten almost identical test cases.\nA measurement from a stable environment may offer better comparability\nthan one from a highly fluctuating production system.<\/p>\n<p>Relevant types of uncertainty include:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Random variation:<\/strong> Repeated measurements\nfluctuate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Systematic bias:<\/strong> The testing procedure favours\nparticular cases or alternatives.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Model uncertainty:<\/strong> Important parts of reality are\nnot fully represented.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Parameter uncertainty:<\/strong> Load, failure frequency or\nusage patterns are only estimated.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Normative uncertainty:<\/strong> Participants weight risks\nand interests differently.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A <strong>confidence interval<\/strong> is a range calculated under\ncertain model assumptions using a procedure intended to cover the true\nparameter with a specified long-run frequency over repeated samples. It\nis <strong>not<\/strong> the probability that the fixed true value lies\nin this exact interval after observing these data. M7-L5 provides\nspecific worked examples.<\/p>\n<p>For an architectural decision, a simple <strong>uncertainty\nlog<\/strong> may suffice: ‘Latency measured; long-term costs estimated;\nfailure behaviour modelled only; data protection controls not yet\nindependently reviewed.’ This wording is more informative than a single\noverall traffic-light indicator.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S19\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning from negative results and stopping criteria<\/h2>\n<p>A research approach has not failed merely because the original\nhypothesis is not confirmed. If a distributed database design requires\nmore effort and is slower than a single instance under realistic\nrequirements, that is a <strong>valuable finding<\/strong>, provided the\ncomparison was fair.<\/p>\n<p>Useful stopping criteria may include:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The expected benefit is below a previously specified threshold of\npractical relevance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A hard security or data protection condition is not met.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Costs exceed the affordable limit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The measured improvement disappears after accounting for\nvariation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The proposed solution relies on assumptions that do not hold at the\ndeployment site.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>A negative result should not be concealed or reinterpreted through an\never-changing set of metrics. <strong>Exploratory analyses<\/strong> may\nsuggest new hypotheses, but their results must be identified as\nretrospectively discovered. Robust confirmation requires new, suitable\ndata or a dedicated independent investigation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S20\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case study A: Media archive – search, database and user\nexperience<\/h2>\n<p>A local media catalogue is intended to grow. Initially a file\nsuffices. Later, 500,000 entries must be searched quickly and updated\nconcurrently by a few users. Three options are conceptually\navailable:<\/p>\n<p><strong>A:<\/strong> structured files with custom search logic;\n<strong>B:<\/strong> a local relational database with suitable indices;\n<strong>C:<\/strong> a database server with a web application. These\nalternatives do not meet exactly the same operational requirements.\nCloud infrastructure becomes relevant only if external online access is\nneeded.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Research question:<\/strong> Which data storage approach\nprovides adequate response times, traceable changes and acceptable\nmaintenance effort for a defined search profile?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Investigation method:<\/strong> A common dataset, documented\nsearch queries, identical hardware for local variants, separate\nassessment of server operation and repeated measurements after plausible\nwarm-up and modification phases. Alongside p50/p95, data integrity,\ncorrectness of search results and modifiability are assessed.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Potential finding:<\/strong> A local database might already\nmeet the need. A public server would then not be a better ‘database\nsolution’, but an appropriate operational option only upon the emergence\nof a new domain requirement. The result would not be that servers are\nbad, but that <strong>requirements and architectural measures must\ncorrespond<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Connections:<\/strong> M1-L3 and M10-L2 explain data\nstructures and queries; M3-L1–M3-L6 data storage; M7-L1 the experimental\nquestion; M7-L4 the operational consequences. This is a\n<strong>hypothetical teaching scenario with no benchmark\nconducted<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S21\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case study B: Cloud service – reliability versus\ncomplexity<\/h2>\n<p>A service accepts analysis jobs. A user uploads files; processing may\ntake several minutes. A simple implementation handles every job directly\nwithin the web process. A more elaborate alternative decouples the web\nserver, queue and workers.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Direct variant:\nBrowser -&gt; Web server -&gt; Computation -&gt; Response\n\nDecoupled variant:\nBrowser -&gt; Web server -&gt; Job database\n                          |\n                          v\n                         Queue\n                          |\n                          v\n                        Worker -&gt; Results database\n                          |\n                          v\n                       Status query<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><strong>Hypothesis:<\/strong> The decoupled variant keeps short web\nrequests responsive even when individual computations take a long time.\n<strong>Counter-hypothesis:<\/strong> The additional operational effort\nprovides insufficient benefit for the current volume but creates new\ntypes of error.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Method:<\/strong> Vary arrival rates, computation duration and\nconcurrent jobs; deliberately account for errors and retries; verify\ndomain-level job success by unique identifier. Measurements include\nwaiting time until the result, responsiveness when creating a job,\nduplicate side effects and recovery after worker failure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:<\/strong> A queue does not make processing\nreliable by itself. It shifts the problem to persistence, retries, job\nstatus, dead-letter processing and responsibilities. Under very light\nload, the direct call may remain more suitable. This is the same lesson\nas in M7-L3: <strong>distribution is a means, not a quality objective in\nitself<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S22\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case study C: Document-supported AI assistant – quality and\nprotection together<\/h2>\n<p>An organisation wants to make internal documents queryable through an\nassistant. Possible approaches include conventional full-text search and\nsearch with <strong>retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)<\/strong>, in\nwhich retrieved document passages are supplied to a language model as\ncontext.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Research questions:<\/strong> Do users find the correct\npassage faster? Are the answers supported by evidence? Do documents\nbeyond their permissions remain invisible? What types of error and\noperational costs arise?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Evaluation design:<\/strong> A representative set of real,\nlawfully selected questions is prepared with reference answers and\nassociated document passages. Permission configurations are part of the\ntest plan. For each alternative, retrieval accuracy, unsupported claims,\ncitation quality, response time and the need for human corrections are\nevaluated separately.<\/p>\n<p>A modelled process:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Question + verified identity\n           |\n           v\n Permission-checked document retrieval\n           |\n           +------&gt; suitable source available?\n                        |             |\n                       yes            no\n                        |             |\n                        v             v\n                   Model answer    “Not supported”\n                        |\n                        v\n                 Citation + verification<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The sketch is not a guarantee that a language model will\nindependently refrain from answering when no source is available. The\n<strong>application system<\/strong> must control the handling of missing\nor prohibited sources. A prompt alone is not a reliable authorisation\nboundary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Possible conclusion:<\/strong> A generative system may be\nuseful for particular question types without fully replacing\nconventional search. Likewise, in a narrow environment the safer or less\ncostly choice may be precisely the one <strong>without<\/strong> a\nlanguage model. The evaluation procedure draws methodologically on\nM7-L5, the permissions and risk analysis on M7-L4. This scenario too has\n<strong>not been tested in reality<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S23\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Synthesis example: A judgement with several kinds of\nevidence<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose the preceding studies had actually produced results. A\nrigorous final account would need a structure such as the following; the\nrows are <strong>templates for types of evidence<\/strong>, not claimed\nresearch findings:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Evaluation dimension<\/th>\n<th>Required statement<\/th>\n<th>Type of evidence<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Functional correctness<\/td>\n<td>Jobs do not take effect twice<\/td>\n<td>Invariants, integration and retry tests<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User-facing performance<\/td>\n<td>p95 end-to-end latency is within the target range<\/td>\n<td>Measurement series under a specified load profile<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reliability<\/td>\n<td>Recovery after a defined failure succeeds<\/td>\n<td>Failure test with observed result<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data protection<\/td>\n<td>Unauthorised users do not see protected documents<\/td>\n<td>Permissions model, negative access tests, review<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI quality<\/td>\n<td>Answers have verifiable sources or acknowledge lack of\nknowledge<\/td>\n<td>Independent test corpus, error taxonomy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economic viability<\/td>\n<td>Costs remain within the agreed framework<\/td>\n<td>Model calculation plus actual cost observation<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintainability<\/td>\n<td>A change remains manageable through limited interfaces<\/td>\n<td>Architecture review and documented change path<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>An architecture should be approved only for the <strong>demonstrated\nconditions<\/strong>. If permissions checking was not investigated, this\nmust remain explicitly open. It must not be treated as ‘also\ndemonstrated’ by good AI retrieval rates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S24\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Responsibility is not another numerical measure<\/h2>\n<p>Scientific quality and practical responsibility overlap but are not\nidentical. A study may demonstrate with flawless methodology that\ncertain users are served faster. Whether disadvantaging other users is\nacceptable requires additional normative and potentially legal\nassessment.<\/p>\n<p>Responsibility for decisions should remain identifiable:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Domain responsibility:<\/strong> What must the system achieve\nfor its intended purpose?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Technical responsibility:<\/strong> What guarantees and\nlimits does the implementation have?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Operational responsibility:<\/strong> Who notices outages,\nrestores data and decides on changes?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Data protection and security responsibility:<\/strong> Who\nreviews purposes, access and potential consequences for affected\npeople?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Scientific responsibility:<\/strong> Who separates claims,\ndata, interpretation and conflicts of interest?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>With <strong>GOVERN, MAP, MEASURE and MANAGE<\/strong>, the NIST\nframework for AI risks also emphasises responsibilities and continuing\nrisk management [Q9]. A documented procedure is helpful but does not\nreplace actual control and is not certification.<\/p>\n<p>Especially for decisions that matter to people, a system must also\noffer a route for <strong>corrections, objections and clarification of\nresponsibility<\/strong>. A high accuracy rate alone cannot compensate\nfor its absence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S25\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The scientific report as a record of the decision<\/h2>\n<p>A good final report is not as long as possible, but\n<strong>traceable<\/strong>. It distinguishes at least:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Question and context:<\/strong> What problem had to be\nsolved? For whom and under which operational assumptions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Alternatives:<\/strong> Which simple and complex solutions\nwere seriously examined?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Hard conditions:<\/strong> What must not be violated\nirrespective of the score?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Methods:<\/strong> Which proofs, measurements, simulations\nand domain reviews were used?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Results:<\/strong> What was actually observed, with what\nvariation and exceptions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Interpretation:<\/strong> Which explanation is plausible, and\nwhich competing explanation remains possible?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Decision:<\/strong> Which alternative is preferred, and for\nwhich traceable reasons?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">8.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>Limitations and follow-up:<\/strong> What is still untested,\nand when must the decision be reassessed?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>An architectural decision can be recorded as an <strong>architecture\ndecision record (ADR)<\/strong> – a short documented architectural\ndecision. What matters is not the exact template but the separation of\ninitial context, options, decision and consequences. If a new decision\nis made later, the previous state need not be made invisible: a\ntraceable history protects against repeated mistakes.<\/p>\n<p>The statement ‘The available data are not yet sufficient for a\ndecision’ is also scientifically sound. An outstanding check is not a\nflaw as long as it is clearly identified and no unjustified certainty is\nsuggested.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S26\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Putting open research questions in context<\/h2>\n<p>The end of a Master’s core course contains not only finished answers.\nAdvanced computer science thrives on open questions such as:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can formal guarantees for a small critical component be\nmeaningfully combined with empirical tests of a large overall\nsystem?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can the effects of AI assistants be assessed over long periods\nwhen users change their behaviour because of the system?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can a service make trustworthy decisions when data provenance,\nsoftware components and conditions of use continually change?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When does the improvement in availability from a distributed\narchitecture justify the additional failure and operational\nsurface?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which scientific statements remain possible if complete raw data\ncannot be published for data protection reasons?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>These are <strong>research questions<\/strong>, not claims about\nproblems already solved. Addressing each requires a suitable model and a\nverifiable method. Even for undecidable problems, restrictions, special\ncases and approximation procedures may be scientifically fruitful\nwithout overriding the impossibility result from M7-L2.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S27\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>How the six Master’s core lessons are connected<\/h2>\n<p>The six lessons can be read as a cumulative progression of\nreasoning:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lesson<\/th>\n<th>Fundamental question<\/th>\n<th>Contribution to synthesis<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1<\/a><\/strong><\/td>\n<td>What is a verifiable claim?<\/td>\n<td>Models, hypotheses, measurements, causality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2<\/a><\/strong><\/td>\n<td>What can be proved or computed in principle?<\/td>\n<td>Correctness, running-time bounds, undecidability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3<\/a><\/strong><\/td>\n<td>What changes with multiple unreliable computers?<\/td>\n<td>Event ordering, failure models, consistency<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4<\/a><\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Which solution is viable under risks and constraints?<\/td>\n<td>Data, protection, costs, architectural trade-offs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5<\/a><\/strong><\/td>\n<td>How are learning and generative systems evaluated?<\/td>\n<td>Data, metrics, robustness, limits of AI<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L6<\/a><\/strong><\/td>\n<td>How are all findings brought together into a judgement?<\/td>\n<td>Research design, chain of evidence, synthesis, responsibility<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>M7-L1–M7-L6 thus form a <strong>thematically complete Master’s core\npath within this self-organised reading course<\/strong>. This\ndesignation is <strong>not a state or university Master’s degree,\naccreditation or evidence of a passed examination<\/strong>. Actual\nuniversity requirements differ and usually go well beyond reading six\nlessons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S28\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Outlook: Deepen specialist areas rather than repeat the\nbasic principle<\/h2>\n<p>The next voluntary step is the <strong>additional optional Master’s\ntopics<\/strong> from the study-level plan. At the completion of M7-L6,\nthey have <strong>not yet been developed as complete lessons<\/strong>.\nIndividual disciplines – such as advanced algorithms, distributed\nsystems, formal methods or robotics – can be treated at considerably\ngreater depth there.<\/p>\n<p>The optional topics need not explain the basic method again from the\nbeginning each time. They can build on M7-L1–M7-L6 while exploring more\nprecise specialised models, more demanding proof ideas and new research\nliterature. A comprehensive later final edit of the entire course\ncollection also remains possible without changing the domain status of\nthe M7-L6 lesson written today.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S29\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Final thought: Good computer science is reasoned\nlimitation<\/h2>\n<p>The most important insight of the entire Master’s core area is not\nthat more complex systems are fundamentally better. It is:<\/p>\n<p><strong>A good technical decision is the simplest sufficiently viable\nsolution whose assumptions, limitations, costs and potential harms have\nbeen understood and made verifiable.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Sometimes that is a local program with a few files. Sometimes it is a\nrelational database, a single server or a modular monolith. Other\nrequirements call for replication, distributed protocols, elaborate\nsecurity architecture or an AI system with structured human\noversight.<\/p>\n<p>Scientific work helps make the limits of a claim visible. Formal\nmethods help rule out certain classes of error. Measurements show how a\nparticular system behaves under particular conditions. Responsible\narchitectural work adds the interests of affected people and the\nconsequences throughout the lifecycle. <strong>Only their reasoned\ncombination produces a robust judgement.<\/strong><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S30\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Position within the course-wide reading paths<\/h2>\n<p>This chapter belongs to the <strong>six-part Master’s core<\/strong>,\nnot the optional additional Master’s topics. Navigation: <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">previous Master’s core lesson\nM7-L5<\/a>; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">reading paths and\nsubject-related cross-references<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;page=index&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">course index<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S31\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Last source check:<\/strong> 05.11.2026<\/p>\n<p><strong>Source verification:<\/strong> 05.11.2026. The following\npublications support the methodological framework. They are <strong>not\nsources for the constructed figures, ratings or case studies<\/strong> in\nthis text. The reading course does not document an independently\nconducted empirical study.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q1] Hevner et al. (2004), ‘Design Science in Information\nSystems Research’, MIS Quarterly 28(1):<\/strong> Design and evaluation\nof purposeful research artefacts. <a\nhref=\"https://aisel.aisnet.org/misq/vol28/iss1/6/\">https://aisel.aisnet.org/misq/vol28/iss1/6/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q2] NIST/SEMATECH Engineering Statistics Handbook:<\/strong>\nExperimental design, measurement procedures, analysis and uncertainty in\nengineering. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/programs-projects/nistsematech-engineering-statistics-handbook\">https://www.nist.gov/programs-projects/nistsematech-engineering-statistics-handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q3] Dean / Barroso (2013), ‘The Tail at Scale’:<\/strong>\nImportance of slow edge cases in large distributed systems. <a\nhref=\"https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/\">https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q4] Google, ‘Implementing SLOs’, Site Reliability\nEngineering Workbook:<\/strong> User-oriented reliability objectives and\ndecisions based on error budgets. <a\nhref=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\">https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q5] NIST SP 800-160 Vol. 1 Rev. 1 (2022), ‘Engineering\nTrustworthy Secure Systems’:<\/strong> Systems security engineering\nthroughout the lifecycle. <a\nhref=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/160/v1/r1/final\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/160/v1/r1/final<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q6] National Academies (2019), ‘Reproducibility and\nReplicability in Science’:<\/strong> Explicit conceptual distinctions and\nlimits of transferability. <a\nhref=\"https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25303/chapter/3\">https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/25303/chapter/3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q7] ACM, ‘Artifact Review and Badging’:<\/strong> Research\nartefacts, traceability and independent assessment; check definitions\nfor each version and event. <a\nhref=\"https://www.acm.org/publications/policies/artifact-review-and-badging-current\">https://www.acm.org/publications/policies/artifact-review-and-badging-current<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q8] Wilkinson et al. (2016), ‘The FAIR Guiding Principles\nfor scientific data management and stewardship’, Scientific\nData:<\/strong> Findability, accessibility, interoperability and\nreusability of research data. <a\nhref=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2016.18\">https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2016.18<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>[Q9] NIST, ‘AI Risk Management Framework 1.0’\n(2023):<\/strong> GOVERN, MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE functions; version 1.0 is\ncurrently being developed further. <a\nhref=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/\">https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Scope limitations:<\/strong> No general cloud pricing\noverview, current product benchmark, data protection or legal advice,\nexternal certification or award of an academic degree. Models,\ncalculations and tables serve understanding. Decisions about real\nsystems require appropriate independent observations, protection checks\nand justified priorities.<\/p>\n<\/article>"};
